## Done when
- No Python under `app/`; the family''s save lives in the desktop build (step 2''s decision answered).
- Workers'' browser checks run on the Node dev server; AGENTS.md says how.
- Per-commit checks pass; `trivia-release-check` runs without the removed `nix build .#app` step.
- Owner plan files updated: `plans/18-steam-integration.md`, `plans/01-architecture.md`, `plans/19-repo-layout.md`; D-59 superseded by decision 111 in `plans/decisions.md`.', '', '2026-10-13T15:16:20Z', 758);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (100, 9, 'UI wording: style guides, TV-check fixes, screen gallery', 380, 'Part 13 of the masterplan (plan 9), from the gamemaster''s TV check (2026-10-13): the UI catalogs read like translations in places (German plural address, inconsistent terms, an Esc menu that says the wrong thing). This plan gives every UI locale a style guide, applies the TV-check fixes, and builds a screen gallery the gamemaster reviews per language, with flags that feed fix passes. Owner: `plans/15a-ui-translation.md` (new section "Wording and review"). Runs before plan 69''s playtest close-out (task 459: LUI-5: playtest fixes and close-out, 340), so the playtest sees the fixed wording.

## Builds on
- Plan 69 (catalogs `es`, `es-ES`, `en`, `de` ship; `check:literals`; joker names/keys D-91). Plan 68 (first-start language screen `LanguageScreen.svelte`, Esc menu language rows).
- `Overlay.svelte`: general column incl. `overlay.restart` (today abandon + configure screen, decision 34 consequence) and `overlay.quit` (desktop only).
- Decision 6 (throw-away Playwright walks) → decision 115: Screen gallery: how does trivia-gallery drive a browser? (nixpkgs Playwright in a flake wrapper, nothing in package.json).
- Decision 74: Gamemaster mode earns no achievements; decision 111: Steam only (desktop rows like quit exist only in the shell).
- D-59: the family''s Spanish game changes only in the strings decided here (milestone 9). D-29: colours within `--u`.

## Design
- **Style guides** `app/client/src/locales/style/<locale>.md` (`en`, `es`, `es-ES`, `de`), `.md` style of the repo (intro paragraph, then bullets):
  - Glossary: fixed terms (mode names, joker names from D-91/plan 24, «Spielleitung»/«anfitrión», level, bundle, flag, question/answer, Esc menu rows), Steam''s own terms where Steam has one (achievements: «Steam achievements» / «logros de Steam» / «Steam-Erfolge»; Steam Cloud, Steam Deck as Steam writes them per language).
  - Address: `es` ustedes/tú as the catalog uses today; `es-ES` vosotros where `es` addresses the group (plan 69); `en` you; `de`: never assume plural, infinitive style for prompts and buttons («Frage wählen», not «Wählt eine Frage»), no «ihr/euch/euer», «du» only where infinitive can''t work (decide per string, note it in the guide).
  - Buttons and prompts: verb first / infinitive, length, capitalisation, punctuation («…» for dialogs, «¡…!» in `es`), how keys are shown.
  - Bad patterns seen so far (from the TV check: plural address in `de`, «End game (set up another)», bare «achievements»); each with a wrong → right example. Guidance, not a lint.
- **Who reads them**: a line in `AGENTS.md` Rules: "Changing UI strings (`app/client/src/locales/`): read `locales/style/<locale>.md` first; new bad patterns found go into it." Same rule in `directory.md` for `locales/style/`.
- **Translation prompts**: question texts address players too (hints, wording), so `authoring/tools/prompts/locale-<l>.md` get the guide''s address and glossary (e.g. a `{style}` placeholder filled from the guide by `prompt(..., locale=)`, or a short pasted block: the task proposes one). Prompts are the gamemaster''s: the draft goes into a pm decision (like decision 35: Translation prompts and markets per locale); nothing changes before the answer.
- **TV-check fixes** (catalogs; behaviour only for the Esc menu row):
  - `de` mode name «Spielleitermodus» (`mode.gamemaster`; `en` «Gamemaster mode», `es` «Modo anfitrión» stay).
  - Achievements: every mention says «Steam achievements» / «logros de Steam» / «Steam-Erfolge» (today `customize.body`; grep all catalogs).
  - Esc menu: `overlay.restart` → «Back to main menu» / «Volver al menú principal» / «Zurück zum Hauptmenü»; it abandons the game (confirm as today) and goes to the start screen, not the configure screen (milestone 9 replaces decision 34''s "restart = configure screen"). `overlay.quit` «Quit game» / «Salir del juego» / «Spiel beenden» in its own colour: a new `--u` token (e.g. a muted red), apart from every other button, all states (focus, hover, pad).
  - `de` without plural address: every «ihr/euch/euer/wählt/habt/seid…» string rewritten per the guide (17 hits today in `de.ts`, plus imperatives without pronoun).
  - `es-ES`: override only where it differs; `es` changes only in the strings above (D-59).
- **Screen gallery** (`trivia-gallery`, decision 115):
  - Flake wrapper `trivia-gallery` → `authoring/tools/gallery.py` (fromCheckout, pinned `python3` with `playwright`, `playwright-driver.browsers`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` unset for the browser). Starts `trivia` (built client, `npm run build` first if stale) on a free port with `TRIVIA_STATE` in a temp dir; never touches `state/`.
  - Demo save per UI language: written by the script (players, `language: {ui, questions: es}`, a game history for victory/outlook screens); a fresh save (no file) for the language screen. No `?lang=` (dev-only).
  - Shots: every screen and dialog in a step list in the script (language, start, player, choice/configure, customize, level, select, question incl. each joker, correct, wrong, victory, Esc menu general + gamemaster cheats, confirm dialogs, flag dialog, flag list, credits, supply notice/report, letter picker, game settings); the desktop bridge stubbed (`addInitScript`) so desktop-only rows (quit, Steam notice) show. 1920 × 1080; `--locale`, `--only <shot>` options. Missing media: fine (question content isn''t reviewed here).
  - Catalog keys: `t()` records rendered text → key when the page has `?gallery` (any build, a few lines in `lib/i18n.ts`, no effect otherwise); the script matches visible text nodes and `aria-label`/`title`/`placeholder` against it and stores key, text and box per shot. Unmatched visible text listed as such (catches literals).
  - Output `work/gallery/<locale>/<shot>.png` + `work/gallery/manifest.json` (shots, keys, boxes, run date, git commit). Re-runnable; overwrites.
- **Gallery page** (`trivia-authoring`, `/gallery`):
  - `authoring/server/main.py`: `GET /api/gallery` (manifest + flags), `GET /gallery/<locale>/<shot>.png` from `work/gallery/`, `POST /api/gallery/flags` (add/edit/remove).
  - `authoring/ui`: per language, the shots; hover/click a text box → its key and text; flag it with a note; list of open flags per language. Link from the authoring start page.
  - Flags in `authoring/data/ui-flags.json` (tracked, so fix tasks see them): `{id, locale, key, text, shot, note, flagged_on, fixed_on?}`. A fix pass sets `fixed_on`; the page hides fixed ones by default.
- **Review**: one `pm decision add --once` per UI language "Screen gallery reviewed? (<lang>)" (options: reviewed, flags in /gallery / reviewed, nothing to fix); the fix task waits for all four.

## Tasks (for the split)
1. **Style guides** (380): four guides as above, glossary filled from the current catalogs and plans 24/69 decisions; `AGENTS.md` Rules line; `directory.md`; 15a section "Wording and review" (what, where, the review loop). Check: the TV-check bad patterns are in the guides.
2. **TV-check fixes** (380, after 1): catalogs `es`, `es-ES`, `en`, `de` per the guides; Esc «Back to main menu» → start screen (`Game.svelte`/`Overlay.svelte`), quit colour token in `theme.css`; `de` plural sweep. `check:literals`, `npm run check`, `npm test`, `npm run build`; throw-away walk of the Esc menu (desktop stub) and the `de` screens; `plans/04-ui-tv-display.md` Esc menu spec; `pm decision consequences 74 …` if the achievement wording touches it. 15a notes.
3. **Translation prompts read the guide** (370): draft the `locale-<l>.md` change (and `prompt()` fill if a placeholder), `pm decision add` with the draft as body (recommended: approve as drafted), `pm task-pending-decision` on itself, stop; once answered apply it, `qgen validate`, consequences.
4. **`trivia-gallery`** (375): `?gallery` key trace in `lib/i18n.ts`, `authoring/tools/gallery.py`, flake wrapper, demo saves, step list for every screen/dialog, manifest; README how-to, AGENTS.md Commands line, `directory.md`. Check: a full run for all four languages, every step produces a shot, keys found for the visible text.
5. **`/gallery` page and flags** (375, after 4): server routes, `authoring/ui` page, `authoring/data/ui-flags.json`; `npm run check`, `npm run build`; browser check: flag, edit, remove, reload.
6. **Review decisions** (370, after 5): run `trivia-gallery`; four `pm decision add --once` "Screen gallery reviewed? (<lang>)" with how-to (`trivia-gallery`, `trivia-authoring` → `/gallery`); add task 7 and make it pending on them.
7. **Gallery fixes** (360; added by task 6, pending on its decisions): read open flags from `authoring/data/ui-flags.json`, fix catalogs per the guides, add new bad patterns to the guides, set `fixed_on`; re-run `trivia-gallery` for the changed shots; anything beyond catalogs → `pm task add`. Consequences on the four decisions; 15a "Wording and review" done.

## Done when
- Guides for `en`, `es`, `es-ES`, `de` exist and `AGENTS.md` points to them; the locale prompts follow the gamemaster''s answer.
- The TV-check fixes are in; the family''s Spanish game differs only in the decided strings and the Esc row''s target.
- `trivia-gallery` renders every screen and dialog in every UI language; `/gallery` shows keys and takes flags; the four review decisions are answered and their flags fixed.

## Out of scope
- Store page texts (LUI-6, S5); question translations (plans 66, 84); a wording lint; languages beyond the four catalogs.', '', '2026-10-13T15:41:11Z', 796);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (101, 14, 'masterplan', 1000, 'Masterplan of milestone 14 (opt-in telemetry), divided into parts. **This plan''s own tasks are not implementation work: each writes the plan for one part** (`pm plan add 14 …` with its priority), and that part plan is split into work tasks. The milestone ships in the launch build if ready, else in the first patch after launch: no plan of milestone 11 (launch, release check) waits for it, and no part adds a launch dependency.

## Ground rules (for every part)
- Opt-in only (AGENTS.md "telemetry only opt-in"): default off; nothing is recorded for sending, and no request goes out, without a stored yes. The game plays the same with it off, on or failing.
- Steam only (decision 111: Steam only): sending needs a valid Steam user; the server checks a Steam Web API ticket and stores no SteamID. The dev switch `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1` + a local server with `dev-*` tickets (plan 103''s dev mode) is the test path; no "without Steam" work.
- Anonymous: a random install id (not derived from the SteamID, machine or user name), no SteamID, no IP, no player names, no free text.
- Sending goes out from the shell''s main process, like the report send (plan 93: Question reports, `report:send`); the page''s request block in `app/desktop/main.mjs` stays. No retry loops, no request at start beyond what a part plan justifies.
- A failed send is silent for the player (no dialog, no toast); dev log only, never bodies or tickets.
- Our questions only: mod and player-bundle question ids (milestone 15) are not sent per question; the summary may count games with mods on.
- Builds on milestone 11 (don''t rebuild): plan 103: Report server (POST /reports) (same Worker, D1, secrets, `trivia-report-server`, ticket check, dev mode, `PRIVACY.md`); plan 93 (shell send pattern, `reportUrl`, `report:available`); plan 91 (`buildInfo()` → version + pool hash, decision 91, decision 92); plan 87 (valid Steam user, save per Steam user).
- Docs to change with the work: `plans/11-steam.md` principle 3 ("no telemetry … never phones home") and ST-5''s "no telemetry" line now contradict the milestone; they become "opt-in telemetry, see milestone 14".

## Parts

### 1. Telemetry API and server (priority just below plan 103, e.g. 690; after plan 103 tasks 865/866)
- Extends `report-server/` (plan 103''s layout): own D1 table(s) via a new migration, own routes, same ticket check with its own identity string (e.g. `trivia-telemetry`), same secrets, kill switch var, rate limits per salted SteamID hash and in total.
- Fixes the API contract first (like plan 103''s "API contract (v1)"), changed only by editing that plan: `POST /telemetry` (one session: summary + per-question rows, size cap, field rules, status codes), a pull route for authoring with the pull token (or a separate token; the plan decides), ack/delete semantics.
- Data shape, from the milestone: session summary (install id, game version, pool hash, session length, question language, per game: mode, age group or custom difficulty range, levels configured and reached, result, jokers used) and per answered question (question id, locale, right/wrong, level, age group, jokers on it, game version, pool hash).
- Retention limit (the plan proposes numbers, e.g. raw rows deleted on pull ack or after N days, whichever first; install id rows never joined to anything else); daily cron as plan 103.
- Dev mode: works with `trivia-report-server dev`, `dev-*` tickets.

### 2. Recording in the game (core, ≈ 600)
- Where the engine or client records events: per answered question and per game end, in the engine''s save transaction or a side buffer (the plan picks; keep the save shape additive, no version bump unless needed: decision 102''s precedent).
- Install id: random, created on the first yes, stored locally per install (not in the Steam Cloud save unless the plan argues for it); a "no" deletes the buffer and the id.
- Session buffer: memory, or a small local file so a crash or a failed send doesn''t lose or double-send (cap size; drop oldest).
- Session length: app start → close; question language and pool hash per game (`loadEngineData(locale).poolHash`).
- Nothing recorded while consent is off.

### 3. Sending from the shell (client-side/shell, ≈ 500; after parts 1 and 2)
- `main.mjs`: `telemetry:send` (or one call at close) with a fresh ticket, timeout, URL from the same config as `reportUrl` (one base URL, plan 93), dev override as plan 93.
- When: on game close (Electron `before-quit`, which already waits for pending save writes); the plan checks how reliable that is (Steam Deck sleep, Steam''s "stop", crashes) and, if not, also sends at game end or the next start; one request per session as the aim.
- Failure: silent, buffer kept for one later try at most (or dropped: the plan picks, counting double-sends against the server''s dedup by session id).
- Check against `trivia-report-server dev`: an opted-in session arrives; opted-out: no request at all (main log).

### 4. Consent UI and privacy texts (UI, ≈ 400)
- First-start question (default off; focus on «No»): after the language screen (`LanguageScreen.svelte`) for a fresh save, and once for existing saves without an answer; keyboard, mouse and pad as every screen.
- Esc menu (`Overlay.svelte`, general part): on/off, with a line saying what is sent.
- Where the answer lives: the save (follows the Steam user, Steam Cloud) or per install; the plan picks one and states why.
- Strings via `t()` in es, en, de (+ es-ES where it differs); `check:literals` passes.
- Privacy: extend `PRIVACY.md` and the store text written by plan 103 task 867 (what telemetry sends, what is kept, how long, how to turn it off); the opt-in screen''s short text from there.

### 5. Played vs. rated difficulty in `qgen report` (authoring, ≈ 600)
- Pull: a `qgen` command (e.g. `qgen telemetry --pull`) fetches rows with the pull token (as plan 93''s `qgen flag --pull`: `TRIVIA_REPORT_TOKEN` / `state/report-token`), stores them locally under gitignored `state/` (the repo is public: no raw player data in git), acks.
- Report: `qgen report --played` (name by the plan): per question and per age group, answered count, right rate, observed difficulty vs. rated `difficulty` (source, or the locale''s own difficulty for translations, D-81), with a minimum sample size before a question counts; per level and per age group summaries; session stats (games, results, joker use, modes, session length).
- Outliers (too easy / too hard beyond a threshold, enough samples) become review candidates: a list and a way to open them in `/review` (filter or `needs_work` with a «Datos de juego» note; the plan picks, without running `qgen rework` unasked).
- Calibration model: how a right-rate maps to the 1–15 scale per age group (`app/data/age-groups.json` windows) is the plan''s main design point; a `pm decision record` for it.

### 6. Gamemaster setup, end-to-end check and close-out (≈ 150, last)
- Deploy the server change (`trivia-report-server deploy`, migration) through a `pm decision add` (kind once) checklist for the gamemaster; workers never log in.
- End-to-end: an opted-in session from a `beta` build under Steam arrives; `qgen report` shows played vs. rated difficulty (the milestone''s "Done when").
- Docs: `plans/11-steam.md` (principle 3, ST-5), `plans/18-steam-integration.md` (IPC, ticket identity), `directory.md`, `README.md` how-tos (pull telemetry, read the report), AGENTS.md commands line if a `qgen` command is added, `plans/project.md`.

## Order
1 → 2 → 3 → 4 can overlap with 2/3 → 5 (needs 1''s contract and a dev server with data) → 6. Parts 1–3 wait for plan 103''s Worker (tasks 865, 866) and plan 93''s shell send (task 699) to exist; their plans say so instead of duplicating that work.

## Done when (milestone)
- An opted-in session''s data arrives on the server; an opted-out game sends nothing.
- `qgen report` shows played vs. rated difficulty and lists outliers as review candidates.', '', '2026-10-13T16:24:16Z', NULL);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (102, 15, 'masterplan', 1000, 'Masterplan of milestone 15 (Modding and Steam Workshop), divided into parts. **This plan''s own tasks are not implementation work: each writes the plan for one part** (`pm plan add 15 …` with the priority given below), and that part plan is split into work tasks. Milestone 11 (launch) waits for this milestone (plan 95 already lists the modding gate).

## Ground rules (for every part)
- Page only: mod code (JS, HTML, CSS) runs only in the sandboxed page, never in the main process or preload, never `require`d, `import`ed or `eval`ed by Node; the shell only reads mod files as bytes and serves them. Workshop items update silently, so this rule has no exception. Native needs → a named addition to the preload bridge, on request (a pm decision each).
- No second path: our own bundles and content load through the same format and loader as player bundles; the shell''s overlay serves our client the same way it serves a mod (our client = the last layer).
- Our data stays ours: player content never enters `authoring/`, our pool, `qgen`''s seen counts (`authoring/tools/save_file.py`), question reports (plan 93, decision 93) or telemetry per question (milestone 14, plan 101''s rule "Our questions only").
- No stability promise for mods: `window.trivia` and module paths may change in any update; the guide says so. Still, renames of public module paths are noted in patch notes.
- Steam only (decision 111): Workshop needs a valid Steam user; local `mods/` works for development under the dev switch (`TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`). No "without Steam" work.
- Offline stays: the page''s request block in `app/desktop/main.mjs` stays for mods too (no network for mod code); the Workshop download is Steam''s.
- Path safety: overlay lookups use `resolve.mjs`''s `contained()` per layer (no `..`, no symlink escape, files only).
- Strings via `t()` in es, en, de (+ es-ES where it differs); `check:literals` passes. Docs per AGENTS.md style; `directory.md` updated with every new path.
- Per-commit checks (AGENTS.md, D-73) stay green; `trivia-release-check` steps (credits both ways, smoke game, desktop builds) keep passing after each part.

## What exists (2026-10-13)
- Client build: Vite default (hashed, minified chunks) → `app/client/dist/`; `credits` plugin writes `dist/credits.json`, `CREDITS.md`; version from `app/desktop/package.json` (decision 91).
- Shell: `app/desktop/main.mjs` (`app://trivia/`, roots `dist`/`data`/`media`, flags `--import-save`, request block), `resolve.mjs` (`makeResolver`, `contained()`), `preload.cjs` (bridge **`window.trivia`**: save, fullscreen, quit, `.steam`), `lib/desktop.ts` (page side).
- Steam binding: `steamworks-ffi-node` (decision 28, plan 85) includes a Workshop manager (`getSubscribedItems`, `getItemInstallInfo`, `getItemState`, `downloadItem`, `createItem`, `startItemUpdate`, `setItemContent/Title/Description/Preview/Tags/Visibility`, `submitItemUpdate`, `getItemUpdateProgress`).
- Bundles: `app/data/bundles.json` (all bundles, `kind` base/region, languages), per pack `app/data/bundles/<id>/` (categories, names, `pool[.<locale>].json`; decision 112), loaded by `lib/data.ts` `loadEngineData()`; packs as free DLC (decision 66, decision 110); bundle selector `BundleSelector.svelte` (decision 33, decision 51).
- Achievements: plan 86 (`earned(save, data)`, qualifying games: decision 19, decision 74).
- Big screens: `Game.svelte` (1082 lines), `Question.svelte` (1243 lines): code review part 5 candidates.

## Parts

### 1. Readable client build (≈ 560; first: parts 4, 10, 11 name module paths)
- `app/client/vite.config.ts`: Rollup `preserveModules` (+ `preserveModulesRoot: "src"`), file names without hashes (`[name].js`, assets by path), `minify: false`, `sourcemap: true`; Svelte components compile to `game/Game.svelte.js`-style paths (the plan fixes the naming rule; the guide states it).
- Mod override unit = one output file at its path under `dist/`; the plan lists what can''t be overridden by path (Vite''s shared chunks, CSS extraction: one CSS per component or one `style.css`; pick the one that lets a theme mod replace or add CSS).
- Cache: no hashes means the shell must not serve stale files across updates (Electron''s HTTP cache for `app://`: check, disable or key by version).
- Check impact: build size, start time on the Steam Deck (many small module requests over `app://`), `credits` plugin, `credits_check.mjs`, `smoke.mjs`, `trivia-desktop-build` (asar), plan 99''s dev server.
- Docs: `plans/18-steam-integration.md` or `01-architecture.md` build bullet; decision record for the naming rule.

### 2. Mod format, local `mods/` and the `app://` overlay (shell, ≈ 700)
- `mod.json` schema: `id` (slug, unique; Workshop items: also their published file id), `name`, `description`, `version`, `game` (game version it was made for, semver of decision 91), `languages`, optional `entry` (part 4''s script), optional `kind` hints only for the UI (bundle, theme, code, conversion; derived from the files when missing). Validator in pure Node (`app/desktop/mods.mjs`, testable like `resolve.mjs`), JSON Schema text for the guide.
- Discovery: local folder (dev: repo `mods/`, gitignored; packaged: `<userData>/mods/`; env override `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`), Workshop folders from part 3; invalid mods listed with the reason, never loaded.
- Mod settings: enabled ids + load order per install, `<userData>/mods.json` (not the save: mods are per machine, the save is Steam Cloud''s; the plan confirms); read once at start, changes apply on restart (the overlay is fixed per run).
- Overlay: `resolve.mjs` gets an ordered list of roots: enabled mods top-down in load order, then `dist`; same for `/data/` (part 5 decides which data files a mod may overlay vs. add). First hit wins. A mod''s `index.html` at its root makes it a full conversion (it simply wins `/index.html`; the plan states how the mods screen stays reachable: part 6''s safe start).
- Main-process rule above: mod files read as bytes only; MIME by extension (`MIME` table); the plan adds a test of `..`, absolute paths, symlinks in a mod.
- Version mismatch (`game` ≠ running version): loaded, with a warning on part 6''s screen.

### 3. Workshop source: subscribed items in the shell (≈ 690; after part 2)
- At start, with a valid Steam user (decision 111): `getSubscribedItems` → `getItemState`/`getItemInstallInfo` → install folders as mod sources (`mod.json` required at the item''s root); items still downloading: listed as such, not loaded this run.
- New subscriptions while running: listed after a refresh (`ItemInstalled` callback or re-query on opening the mods screen), loaded on the next start.
- Default for newly subscribed items: enabled at the end of the load order (the plan confirms against the mods screen).
- Gamemaster setup (pm decision, kind once; workers never log in): enable Workshop for the app in Steamworks, visibility, tags (bundle, theme, code, conversion, languages), Workshop legal agreement notice, content descriptors; the second Steam account for the "Done when".
- Bridge additions (page): mod list with source, state, errors; enable/disable/reorder writes `mods.json` through IPC with the existing origin check.

### 4. Code add-ons: `mods.js` and the `window.trivia` mod API (client, ≈ 500; after parts 1, 2)
- Name clash: the preload bridge is `window.trivia` today and `contextBridge` objects are frozen; the bridge moves to another name (e.g. `window.triviaShell`; `lib/desktop.ts` the only reader) so `window.trivia` becomes the mod API. Obvious, record it (`pm decision record`).
- `index.html` loads `mods.js` (served by the shell from the enabled mods'' `entry` fields, in load order; the plan picks generated-by-the-shell vs. a static file that fetches a list) before the game mounts; each entry is `import()`ed; a throwing mod is reported on the mods screen and skipped, the game still starts.
- `window.trivia`: engine (`engine/*` actions and data hooks), catalogs (add or override strings per locale, add a locale), screens (Svelte components by name, registration of a replacement or an extra screen), events (game start/end, question shown/answered), version. Thin: re-exports of existing modules plus a few hooks; the plan lists exactly what and where (`lib/mods.ts`).
- Hooks need a defined moment: mods run after data load and before mount (or receive a `ready` callback); the plan fixes the order.
- Optional part 11 first if it changes the screen names `window.trivia` exposes.

### 5. Bundles from mods: one bundle loader (core, ≈ 600; after part 2)
- One bundle format for ours and theirs: a bundle = folder with its entry (today a row of `bundles.json`), categories, names, `pool[.<locale>].json`; the plan decides whether `bundles.json` splits into per-bundle `bundle.json` files (then `qgen export`, `qgen validate`, `authoring/tools/categories.py`, `release_check.py`, `stage-media.mjs`, `steam.py stage` follow) or the shell merges mods'' bundle lists into one served `bundles.json`. Overlaying one shared file can''t merge several mods: no.
- `loadEngineData()` reads bundles from every source in one path; a mod bundle carries `source: "mod"` (+ mod id) at runtime; the bundle selector marks it as player-made (icon + text, `t()`).
- Question ids: player questions get a namespace (e.g. `<mod id>:<id>`) so they never collide with ours; the plan checks the save (seen counts, burns, blocked, flags) and `parseSave` with such ids, and that removing a mod leaves a playable save (unknown ids ignored).
- Media in player bundles: files inside the mod folder (relative paths), served by the overlay; no Commons download (the game never downloads, AGENTS.md). Credits for mod media: the mod''s own file, shown or not (plan decides).
- Not ours: `save_file.py` and `qgen` ignore namespaced ids; flag action hidden for player questions (plan 93 reports only for ours); telemetry per question skips them (plan 101).
- Kinds: a player bundle may be a non-base bundle (adds to ours) or a base bundle (replaces ours as the game''s one base bundle, decision 38); the plan states which are allowed and how per-language availability works.

### 6. Mods screen and safe start (UI, ≈ 400; after parts 2–4)
- Screen from the start menu and the Esc menu (general part): list (name, version, source local/Workshop, kind, languages, warnings: version mismatch, invalid, download pending, failed at load), on/off, load order (move up/down), «restart to apply» (the shell restarts itself: `app.relaunch()`).
- One-time notice before the first mod is enabled: mods run code with the game''s access to the save and Steam features; stored per install.
- Crashed start: the shell writes a marker before loading the page with mods and clears it when the page reports ready (or after N seconds); a marker found at the next start → start without mods, mods screen opens with a line why. Also a manual way: a launch flag (`--no-mods`) and holding a key/pad button at start (plan picks; Steam launch options for the flag).
- Pad, keyboard, mouse as every screen (IN-1..IN-5); TV-check of plan 100.

### 7. Achievements, flags and telemetry with mods on (≈ 590; waits for decision 123: Achievements in games with mods on: allowed or off?)
- Applies decision 123 in plan 86''s `earned()` (and the unlock gate) via a per-run `modsActive` (and kinds) from the shell; games played with mods record that in the game row so the retroactive pass stays correct (save field: additive, decision 102''s precedent).
- Flags: hidden for player questions (part 5 marks them); reports (plan 93) and telemetry (plan 101) only for our questions and, per plan 101, a "mods on" count in the summary.
- Rich presence: unchanged or "modded" (plan picks).

### 8. Publishing: upload a mod folder as a Workshop item (≈ 670; after parts 2, 3)
- The command ships with the game, so players can use it: the game executable with a flag, e.g. `trivia --publish-mod <folder> [--changenote …] [--visibility …]`, runs headless in the main process (our code; it reads the folder, never runs it), validates `mod.json` (part 2''s validator), creates the item on first publish (writes the published file id into the folder''s `mod.json` or a sidecar), then `startItemUpdate` → content, title, description, preview image, tags, languages → `submitItemUpdate` with progress on stdout; `--dry-run`.
- Needs a valid Steam user and the Steam client; the Workshop legal agreement result is shown with its URL.
- Later maybe an in-game button: out of scope, a backlog row.
- Docs: guide section and README how-to; `trivia-steam` stays for our own builds, not mods.

### 9. Player bundle kit: format doc, template, AI prompt (docs, ≈ 350; after part 5)
- Bundle format documented for players (fields, categories, names, pool file, media, languages, difficulty scale 1–15 and age groups from `app/data/age-groups.json`), from part 5''s final format.
- Template mod folder (one small bundle, two languages, one image question) that loads as is.
- An AI prompt players paste into their own AI: produces the pool file in our format, asks for their topic, audience and language; adapted from `authoring/tools/prompts/` house style but standalone (no `qgen`, no web search assumed); the plan states how a player checks the result (the mods screen''s error list, or `trivia --check-mod <folder>` sharing part 8''s validator).
- Where: part 10''s guide folder; shipped in the game''s install folder too, or only online (plan picks).

### 10. Modding guide, examples and the store link (docs, ≈ 300; after parts 1–9)
- Guide (public in the repo, e.g. `modding/README.md`; the plan picks the path, linked from `README.md` and `directory.md`): mod format, sources and load order, overlay rules (module paths from part 1), `window.trivia` (part 4), mods screen and safe start, publishing (part 8), player bundles (part 9), no stability promise, page-only rule.
- Four example mods that load from `mods/` as is: a bundle, a CSS theme, a rules tweak (code add-on via `window.trivia`), a minimal full conversion (own `index.html`).
- Store page link: add to `plans/17a-store-page.md` (plan 88: the gamemaster enters store texts) and Workshop description; mention mods in the store feature bullets (pm decision for the text, as plan 88''s sets).

### 11. Optional: split `Game.svelte` and `Question.svelte` (client, ≈ 520; before part 4''s screen list and part 10''s examples)
- Code review part 5 candidates (`code-review.md`): screen state machine vs. per-screen pieces; media, answers, reveal, jokers as own modules, so mods replace small files. The plan decides whether it''s worth it now; if not, it drops itself with a reason. No behaviour change; per-commit checks and a smoke game.

### 12. End-to-end check and close-out (≈ 150, last)
- The milestone''s "Done when": the four example mods each load from `mods/` and, published by part 8, from the Workshop on a second Steam account (gamemaster runs the Steam steps from a checklist, pm decision kind once).
- Store page shows the guide link (gamemaster confirms).
- Release check: does `trivia-release-check` need a mods row (game starts with `--no-mods` and with the examples)? Add it to plan 104 if so.
- Docs: `plans/18-steam-integration.md` (Workshop, bridge rename, IPC), `plans/01-architecture.md` (overlay), `plans/11-steam.md`, `plans/project.md`, `README.md`, AGENTS.md "Commands" (publish flag, `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`).

## Order
1 → 2 → (3, 5 in parallel) → 4 (after 1, 2; 11 before it if done) → 6; 7 after 5 and decision 123; 8 after 3; 9 after 5; 10 after all others; 12 last.

