INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (105, 14, 'Telemetry API and server', 690, 'Part 1 of masterplan 14 (plan 101): the server half of opt-in telemetry. It extends plan 103''s Worker (`report-server/`, Cloudflare Workers + D1) with one route that takes one session''s summary and per-question rows, a pull/ack pair for authoring, its own table, its own ticket identity, kill switch and rate limits. The API contract below is fixed first; parts 2 (recording), 3 (shell send) and 5 (`qgen` pull/report) build on it, and it changes only by editing this plan. Plan 101''s ground rules apply: opt-in, Steam only (decision 111: Steam only), anonymous, silent failure.

## Builds on (don''t rebuild)
- Plan 103: Report server (POST /reports): layout, `wrangler.toml`, D1 binding `DB`, secrets (`STEAM_WEB_API_KEY`, `PULL_TOKEN`, `HASH_SALT`), error body shape, 404/405, `scheduled` cron (03:00 UTC), `trivia-report-server dev|deploy|tail`, fake `dev-*` tickets. Its work: task 865: Report server: Worker, D1 schema and local dev mode, task 866: Report server: Steam ticket check. This plan''s tasks run after both exist (priority 690 < 700); if one is still open, wait, don''t duplicate it.
- Tooling: decision 117: Report server tooling: wrangler from nixpkgs in the flake? (pending when written; follow whatever it answers, no new dependency here).
- Version and pool hash: plan 91 `buildInfo()` (decision 91, decision 92: Pool version: export hash in pool.json, 8 hex).
- Engine names: modes `gamemaster | solo | rr-coop | rr-competitive | timed` (`app/client/src/lib/types.ts` `GameMode`), jokers `hint | skip | easier | category | snipe` (`STANDARD_JOKERS`), age groups `app/data/age-groups.json` (`kids`, `young_teens`, `young_adults`, `adults`), our question ids `q-NNNN` (base and regional bundles alike).

## Storage: one row per session
- D1 free plan ≈ 100k rows written/day: one row per answered question would hit it at a few thousand sessions. So a session is one row: the validated body re-serialized from known fields only (JSON text). Analysis happens in authoring (part 5) after the pull; the server never queries inside it.
- Migration `report-server/migrations/0002_telemetry.sql` (plan 103''s tables untouched):
  - `telemetry(session_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, received_day TEXT NOT NULL, body TEXT NOT NULL)`; `received_day` = UTC `YYYY-MM-DD` (no time of day); index on `received_day`.
  - `telemetry_rate(user_hash TEXT, day TEXT, count INTEGER, PRIMARY KEY(user_hash, day))`; same HMAC as plan 103 (`HASH_SALT`, SteamID64), global row `''*''`. Own table so report and telemetry limits never share counts.
- Not stored: SteamID, user hash next to data, IP, headers, client clock times, names, any free text. The install id is in `body` only; never joined with `telemetry_rate` or `reports`.

## Retention
- Session row deleted on pull ack; unacknowledged: deleted `TELEMETRY_KEEP_DAYS` (30) days after `received_day`.
- `telemetry_rate` rows deleted once their day is over.
- Both in plan 103''s daily `scheduled` handler (extended, same cron).
- The gamemaster''s pulled copy lives under gitignored `state/` (part 5); its retention is part 5''s and part 4''s privacy text''s business.

## API contract (v1)
Same base URL, JSON/UTF-8, error body `{"error": "<code>", "field"?: "<path>", "detail"?: "<short English text>"}`, no CORS, as plan 103.

### `POST /telemetry` (the shell, once per session)
Request `Content-Type: application/json`, body ≤ 64 KiB:
```json
{
  "session": "6f1c2a9e-3b7d-4c1e-9a2f-0d5e8b7c6a41",
  "install": "0b9d4e2f-8a6c-4f1b-b3d7-5e2a9c1f8d60",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "length_s": 3720,
  "games": [
    {
      "mode": "solo",
      "locale": "es",
      "pool": "1f30d10c",
      "age_group": "young_teens",
      "difficulty": null,
      "levels": 12,
      "reached": 9,
      "result": "lost",
      "customized": false,
      "mods": false,
      "jokers": {"hint": 3, "skip": 1, "easier": 0, "category": 1, "snipe": 0},
      "answers": [
        {"question": "q-0001", "level": 1, "correct": true, "jokers": {}},
        {"question": "q-0417", "level": 2, "correct": false, "jokers": {"hint": 2, "easier": 1}}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "ticket": "14000000…(hex)"
}
```
| Field | Rule |
|---|---|
| `session` | required, UUID v4 lowercase (random per session, from part 2): the dedup key |
| `install` | required, UUID v4 lowercase (random install id, part 2; never derived from SteamID, machine or user) |
| `version` | required, plan 103''s version regex, ≤ 32 chars |
| `length_s` | required, integer 0–86400 (app start → close; part 2 caps it) |
| `games` | required array, 0–30 items (0: a session without a game still counts) |
| `games[].mode` | required, `^[a-z][a-z-]{0,23}$` (today''s five modes; a pattern so a new mode needs no deploy) |
| `games[].locale` | required, plan 103''s `lang` regex (question language of that game) |
| `games[].pool` | required, `^[0-9a-f]{8}$` or `null` |
| `games[].age_group` / `games[].difficulty` | exactly one non-null: `age_group` `^[a-z_]{1,24}$`, or `difficulty` `{"start": 1–15, "end": start–15}` (decision 33 custom range) |
| `games[].levels` | required, integer 1–20 (configured) |
| `games[].reached` | required, integer 0–`levels` (highest level reached) |
| `games[].result` | required, `won` \| `lost` \| `abandoned` \| `open` (session closed mid-game) |
| `games[].customized` | required boolean (decision 19: customized solo game) |
| `games[].mods` | required boolean (mod or player bundles on, milestone 15) |
| `games[].jokers` | required object: uses per joker, keys `^[a-z]{1,16}$` (≤ 8 keys), values integer 0–99 |
| `games[].answers` | required array, 0–100 items; total over all games ≤ 500 |
| `answers[].question` | required, `^q-\d{4,6}$`: our questions only; anything else (mod, player bundle) → 400 |
| `answers[].level` | required, integer 1–`levels` |
| `answers[].correct` | required boolean (answered questions only; a question replaced by `skip` has no row) |
| `answers[].jokers` | required object as `games[].jokers`, jokers used on this question (`{}`: none) |
| `ticket` | required, hex 2–4096: `GetAuthTicketForWebApi("trivia-telemetry")`, fresh per send |
- Unknown fields anywhere: dropped (the stored body has the known fields only, so no free text can slip in). Strings outside the patterns → 400 with `field` as a path (`games[0].answers[3].question`).
- Per-answer locale, version, pool and age group come from its game / the session (one version per session, locale and pool per game); part 5 flattens them.
- Order of checks: kill switch → content type/size/JSON/fields → ticket with Steam (identity `TELEMETRY_IDENTITY`) → dedup → rate limits → insert (row + both rate counters in one D1 batch).

| Status | Body | Meaning for the shell (part 3 decides the buffer) |
|---|---|---|
| `201` | `{"stored": true}` | stored; drop the buffer |
| `200` | `{"stored": false, "duplicate": true}` | session id already stored (or acked within the last `TELEMETRY_KEEP_DAYS`, see dedup); drop the buffer; doesn''t count against limits |
| `400` | `{"error": "bad_request", "field": "…"}` | never retry; drop |
| `401` | `{"error": "invalid_ticket"}` | drop (or one later try: part 3) |
| `413` | `{"error": "too_large"}` | drop |
| `415` | `{"error": "unsupported_media_type"}` | drop |
| `429` | `{"error": "rate_limited", "retry_after": <s>}` + `Retry-After` | drop or one later try |
| `502` | `{"error": "steam_unavailable"}` | one later try at most |
| `503` | `{"error": "closed"}` (`TELEMETRY_OPEN` = `"0"`) | drop |
| `500` | `{"error": "internal"}` | one later try at most |
- Dedup: `session_id` primary key; acked ids are kept as bare tombstones (`telemetry_seen(session_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, day TEXT)`, same migration, deleted with the retention cron after `TELEMETRY_KEEP_DAYS`) so a late resend after a pull isn''t stored twice. No other data in it.
- Rate limits per UTC day, vars not code: `RATE_TELEMETRY_USER` 10 sessions per salted SteamID hash, `RATE_TELEMETRY_TOTAL` 2000 in total (≈ 6000 D1 writes/day worst case, far under the free plan).

### `GET /telemetry?limit=N` (part 5''s pull)
- `Authorization: Bearer <PULL_TOKEN>`: the same secret as `GET /reports` (one gamemaster, one token file: `TRIVIA_REPORT_TOKEN` / `state/report-token`, plan 93); wrong/missing → `401 {"error": "unauthorized"}`, constant-time compare.
- `limit` default 100, max 200; oldest `received_day` first, then `session_id`.
- `200 {"sessions": [{"received_day": "2026-11-02", "session": "…", "install": "…", "version": "1.0.0", "length_s": 3720, "games": [ … as posted, ticket removed … ]}], "more": false}`.
- Read-only: same rows until acked.

### `POST /telemetry/ack`
- Same bearer token; body `{"sessions": ["<uuid>", …]}` (1–200).
- `200 {"acked": n}`: deletes those rows, writes their tombstones; unknown or already acked ids count 0 (idempotent). `400`, `401` as above.

`GET /health` stays as plan 103 defines it (no telemetry field; v1 here = path exists).

## Ticket identity, vars, secrets
- Identity `trivia-telemetry` (var `TELEMETRY_IDENTITY`; the shell''s constant in part 3). Plan 103''s `src/steam.ts` takes the identity as a parameter (task 866 builds it so; if it hard-codes `TICKET_IDENTITY`, this plan''s task makes it a parameter).
- New `wrangler.toml` vars: `TELEMETRY_IDENTITY`, `TELEMETRY_OPEN = "1"`, `RATE_TELEMETRY_USER = "10"`, `RATE_TELEMETRY_TOTAL = "2000"`, `TELEMETRY_KEEP_DAYS = "30"`. No new secret.
- Logging as plan 103: no bodies, tickets, SteamIDs, hashes, install or session ids in `console.*`.

## Layout (additions to `report-server/`)
- `src/telemetry.ts`: the three routes + retention SQL, called from `src/index.ts`''s router and `scheduled`.
- `src/validate-telemetry.ts`: pure body check + re-serialization (known fields only), testable under `node`.
- `migrations/0002_telemetry.sql`.
- `fixtures/telemetry-session.json`: the example body above (valid, `dev-` ticket) for parts 3 and 5.

## Dev mode
- `trivia-report-server dev` unchanged: applies `0002` locally, fake `dev-*` tickets as plan 103 (`dev-invalid` → 401, `dev-steamdown` → 502), only with `DEV_FAKE_TICKETS=1` and a localhost host.
- `trivia-report-server seed-telemetry [N]` (dev only, refuses a non-localhost URL): posts N synthetic sessions (random ids, `dev-<n>` tickets, question ids from `app/data/pool.json`, right rates drawn from rated difficulty vs. level window) so part 5 can build its pull and report before parts 2–3 send real data.

## Tasks to split into
1. **Telemetry routes, migration and validation** (after task 865 and task 866; waits on decision 117 like them) — `0002_telemetry.sql` (`telemetry`, `telemetry_rate`, `telemetry_seen`), `src/validate-telemetry.ts`, `src/telemetry.ts` per the contract (status table, order of checks, dedup + tombstones, rate limits from vars, kill switch, bearer pull/ack), `src/steam.ts` identity as parameter if needed, retention in `scheduled`, vars in `wrangler.toml`, `fixtures/telemetry-session.json`; `npm run check` covers it. Done when a throw-away curl script against `trivia-report-server dev` shows: every status row of `POST /telemetry` (201, 200 duplicate, 400 incl. a mod question id `mod-x:1` and a field path, 401, 413, 415, 429 on the 11th session of one fake user, 502, 503), unknown fields absent from the pulled body, pull → ack → pull empty, resend of an acked session → 200 duplicate, ack idempotent, 401 without token, cron deletes a backdated session, tombstone and old `telemetry_rate` row; reports routes unchanged. Docs: `directory.md` (`report-server/` row: telemetry), `plans/11-steam.md` ST-5 one line "telemetry route: plan <this>".
2. **Dev seed for telemetry** (after task 1) — `trivia-report-server seed-telemetry [N]` in the flake wrapper (a small Node or shell script under `report-server/`, no new dependency) per "Dev mode". Done when `seed-telemetry 50` against `trivia-report-server dev` stores 50 sessions that `GET /telemetry` returns, and running it against a non-localhost URL refuses. AGENTS.md "Commands" `trivia-report-server` line mentions it.
- Not here: deploy and the migration on the real D1 (part 6''s gamemaster checklist), privacy text (part 4), the shell''s send (part 3), the pull command (part 5).

## Done when
- The contract above exists and parts 2, 3, 5 cite it; `report-server/` takes, dedups, rate-limits, serves and deletes telemetry sessions locally with `dev-*` tickets; `npm run check` passes; nothing deployed by a worker.', '', '2026-10-13T17:27:26Z', 872);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (106, 14, 'Telemetry recording in the game', 600, 'Part 2 of masterplan 14 (plan 101): what the game records for opt-in telemetry, where it keeps it until part 3 sends it, and how a "no" wipes it. The save already holds everything a session summary and the per-question rows need (games with mode, settings, locale, result, and a history of answers with level, right/wrong and jokers), so recording is a pure derivation from the save after each action into a small per-install side file in the shell; the save''s shape doesn''t change. The output is exactly plan 105''s `POST /telemetry` body minus `ticket`. Plan 101''s ground rules apply: opt-in, Steam only (decision 111: Steam only), anonymous, silent failure.

## Builds on (don''t rebuild)
- Plan 105: Telemetry API and server: the API contract (v1) is the target shape; its field names and limits are used as is (`session`, `install`, `version`, `length_s`, `games[]`, `answers[]`, ≤ 30 games, ≤ 100 answers per game, ≤ 500 in total, ≤ 64 KiB, our ids `^q-\d{4,6}$` only). decision 118: Telemetry server storage and pull token.
- Plan 87: Steam Cloud saves: `validUser` (shell `validUser`, page `lib/desktop.ts`), the Auto-Cloud pattern (`trivia/save/{64BitSteamID}`, `game.json`, non-recursive): the telemetry folder lies outside it, so it never syncs.
- Plan 91: `buildInfo()`, decision 91 (version from `app/desktop/package.json`; the shell has it as `app.getVersion()`), decision 92 (pool hash, 8 hex; `loadEngineData(locale).poolHash`, `null` for an old pool).
- `app/desktop/save.mjs` (atomic `.tmp` + rename) and `main.mjs`''s `queued()` / `before-quit` wait: the side file uses the same pattern.
- Part 3 (task 874: write plan: Telemetry send from the shell) sends and deletes the files; part 4 (task 875: write plan: Telemetry consent UI and privacy texts) asks the question and calls the consent setter below. Neither is done here.

## Choices (decision recorded with this plan: Telemetry recording: derived from the save into a per-install side file)
- **Side buffer, not the save.** No field added to `SaveData`, `GameRow` or `GameState`, no `SAVE_VERSION` bump (decision 102''s precedent of additive changes isn''t even needed). Reasons: the save follows the Steam user through Steam Cloud (plan 87), so a buffer in it would be sent once per machine; a "no" must delete the data without touching the family''s progress; telemetry state must never make a save unreadable.
- **Derived, not event-logged.** After every saved action the page rebuilds this session''s games from the save (`history` entries with `correct`, `result`, `settings`, `locale`). Undo (gamemaster correction) therefore removes the undone answer from telemetry too, a `deletePlayer` doesn''t remove what was already recorded (entries stay in the buffer), and there is no second source of truth to drift.
- **Page builds, shell stores.** The page has the engine and pool data; the sandboxed page can''t write files. The shell owns the ids, the clock and the file. Network: none here.

## Install id and consent storage (`app/desktop/telemetry.mjs`)
- Folder `T`: `TRIVIA_TELEMETRY_DIR` if set, else packaged `<userData>/telemetry` (sibling of `save/`, outside Auto-Cloud), dev `state/telemetry` (gitignored `state/`).
- `T/install.json`: `{"consent": true, "install": "<uuid v4>"}` or `{"consent": false}`; missing file = never asked. The id is `crypto.randomUUID()` (lowercase v4), created on the first yes, not derived from SteamID, machine or user name.
- Yes → id created if missing. No → `install.json` becomes `{"consent": false}`, `T/sessions/` deleted (buffer and id gone); a later yes gets a new id.
- Proposed consent storage = this per-install file (the id is per install; a yes on one machine shouldn''t make another machine send; nothing in the cloud save). Part 4 decides; if its plan keeps the answer in the save instead, it edits this plan, and the save''s answer then drives `setConsent()` at load so the id file still follows it.
- Only with a valid user (`validUser` in `main.mjs`): without one every telemetry IPC is a no-op (`state` → `{consent: null, available: false}`), no folder created.

## Session buffer
- One session = one app run. At start (consent yes, valid user) the shell picks `session = crypto.randomUUID()` and writes `T/sessions/<session>.json` with `games: []` (a session without a game still counts, plan 105). A "yes" given mid-run starts the session then.
- File (shell-owned fields + the page''s games): `{"session", "install", "version", "length_s", "games": [ … contract games … ], "index": [{"key": "<game id>@<started_on>", "from": <history index>}]}`. `index` is page bookkeeping; part 3 strips it (the server would drop it anyway).
- `length_s` = `Math.round(process.uptime())` capped at 86400, stamped by the shell on every write and once more in `before-quit` (part 3 hooks the send there). After a crash the file holds the last checkpoint''s length; games still running carry `result: "open"`.
- Writes: atomic (`.tmp` + rename) through a queue like `queued()`; `before-quit` already waits for pending writes, the telemetry queue joins that wait.
- Rollover: when the next write would exceed a contract cap (30 games, 500 answers, 60 KiB serialized), the shell closes the current file and starts a new session id; the page''s games that didn''t fit go into the new one. Per game the first 100 answers are kept.
- Cap on pending files: at most 5 in `T/sessions/` (finished sessions part 3 couldn''t send plus the current one); on a new session the oldest by mtime is deleted. Leftover files from earlier runs are part 3''s "one later try" input; the shell exports `pending()` (list + read) for it, no IPC.
- Double-send: part 3 deletes a file after any final answer (plan 105''s status table); the server dedups by `session` (plan 105''s `telemetry_seen`), so a crash between send and delete costs nothing.

## What a game becomes (page: `app/client/src/lib/telemetry.ts`, pure part in `app/client/src/engine/telemetry.ts`)
- Session''s games: every game row first touched in this run: a game started this run (`from` 0) and the game running at load (`from` = its history length at load, so answers from an earlier session aren''t sent twice when a game spans two evenings). The first record of a key in a session fixes its `from` in `index` (survives a page reload: the page reads the buffer back from the shell).
- Per game (contract names): `mode` = `settings.mode ?? "gamemaster"`; `locale` = `gameLocale(row)`; `pool` = that locale''s `loadEngineData(locale).poolHash`; `age_group` / `difficulty` from `gameChoice()` (exactly one non-null); `levels` = `settings.levels ?? 12`; `reached` = `levels` when won, else `state.level` (the level the game is on or ended on); `result` = `row.result ?? "open"`; `customized` = `settings.customized ?? false`; `mods` = `false` until milestone 15 gives a test (a `PLACEHOLDER(<task id>)` comment isn''t needed: the field is correct today); `jokers` = per-joker counts over this session''s history slice (`history[from..]` jokers, plus `state.jokers` on the card on screen), zero counts kept for the five `STANDARD_JOKERS`.
- Per answer: `history[from..]` entries with `correct` (replaced/skipped entries have `outcome` and no row, as plan 105 says); `question` = entry id, only if it matches `^q-\d{4,6}$` and is in one of our shipped bundles (`data.bundles`; milestone 15 adds the mod/player-bundle exclusion at this one spot); `level` = entry level (skipped if outside 1–`levels`, e.g. after a cheat level jump); `correct`; `jokers` = counts of the entry''s `jokers` list (`{}` when none).
- Not recorded: player names, player ids, game ids (the key stays in `index`, never sent), dates/times, the save''s flags or notes, any text.
- Checkpoint: in `lib/engine.ts` `exec()` after a successful `run()` adopts its save (answer, game end, new, abandon, undo, jokers, cheats), and once in `load()` for the running game; fire-and-forget, never awaited by the UI, errors to the dev console only (no notice, no toast).
- Consent gate: the page reads `telemetry.state()` once per page load and on part 4''s setter; while not `true` (or no valid user, or no bridge as in the browser game and plan 99''s dev shim), the builder never runs and nothing goes to the shell. The shell checks again and refuses writes without a stored yes.

## Bridge (`preload.cjs`, `lib/desktop.ts`)
- `telemetry.state()` → `{available: boolean, consent: boolean | null}` (part 4: ask when `available && consent === null`).
- `telemetry.setConsent(yes: boolean)` → as "Install id and consent storage".
- `telemetry.load()` → the current session''s `{games, index}` or null; `telemetry.record({games, index})` → stamps `session`, `install`, `version`, `length_s`, writes, rolls over.
- Optional on the bridge type (the browser game and the dev server shim lack it → no-op). Channels checked by `bridge()`''s origin check like the others; arguments validated in the shell (plain objects, size).

## Tasks to split into
1. **Shell: telemetry store** — `app/desktop/telemetry.mjs` (folder, `install.json`, consent set/get, session file per run, atomic writes, rollover, 5-file cap, `pending()` export, delete on no) wired in `main.mjs` (`validUser` gate, `before-quit` waits for its queue, last `length_s` stamp), IPC `telemetry:state|consent|load|record`, `preload.cjs`, `lib/desktop.ts` types (optional `telemetry`). No network, no logging of ids or bodies. Check (throw-away script or `trivia-desktop` with `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`, `TRIVIA_TELEMETRY_DIR` in `/tmp`): no `install.json` → no `sessions/` written; `{consent: true}` via `setConsent(true)` → id created, a session file per run with `games: []`, `length_s` grows; `setConsent(false)` → `sessions/` gone, no id; six runs → five files; no valid user → nothing under `T`. Docs: `plans/18-steam-integration.md` IPC list, `directory.md` (`app/desktop/telemetry.mjs`, `state/telemetry/`).
2. **Page: recording from the save** (after task 1) — `engine/telemetry.ts` (pure: save rows + `index` + data lookup → contract games, caps, our-ids filter, `from` slices), `lib/telemetry.ts` (consent cache, `loadEngineData` per game locale for the pool hash, bridge calls, swallowed errors), checkpoint in `lib/engine.ts` `exec()` and `load()`. Check with `trivia-desktop` (`TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`, consent set by hand in `install.json` until part 4 exists): a solo game won and a gamemaster game lost → the session file validates against plan 105''s `report-server/src/validate-telemetry.ts` if it exists, else against the contract table by a throw-away script (fields, caps, one non-null of `age_group`/`difficulty`, answers only `q-NNNN`); a jokered question carries its counts; an undone answer disappears; quit mid-game → `result: "open"`; restart and finish that game → only the new answers in the new session; consent off → the file isn''t touched and no IPC record call happens. `npm run check`, `npm test`, `npm run build` pass. Docs: `plans/12-client-engine.md` short section "Telemetry recording" (derived from the save, side file, no save change, consent gate).
- Not here: sending, retries and the close hook''s request (part 3), the question and Esc menu toggle and privacy texts (part 4), the pull and the report (part 5).

## Done when
- With a stored yes and a valid user, each run leaves one session file whose content is plan 105''s request body minus `ticket` (after stripping `index`), covering every game played that run and its answered `q-NNNN` questions; a crash leaves the last checkpoint.
- With no answer or a no, no file is written and no telemetry IPC call carries data; a no deletes the id and every session file; the save file is byte-for-byte the same shape as before this plan.', '', '2026-10-13T17:30:43Z', 873);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (107, 14, 'Telemetry send from the shell', 500, 'Part 3 of masterplan 14 (plan 101): the shell''s main process sends each recorded session to the telemetry server. The page records (part 2), the server takes and dedups (part 1); this plan only adds the request: when it goes out, with which ticket and URL, what happens on failure, and a check that an opted-out game sends nothing at all. Plan 101''s ground rules apply: opt-in, Steam only (decision 111: Steam only), anonymous, silent failure; the page''s request block in `main.mjs` (`onBeforeRequest`) stays untouched.

## Builds on (don''t rebuild)
- Plan 105: Telemetry API and server: `POST /telemetry` contract (v1), its status table, ticket identity `trivia-telemetry`, dedup by `session` incl. tombstones after ack (`telemetry_seen`), decision 118: Telemetry server storage and pull token. Dev: `trivia-report-server dev` (`127.0.0.1:8787`, `dev-*` tickets, `dev-invalid` → 401, `dev-steamdown` → 502), `fixtures/telemetry-session.json`.
- Plan 106: Telemetry recording in the game, decision 119: Telemetry recording: derived from the save into a per-install side file: `app/desktop/telemetry.mjs` (folder `T`, `T/install.json` consent + install id, `T/sessions/<session>.json` = contract body minus `ticket` plus page bookkeeping `index`, atomic writes through its own queue, ≤ 5 files, `pending()` export, last `length_s` stamp in `before-quit`, delete on "no").
- Plan 93: Question reports, task 699: Shell: report ticket and send (report:available, report:send): `reportUrl` in `app/desktop/package.json`, dev override `TRIVIA_REPORT_URL` only with `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1` (ticket then `dev-<random>`), fresh ticket per send cancelled after the response, Node `fetch` with timeouts, ticket never logged nor given to the page.
- Plan 85: Steamworks binding: `steam.user.getAuthTicketForWebApi(identity)` is async (callback via the 100 ms `runCallbacks` timer), `cancelAuthTicket`; `stopSteam()` runs in `will-quit`, so every ticket is fetched before that.
- Wait: this plan''s task runs after task 699, plan 105''s routes task (task 1 of plan 105, from task 878''s split) and plan 106''s shell store task (task 1 of plan 106, from task 879''s split). If one is still open: wait, don''t duplicate it.

## Choices (decision recorded with this plan: Telemetry send: at quit, one later try at the next start)
- **Send at quit, from `before-quit`.** One request per session file. Normal closes (window ✕, Alt+F4, «Salir» in the Esc menu, Steam''s "Exit game" on Deck) all reach `before-quit`.
- **No send at game end.** The server dedups by `session` and never updates a stored row (plan 105), so a mid-session send would either lose the later games or need a rollover per game; calibration isn''t time-critical, so the next-start try covers unreliable closes instead.
- **One later try at the next start, then delete.** Every file is sent at most twice: at its own quit, and once at a later start. After the second try it''s deleted whatever the answer. A crash between send and delete costs nothing (server dedup, plan 105 `200 duplicate`).
- **Unreliable closes and how each is covered** (task 1 checks the first three locally, part 6 the Steam ones):
  - crash, `kill -9`, power loss: no event; the file holds the last checkpoint (plan 106) → next start.
  - Linux `SIGTERM` (Steam "Stop" on Linux/Deck sends it, then kills): add `process.on("SIGTERM", () => app.quit())` if Electron doesn''t already route it through `before-quit` (task 1 checks which); the quit budget below stays under Steam''s grace period; if killed first → next start.
  - Windows Steam "Stop" (`TerminateProcess`), OS shutdown: no event → next start.
  - Deck sleep: the process is frozen, not quit; resume and close is a normal quit; no network on wake → retryable status → next start.
  - Never started again: that session is lost. Accepted (opt-in statistics, no player-visible effect).
- **URL: one base.** `reportUrl` in `app/desktop/package.json` is the server''s base URL; main appends `/reports` and `/telemetry`. If task 699 stored the full `/reports` URL, task 1 turns it into the base (one value, plan 93''s place) and adjusts the report send. Empty → no telemetry request either. Same dev override `TRIVIA_REPORT_URL`.

## Send (`app/desktop/telemetry.mjs` + `main.mjs`)
- Gate, checked at every send, nothing else touched before: valid user (`validUser`), `install.json` consent `true`, non-empty URL. Any no → no ticket, no request, no file read beyond `install.json`.
- Body: the session file parsed, `index` removed, `install` taken from the current `install.json` (a file written under an older id that was since revoked doesn''t exist: "no" deletes `sessions/`), `ticket` added; serialized ≤ 64 KiB, else deleted unsent. Unreadable JSON → deleted, dev log.
- Ticket: fresh per request, `getAuthTicketForWebApi("trivia-telemetry")` (constant next to the reports'' identity), cancelled after the response; 2 s timeout for the callback (→ treated as status 0). Dev switch without a SteamID: `dev-<random>` as task 699.
- Request: `POST <base>/telemetry`, `Content-Type: application/json`, Node `fetch` with `AbortSignal.timeout`; reuse task 699''s ticket + fetch helper (one function, identity and path as parameters; factor it out if task 699 inlined it).
- Answer → file (plan 105''s table): `201`, `200 duplicate`, `400`, `413`, `415`, `503` → delete. `401`, `429`, `500`, `502`, no connection/timeout (status 0) → keep for the one later try (mark it: rename to `<session>.retry.json`, so `pending()` still lists it and the 5-file cap still counts it); at the later try any answer → delete.
- Silent: no dialog, no toast, nothing to the page. Dev log: one `console.error` line per request, `trivia: telemetry → <status>` (and `kept` / `dropped`); never body, ticket, session or install id, URL query or SteamID. With the gate closed: one line at start `trivia: telemetry off` (the done-when''s log proof), no other line.
- No IPC for sending: the page never triggers a request (plan 106''s channels stay the only telemetry IPC).

## When
- **Quit**: `before-quit` handler (extends the existing one, which waits for `pending` save writes): on the first `before-quit` with the gate open and a current session file, `preventDefault()`, hide the window (the player sees the game closed), wait for the save queue and plan 106''s telemetry queue (its last `length_s` stamp), send the current file, then `app.quit()` again with a `flushed` flag so the second `before-quit` passes straight through. Budget: 4 s for ticket + request together; on timeout quit anyway (status 0 → `.retry.json`). Steam stays up until `will-quit` (`stopSteam`), so the ticket is still available.
- A session without games (`games: []`) is sent too (plan 105 counts it); a "no" given during the run means the file is already gone.
- **Next start**: once, 30 s after the window has loaded (not on the start path, never before the first screen), if the gate is open: every file from `pending()` except the current session''s, oldest first, one at a time, normal timeout (5 s connect + 10 s total, as task 699); `<session>.json` (never tried: crash/kill) and `<session>.retry.json` both get this one try, then are deleted whatever the answer. Justification for a request at start (plan 101 ground rule): the only way crashed/killed sessions arrive.
- Kill switch (`503 closed`): only drops that file; no extra state.

## Tasks to split into
1. **Shell: telemetry send at quit and next start** (after task 699, plan 105''s routes task, plan 106''s shell store task) — in `app/desktop/telemetry.mjs` + `main.mjs` per "Send" and "When": gate, body from the session file, shared ticket + fetch helper with the report send (identity/path parameters), base URL from `reportUrl` (adjust task 699''s use if it is the full URL), quit flush with 4 s budget and `flushed` flag, `SIGTERM` → `app.quit()` if needed, `.retry.json` marking, next-start pass 30 s after load, dev log lines. No new dependency, no new IPC. Check against `trivia-report-server dev` with `trivia-desktop`, `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`, `TRIVIA_REPORT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787`, `TRIVIA_TELEMETRY_DIR` in `/tmp`: consent yes, play one question, quit → `GET /telemetry` (pull token) shows the session with its game, file gone, log `→ 201`; quit with the server down → `.retry.json` kept, quit fast (≤ 4 s); next start with the server up → sent after ~30 s, file gone; `kill -9` mid-game → next start sends it with `result: "open"`; `kill -TERM` → sent at quit (or record that Electron ignores it and the handler fixes it); resend of an acked session → `200 duplicate`, file gone; consent off or no `install.json` → log `telemetry off`, no `telemetry →` line, the dev server''s log shows no request. Per-commit checks (AGENTS.md). Docs: `plans/18-steam-integration.md` (ticket identity `trivia-telemetry`, base URL, send at quit / next start), `directory.md` row for `app/desktop/telemetry.mjs` (send part).
- Not here: the consent question and Esc toggle (part 4, task 875: write plan: Telemetry consent UI and privacy texts), the pull (part 5), deploy and the Steam-side close checks (Steam "Stop" on Linux/Deck and Windows, Deck sleep → resume → quit; part 6, task 877: write plan: Telemetry gamemaster setup, end-to-end check and close-out, takes them as checklist rows).

## Done when
- Against `trivia-report-server dev`, an opted-in session arrives on quit, and a crashed one on the next start; each file is sent at most twice and then deleted.
- Opted out (or no answer, no valid user, empty URL): no ticket, no request at all; the main log shows `telemetry off` and no `telemetry →` line.
- A failed send never shows anything to the player and doesn''t delay the close by more than 4 s.', '', '2026-10-13T17:33:00Z', 874);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (108, 14, 'Telemetry consent UI and privacy texts', 400, 'Part 4 of masterplan 14 (plan 101): the player''s yes/no to opt-in telemetry and the texts that say what it means. A first-start question (default «No») asks once per install; the Esc menu shows and changes the answer; `PRIVACY.md` and the store text gain a telemetry section, and the on-screen texts are short versions of it. Recording (part 2, plan 106) and sending (part 3, plan 107) already obey the stored answer; this plan only asks, stores through plan 106''s bridge, and explains. Plan 101''s ground rules apply: opt-in, default off, Steam only (decision 111: Steam only), the game plays the same either way.

## Builds on (don''t rebuild)
- Plan 106: Telemetry recording in the game: bridge `telemetry.state()` → `{available, consent: boolean | null}`, `telemetry.setConsent(yes)` (yes → install id; no → `{"consent": false}`, `sessions/` deleted), the page''s consent cache in `lib/telemetry.ts`; optional on the bridge (browser game, dev shim → no-op). Its task 1 (shell telemetry store) must be done first.
- Plan 105: Telemetry API and server (fields sent, decision 118: raw rows deleted on ack or after 30 days, no SteamID/IP stored, salted-hash rate counter) and plan 107 (sent at quit, one later try at the next start, decision 120): the privacy text describes exactly these.
- Plan 103 task 867: Report server: privacy texts (PRIVACY.md, store, dialog): `PRIVACY.md` (all launch languages), ST-5 "Privacy" store text and steps. Its "no other data leaves the game; no analytics" line is rewritten here. Must be done first.
- Plan 68: `LanguageScreen.svelte`, `Game.svelte` `refresh()` / `askLanguage()` / `screen = "language"`; the language pick may `location.reload()`.
- `Overlay.svelte` general column (col 0): rows like the mute toggle (`label` from state, `run` flips it) and the read-only `row readonly info` line.
- Plan 100: UI wording: style guides (`app/client/src/locales/style/<locale>.md`: glossary, address, button style; de without plural address); read before writing strings. Gallery (`trivia-gallery`) step list gets the new screen if it exists.
- Plan 69 catalogs `es`, `es-ES`, `en`, `de` (+ dev pseudo-locale `xx` derives itself); `check:literals`.

## Choices
- **Answer lives per install** (decision 121: Telemetry consent: stored per install, not in the save): plan 106''s `install.json`, not the Steam Cloud save. Why: install id and buffer are per install; a yes in the cloud save would make another machine send unasked; a no must wipe that machine''s id and buffer; no save change (decision 119). Cost: a second Steam account on the same PC inherits the answer; it sees and can change it in the Esc menu. Plan 106 needs no edit.
- **Who is asked**: only when `available && consent === null` (valid Steam user, desktop bridge, no `install.json` yet). That covers both a fresh save and an existing save on an install that never answered: one check, no save flag. No valid user or browser game → never asked, no Esc row.
- **When**: after the language screen on a fresh save; otherwise right after loading, before the start screen. The check runs in `Game.svelte` `refresh()` after `askLanguage()`, so it also works after the language pick''s reload. `onpicked` goes to the consent screen instead of start when it applies.
- **Skipping isn''t an answer**: Esc/menu does nothing on the screen (as the language screen); `back` (Backspace, pad B) = «No». Quitting the app without answering → asked again next start (still `null`, nothing recorded meanwhile).
- **No "more info" link**: the page can''t open URLs (shell request block) and a TV has no browser at hand; the screen''s text is complete on its own, `PRIVACY.md`/store page hold the long version.

## Consent screen (`app/client/src/game/ConsentScreen.svelte`, screen `"consent"` in `Game.svelte`)
- Layout like `LanguageScreen.svelte` (same `Stage`, title, centred column): title, one sentence, 3 short lines (what is sent; what isn''t: no names, no Steam account, no IP; change it any time in the Esc menu), two buttons «No» and «Sí».
- «No» first, `data-focus-default`, focused on mount (`focusControl`); arrows move between the two; Enter/Space/pad A press the focused one; mouse click; `pick` 1 = No, 2 = Sí (order on screen).
- Press → `setConsent(yes)` (await, buttons disabled meanwhile) → `go("start", "whoosh")`. A failed call: log to the dev console, go to start anyway (silent; state stays `null`, asked again next start). Never a toast or error text.
- Scope `name: "consent"`, `root` the section, `menu` swallowed as on the language screen.
- Draft wording (es; final per style guide and task 1''s privacy text): title «¿Nos ayudas a mejorar el juego?»; body «Si aceptas, el juego envía datos anónimos de las partidas al cerrarse: qué preguntas se respondieron, si bien o mal, nivel, grupo de edad y comodines.»; lines «Sin nombres, sin cuenta de Steam, sin dirección IP.» / «Lo puedes cambiar en el menú (Esc).»; buttons «No» / «Sí, enviar datos». The task drafts en, de (infinitive style, no plural address), es-ES only where it differs.

## Esc menu row (`Overlay.svelte`, general column)
- Shown only when `telemetry.state().available` (read once when the overlay opens; the cache in `lib/telemetry.ts`).
- One toggle row in the settings group (next to mute / languages, `sound: true` group): label from state, e.g. «Datos anónimos: sí» / «… no» (`null` shows as «no»); `run` → `setConsent(!on)`, then the label updates; no confirm step (either direction is harmless and reversible; "no" deleting the buffer is intended).
- Below it a read-only info line (`info: true`, not focusable as the choice line): what is sent, one line, e.g. «Al cerrar: respuestas, niveles, comodines; sin nombres ni cuenta.»
- Turning it on mid-run starts recording from that moment (plan 106 handles it); off stops it and deletes what wasn''t sent.

## Strings (catalogs `es.ts`, `es-ES.ts`, `en.ts`, `de.ts`)
- New keys under `consent.*` (screen: `title`, `body`, `noNames`, `change`, `no`, `yes`) and `overlay.telemetryOn`, `overlay.telemetryOff`, `overlay.telemetryInfo`; all through `t()`; `es-ES` keys only where the wording differs (D-91 / decision 27); `xx` needs nothing (derived).
- Wording follows the style guides (plan 100) and task 1''s privacy text; length checked on the TV layout (1920 × 1080, Deck 1280 × 800) and with `?lang=xx`.
- `check:literals` passes.

## Privacy texts
- `PRIVACY.md` (every launch language section from task 867): new sub-section "Anonymous gameplay data (opt-in)" / «Datos de juego anónimos (opcional)» / «Anonyme Spieldaten (freiwillig)»:
  - off by default; asked once; changeable in the Esc menu; off deletes the unsent data and the install id on that computer
  - what is sent, at game close (or the next start if the close failed): a random install id (not linked to the Steam account or the computer), a random session id, game version, pool version, session length, per game: mode, age group or difficulty range, levels set and reached, result, jokers used, question language, whether it was customized; per answered question: question id, right/wrong, level, jokers used on it
  - not sent: names, Steam account id, IP address (the server doesn''t store it), free text, mod/player questions
  - a one-time Steam ticket proves a real copy; checked with Valve, the account id isn''t kept; a daily counter per salted hash for abuse limits, deleted after the day
  - kept until the developer downloads it, at most 30 days (decision 118); afterwards only aggregated statistics per question
  - processors: Cloudflare (host), Valve (ticket check), as the reports section
  - the old "no other data leaves the game; no analytics" line becomes "nothing else is sent; no third-party analytics, no ads"
- ST-5 "Privacy" in `plans/11-steam.md`: the store-page text per language gets the same section (short); the steps for the gamemaster note that the store text must be updated in the same release as the first build with telemetry (part 6''s checklist runs it).
- On-screen texts (screen + Esc info line) are shortened from this section; the task keeps them consistent (no field promised on screen that isn''t in `PRIVACY.md`).
- Exact fields: check against plan 105''s contract and plan 106''s builder as built; if they differ, the texts follow the code and the difference is noted in the task''s done note.

## Tasks to split into
1. **Privacy texts for telemetry** (after task 867; reads plans 105–107 as built or planned) — `PRIVACY.md` sub-section in every language, ST-5 store text, the "no analytics" line rewritten, short on-screen texts drafted per language in the task''s done note for task 2 (es, es-ES where different, en, de; style guides of plan 100 if they exist). `.md` style per AGENTS.md. Per-commit checks.
2. **Consent screen and Esc menu toggle** (after task 1 and plan 106''s shell telemetry store task) — `ConsentScreen.svelte`, the `"consent"` screen and check in `Game.svelte` (`refresh()` and the language screen''s `onpicked`), Esc row + info line in `Overlay.svelte`, catalog keys in es/es-ES/en/de from task 1''s drafts. Check with `trivia-desktop`, `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`, `TRIVIA_TELEMETRY_DIR` and `TRIVIA_SAVE_DIR` in `/tmp`: fresh save → language → consent (focus on «No», Enter → start, `install.json` `{"consent": false}`); delete `install.json`, existing save → consent before start; «Sí» by pad/mouse/keys 2 → id created; Esc row flips both ways and `sessions/` disappears on off; no valid user (switch off, no Steam) → no screen, no row; plan 99''s dev server / browser → no screen, no row; `?lang=xx` and `en`/`de` fit on 1920 × 1080 and 1280 × 800. `npm run check` (incl. `check:literals`), `npm test`, `npm run build`. If `trivia-gallery` exists: add the consent screen and the Esc menu with the row to its step list. Docs: `plans/12-client-engine.md` "Telemetry recording" section (plan 106 task 2) gets one line on the consent screen and row; `directory.md` row for `ConsentScreen.svelte`.
- Not here: recording and the consent setter (plan 106), sending (plan 107), `plans/11-steam.md` principle 3 / ST-5 "no telemetry" lines, plan 18, README how-tos and publishing the store text (part 6, task 877: write plan: Telemetry gamemaster setup, end-to-end check and close-out).

## Done when
- With a valid user and no stored answer, the game asks once (after the language screen on a fresh save, before start otherwise), «No» focused; keyboard, mouse and pad work; the answer is stored per install and changeable in the Esc menu, which says what is sent.
- Without a valid user or outside the desktop shell: no question, no row.
- `PRIVACY.md` and the ST-5 store text describe telemetry in every launch language, matching the code; on-screen texts derive from them; `check:literals` passes.', '', '2026-10-13T17:34:46Z', 875);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (109, 14, 'Played vs. rated difficulty in qgen report', 540, 'Part 5 of masterplan 14 (plan 101): the authoring side of opt-in telemetry. `qgen telemetry --pull` fetches the sessions from the server, folds them into anonymous per-question counts under gitignored `state/played/` and acknowledges them; `qgen report --played` compares played with rated difficulty per age group; outliers become review candidates in `/review?played=outliers`, where the gamemaster fixes the difficulty or sets `needs_work` by hand. Nothing here changes a question on its own and nothing runs `qgen rework`. Plan 101''s ground rules apply (anonymous, our questions only); `authoring/` only, never shipped.

## Builds on (don''t rebuild)
- Plan 105: Telemetry API and server: contract v1, `GET /telemetry?limit=N` (≤ 200, oldest first, read-only until acked, `more`), `POST /telemetry/ack` (idempotent), bearer `PULL_TOKEN`; dev `trivia-report-server dev` + `seed-telemetry [N]`; `report-server/fixtures/telemetry-session.json`. decision 118: Telemetry server storage and pull token.
- Plan 106: what an answer row means (decision 119): `correct` only for answered questions (skip/replaced → no row), `jokers` used on that question, `level` 1–`levels`, age group or custom `difficulty` per game, `locale` + `pool` per game.
- Plan 93 task 860: qgen flag --pull: server reports become needs_work: URL (`--url`, else `reportUrl` in `app/desktop/package.json`, a base URL since plan 107), token (`TRIVIA_REPORT_TOKEN`, else `state/report-token`; missing → error naming both), pulled-ids-before-ack pattern. Reuse its helpers (move them to one function if task 860 inlined them); if task 860 is still open, wait for it.
- Plan 108''s privacy text (decision 121): raw data kept until the developer downloads it, "afterwards only aggregated statistics per question". So the pull stores **no raw session, no install id, no session id after the ack**.
- Scale and windows: `app/data/age-groups.json` (D-38, D-76; `authoring/tools/age_groups.py`); per-locale difficulty (D-81: a translation''s own `difficulty`); decision 101: qgen.py stays one module (new logic goes to a sibling module like `translations.py`, `health.py`; `qgen.py` only wires commands).
- `/review` filters: `authoring/server/main.py` `source_questions` / `locale_questions` (`licence=replace` is the model), `with_extras`; `authoring/ui/src/review/Review.svelte` `reviewUrl()`; difficulty change `POST /api/questions/<id>/difficulty` and human reviews (`set_review`) already exist.

## Local store: `state/played/` (gitignored `state/`)
- Not `state/telemetry/`: that is the dev shell''s own folder (plan 106).
- `state/played/stats.json` (atomic write, `atomic.py`; version 1):
  - `answers`: `{"<id>|<locale>|<group>|<mode>|<YYYY-MM-DD>|<clean 0/1>": [n, right]}`: group = age group id, or `custom` (custom difficulty range); date = the session''s `received_day`; clean = no helping joker on it (`hint`, `snipe`: they show hints / strike answers; `easier`, `category` pick the question, they don''t help answer it).
  - `levels`: `{"<group>|<level>|<mode>|<YYYY-MM-DD>": [n, right]}` (all ids, ours only).
  - `games`: counts per `version|mode|group|result`, `reached` histogram per group, joker uses per joker, `customized`, `mods`, per `received_day`.
  - `sessions`: per `version|day`: count, games-per-session histogram, `length_s` histogram (buckets 0–5–15–30–60–120–240 min, more).
  - `pending_ack`: session ids counted but not yet acked; `unknown`: count of answers whose id isn''t in the pool any more.
- No install id, no session id beyond `pending_ack`, no raw body on disk. Kept indefinitely (aggregated, anonymous).

## `qgen telemetry --pull`
- Loop: ack any `pending_ack` left by a crash first → `GET /telemetry?limit=200` → fold the page into `stats.json` with its ids in `pending_ack` (one write) → `POST /telemetry/ack` → clear `pending_ack` (write) → repeat while `more`. A crash between write and ack never counts a session twice; the server''s tombstones (plan 105) stop a re-delivery after the ack.
- Each session''s answers fold in with locale/mode/group from its game; ids not matching `^q-\d{4,6}$` can''t arrive (server 400s them).
- `--dry-run`: pull one page, print counts, write nothing, ack nothing. `--from <file>`: read a `GET /telemetry`-shaped JSON (`{"sessions": [...]}`) instead of the server, no ack (dev data path, below). `--url` as task 860.
- Summary: sessions, games, answers folded, pages, acked; failures: one line, exit 1, nothing half-written.

## Calibration model (decision recorded with this plan: Played difficulty: per-group empirical curve, inverted per question)
- Per age group `g`, from the clean answers of every question in `g`: right rate `p_g(d)` per rated difficulty `d` of the window (the locale''s own `difficulty`, D-81), made monotone non-increasing (pool-adjacent-violators), linear between buckets; a bucket with < 30 clean answers borrows from its neighbours (merged into the adjacent bucket for the fit). A group with < 300 clean answers in total: "not enough data", no observed difficulties.
- A question''s cell `(id, locale, g)`: `n`, `right` from clean answers received on or after the entry''s last review date (`review.reviewed_on` of the source or translation: a reworked or re-reviewed question starts over). Shrunk rate `r = (right + 4·p_g(rated)) / (n + 4)`.
- Observed difficulty = the `d` with `p_g(d) = r` (inverse interpolation, 1 decimal); a rate above `p_g(lo)` → `≤ lo`, below `p_g(hi)` → `≥ hi`.
- Relative, not absolute: the curve comes from the data, so it finds questions mis-rated against their peers; whether a whole group or level is too hard is the per-level table''s job (information; `age-groups.json` levels change only through their own plan).
- Outlier (review candidate): `n ≥ 12` clean answers in the cell, `|observed − rated| ≥ 2`, and the 90 % Wilson interval of `right/n` excludes `p_g(rated)`. Direction: too easy (observed < rated) / too hard. Cells of one entry in different groups disagreeing in direction → "mixed", listed apart, not a candidate.
- Constants at the top of the module (`MIN_N = 12`, `BUCKET_MIN = 30`, `GROUP_MIN = 300`, `SHRINK = 4`, `DELTA = 2`, `Z = 1.645`, `HELP_JOKERS = {"hint", "snipe"}`); changing them is a code edit, no decision.
- Modes: every mode counts (the right rate per mode is shown, so a skew is visible); `--mode` restricts. `custom` games: session stats and per-level table only, no curve.

## `qgen report --played`
- Options: `--locale es|en|de|all` (default all), `--age-group`, `--mode`, `--since YYYY-MM-DD`, `--all` (every cell with `n ≥ MIN_N`, not only outliers), `--json`.
- Sections:
  - data: received days span, sessions, games, answers (clean / with help), versions, locales, unknown ids.
  - sessions: per version; games per session; session length median and quartiles (from the histogram); games by mode, result, age group/custom; customized and mods shares; joker uses per game by joker.
  - per age group × level: answers, right rate, mean rated difficulty of the answered questions, games ending there (lost / won).
  - per age group: the curve `d → p_g(d)` with its `n`.
  - outliers: too easy, too hard, mixed; per line id, locale, group, `n`, right rate, rated, observed, Δ; sorted by |Δ|.
  - last line: `review candidates: N → http://localhost:8001/review?played=outliers` (per locale: `&locale=en`).
- Without `state/played/stats.json`: one line "no played data: qgen telemetry --pull", exit 0.

## Review candidates: a filter, not reviews
- Picked: `GET /api/questions?played=outliers[&locale=…]` (source or locale questions, like `licence=replace`), sorted by |Δ|; every question item with data in `stats.json` gets `played: [{group, n, right, rated, observed}]` from `with_extras`, shown in the review panel as one «Datos de juego» line (e.g. «Datos de juego: adultos 37 resp., 92 % bien, dificultad 11 → jugada ≤ 7»).
- Why not `needs_work` with a note: most outliers want a new difficulty, not a rewrite; `needs_work` drops the question out of the export and puts it in the next `qgen rework` queue, which plan 101 says must not happen unasked. The gamemaster decides per question: change the difficulty (the outlier goes away with the next report, since observed doesn''t depend on rated), or set `needs_work` by hand.
- `/review`''s mode for it: `?played=outliers` in `reviewUrl()`, a question stays in the list after a difficulty change until reload (as `licence=replace` behaves).

## Dev data path
- Server: `trivia-report-server dev` + `trivia-report-server seed-telemetry 2000` (plan 105) → `qgen telemetry --pull --url http://127.0.0.1:8787` with a dev token.
- Offline: `qgen telemetry --pull --from <file>`; task 2''s check makes a throw-away generator (not kept) that writes sessions from `app/data/pool.json` with right rates from rated difficulty and a few planted mis-rated ids, and checks they come out as outliers in the right direction and nothing else does at that sample size.
- `--from` files and the generator''s output stay in `/tmp` or `state/`, never in git.

## Tasks to split into
1. **`qgen telemetry --pull` and the played store** (after plan 105''s routes and seed tasks and task 860) — `authoring/tools/played.py` (fold sessions into `stats.json` per "Local store", `pending_ack` handling), `qgen.py` subcommand `telemetry` with `--pull`, `--dry-run`, `--from`, `--url`; URL/token helpers shared with `qgen flag --pull`. Check against `trivia-report-server dev` after `seed-telemetry 50`: 50 sessions folded, acked, a second pull folds 0; kill between write and ack (throw-away: raise after the write) → the next run acks without double counts; `--from report-server/fixtures/telemetry-session.json` folds that one session; `stats.json` holds no install or session id after the ack (grep). Docs: `qgen.py` usage docstring, `directory.md` (`state/played/`, `played.py`).
2. **Calibration and `qgen report --played`** (after task 1) — curve, inversion, shrinkage, outliers per "Calibration model" in `played.py`; report sections and options per "`qgen report --played`" in `cmd_report`. Check with the throw-away generator (planted outliers found, direction right, no false ones at that sample), and on seeded dev data. `pm decision consequences` on the calibration decision with the final constants if they changed. Docs: `authoring/QC.md` one bullet (played outliers as review input), `plans/21-age-groups.md` short note (played vs. rated, per-level table).
3. **`/review?played=outliers` and the «Datos de juego» line** (after task 2) — `main.py`: `played=outliers` filter in source and locale questions, `played` in `with_extras` (stats loaded once per request); `Review.svelte` / `reviewUrl()` and the panel line; `authoring/ui` types. Check: with task 2''s generated data, the filter lists the planted ids for es and en, a difficulty change there removes the id from the next `qgen report --played`; `/review` without the filter shows the line on a question with data. `npm run check`, `npm run build`. Docs: `main.py` route docstring, `plans/08-review-tool.md` filter list.
- Not here: README how-tos and AGENTS.md commands line (part 6, task 877: write plan: Telemetry gamemaster setup, end-to-end check and close-out), the server, game and shell sides (parts 1–4).

## Done when
- `qgen telemetry --pull` folds dev-server sessions into `state/played/stats.json` (no raw rows, no ids kept) and acks them.
- `qgen report --played` shows per age group and level the played vs. rated difficulty and lists outliers; `/review?played=outliers` opens them; no question changes without the gamemaster.', '', '2026-10-13T17:37:28Z', 876);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (110, 14, 'Telemetry gamemaster setup, end-to-end check and close-out', 150, 'Part 6 of masterplan 14 (plan 101), last: brings the telemetry of parts 1–5 live and closes the milestone. The gamemaster deploys the server change and updates the store''s privacy text through one checklist decision; a `beta` build under Steam then proves the milestone''s "Done when" (an opted-in session arrives, an opted-out game sends nothing, `qgen report` shows played vs. rated difficulty); a manual release-check row keeps it tested; the docs that still say "no telemetry" are fixed. Workers never log in, deploy or upload; they prepare the checklists and act on the answers.

## Builds on (don''t rebuild)
- Plan 103: Report server (POST /reports): `trivia-report-server deploy` (`wrangler d1 migrations apply --remote` then `wrangler deploy`), secrets, URL in `reportUrl`; its first deploy is task 868: Report server: gamemaster setup and first test report, its docs task 869: Plan 103 close-out: docs, how-tos, decision consequences. This plan waits for both; it adds no secret and no account step.
- Plan 105: Telemetry API and server (migration `0002_telemetry.sql`, vars `TELEMETRY_*`/`RATE_TELEMETRY_*`, routes, `seed-telemetry`), decision 118: Telemetry server storage and pull token.
- Plan 106: Telemetry recording in the game, decision 119: Telemetry recording: derived from the save into a per-install side file (`<userData>/telemetry/`, IPC `telemetry:state|consent|load|record`).
- Plan 107: Telemetry send from the shell, decision 120: Telemetry send: at quit, one later try at the next start; its Steam-side close checks (Steam "Stop" on Linux/Deck and Windows, Deck sleep → resume → quit) are rows here.
- Plan 108: Telemetry consent UI and privacy texts, decision 121: Telemetry consent: stored per install, not in the save; its ST-5 store text is published here.
- Plan 109: Played vs. rated difficulty in qgen report (`qgen telemetry --pull`, `qgen report --played`, `/review?played=outliers`).
- Plan 104: Release checklist: report rows and Steam offline (task 870): `MANUAL` rows, `report` step, `failed` routing; plan 92''s per-candidate decision template.
- Upload: `trivia-steam upload --branch beta` (gamemaster, plan 89).

## Wait
- Every task here runs after plans 105–109 are done and task 868/869 are closed. If one isn''t: leave the task as it is (no duplicate work, no guessing of URLs or field names).
- No launch dependency (plan 101): nothing in plans 95 (Launch) or 92/104 waits for this plan; the release-check row (task 2) only lands once telemetry is in the build.

## Decisions taken here
- **Two checklist decisions (kind once), not one:** deploy + store text first (the server must take sessions before a build sends them), the end-to-end run second (needs a `beta` upload with parts 2–4). Each answer = date + ✓/✗ per row; a ✗ becomes a follow-up task in the plan that owns it (routing below), not a fix in this plan.
- **Release-check row `telemetry` (full only, manual):** like plan 104''s `report` row: a content-only patch ships no client or shell code; the server deploys apart from builds. No automated step: plan 104''s `report` step already checks the base URL''s `/health`, the only shared dependency.
- **Store text in the same release as the first telemetry build** (plan 108): the deploy checklist has the row; the end-to-end checklist confirms it''s live before the build goes from `beta` to the default branch.
- **`failed` routing:** server (404/500 on `/telemetry`, pull/ack, retention) → plan 105; nothing recorded, wrong fields → plan 106; no request, a request when opted out, close delayed > 4 s, a visible error → plan 107; consent screen/Esc row/texts → plan 108; pull or report wrong → plan 109; once a plan is closed: a new task in it anyway (`pm task add <plan>`), or a new plan in milestone 14 if several are hit.

## Tasks to split into
1. **Gamemaster: deploy telemetry and publish the store text** (after plans 105, 108 and task 868, 869) — `pm decision add` (kind once) "Telemetry: deploy the server change and update the store privacy text", body with exact commands, written against the code as built:
   - dev shell; `git pull`; `trivia-report-server deploy` (applies `0002_telemetry.sql` to the remote D1, deploys the vars from `wrangler.toml`); no new secret.
   - checks with the URL from `reportUrl`: `curl <url>/health` → `{"ok": true, "api": 1}`; `curl -X POST <url>/telemetry -H ''Content-Type: application/json'' --data @report-server/fixtures/telemetry-session.json` → `401 invalid_ticket` (route live; `dev-*` tickets aren''t faked remotely); `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat state/report-token)" <url>/telemetry` → `200 {"sessions": [], "more": false}` (or whatever plan 109''s token place is: check it).
   - Steamworks: replace the store page''s privacy text with ST-5''s telemetry version per language (the field and steps from plan 103 task 867 / plan 108 task 1); "publish" only together with the first build that has telemetry (or now, if the text says "optional, when available").
   - answer = date + ✓/✗ per row. `pm task-pending-decision <this task> <decision>` and stop.
   - After the answer: ✗ rows → follow-up tasks per routing; `pm decision consequences` (deployed date, store text live or scheduled); ST-5 note (deploy date). Per-commit checks (AGENTS.md).
2. **Release check: `telemetry` row** (after plans 106–109, task 870) — `authoring/steam/release_check.py` `MANUAL["telemetry"]` (`full`): beta under Steam, online: answer «Sí» (or Esc menu on), play one game, quit → `qgen telemetry --pull` folds ≥ 1 session from that version, `qgen report --played` lists it in its session stats; switch off, play, quit → main log `trivia: telemetry off`, no `telemetry →` line, the next pull folds 0. `plans/11-steam.md` "Release checklist (ST-4)": rows table, Patch variant (why full only), template example, `failed` routing (per "Decisions taken here"). Check: a `--manual` file answering every row incl. `telemetry` parses; `--skip`-only run lists the new row. Per-commit checks.
3. **Gamemaster: end-to-end on `beta` under Steam** (after tasks 1, 2) — `pm decision add` (kind once) "Telemetry: end-to-end check on beta", rows with exact steps (the worker prepares the tag/version notes, the gamemaster uploads with `trivia-steam upload --branch beta` and plays):
   - fresh install (or `install.json` deleted): consent asked once after the language screen, «No» focused; pad, keyboard, mouse.
   - opted out: one game, quit → no session on the server (`qgen telemetry --pull --dry-run` empty), main log `telemetry off`.
   - opted in: two games (one won, one lost; jokers used), quit via «Salir» → `qgen telemetry --pull` folds them; `qgen report --played` shows played vs. rated difficulty rows and session stats (outliers only with enough samples: a seeded dev run already showed that list, plan 109).
   - closes (plan 107): Steam "Stop" on Linux/Deck → session arrives at quit or next start; Windows Steam "Stop" → next start (≈ 30 s after load); Deck sleep → resume → quit → arrives; network off at quit → arrives on the next online start; close never delayed noticeably, no error shown.
   - switch off in the Esc menu → `<userData>/telemetry/` holds no session and no install id.
   - store privacy text live with this build''s release.
   - answer = date + ✓/✗ per row; `pm task-pending-decision` and stop. After the answer: ✗ → follow-up tasks per routing; consequences on the decision; the milestone''s "Done when" noted in task 4''s input.
4. **Close-out docs** (after task 3) —
   - `plans/11-steam.md`: principle 3 → "Offline, no accounts; opt-in telemetry only (milestone 14)": the game works without network, sends nothing without a stored yes; ST-5''s "(no telemetry, so by hand …)" → reports via plan 103''s server, opt-in telemetry see milestone 14 (plans 105–109); ST-5 note with deploy date and retention (30 days, decision 118).
   - `plans/18-steam-integration.md`: shell section: telemetry store and IPC (`telemetry:state|consent|load|record`), send at quit + one try at next start (decision 120), ticket identity `trivia-telemetry` next to `trivia-reports`, per-install consent (decision 121); Steam close behaviour as found in task 3.
   - `directory.md`: `app/desktop/telemetry.mjs`, client `engine/telemetry.ts`/`lib/telemetry.ts`, `ConsentScreen.svelte`, `authoring/tools/played.py`, `state/played/`, `report-server/` telemetry files (only what parts 1–5 didn''t add).
   - `README.md` "How to …": "Pull gameplay data and read the played difficulty" (`qgen telemetry --pull`, `qgen report --played`, `/review?played=outliers`, nothing changes without the gamemaster), "Test telemetry locally" (`trivia-report-server dev`, `seed-telemetry`, `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`, `TRIVIA_REPORT_URL`, `TRIVIA_TELEMETRY_DIR`), deploy line under the report-server how-to.
   - `AGENTS.md` "Commands": `qgen telemetry --pull`, `qgen report --played`, `trivia-report-server seed-telemetry` if missing (`.md` style rules).
   - `plans/project.md`: milestone 14 line `[x]` with date and pm plans 101, 105–110.
   - `pm decision consequences` on decisions 118–121 where the built result differs or adds facts; check milestone 14''s "Done when" and plan 101''s; a gap → follow-up task. Per-commit checks.

## Done when
- Server change deployed by the gamemaster, store privacy text updated with the first telemetry release.
- On `beta` under Steam: an opted-in session arrives and `qgen report --played` shows played vs. rated difficulty; opted out sends nothing; plan 107''s Steam close rows answered.
- `trivia-release-check` has the `telemetry` row; no doc says "no telemetry" or "never phones home" any more.', '', '2026-10-13T17:39:31Z', 877);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (111, 15, 'Readable client build', 560, 'Part 1 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). The client build becomes readable: one unminified, unhashed output file per source module, with source maps, at a path a modder can predict from the source tree, so a mod (part 2''s overlay) can replace any single module, component or stylesheet by its path. Runs first among the parts: part 2 (overlay), part 4 (`window.trivia`, `mods.js`), part 10 (guide) and part 11 (split of the big screens) name these paths.

## Binding
- decision 124: Readable client build: output naming rule for mod overrides (recorded by task 899):
  - `src/<path>.ts` → `<path>.js`; `src/<path>.svelte` → `<path>.svelte.js`; runes modules `x.svelte.ts` → `x.svelte.js`
  - component CSS next to its component: `game/Game.svelte.css`; global CSS keeps its path (`app.css`, `game/theme.css`)
  - third-party modules: `vendor/<package>/<path>` (Svelte runtime: `vendor/svelte/src/internal/client/…`); Vite helpers: `_virtual/…`
  - no hashes anywhere; Svelte''s scoped class names stable per component path (`compilerOptions.cssHash`), not per CSS content
- Plan 102''s ground rules (page only, no second path, offline, path safety, per-commit checks and release-check steps stay green).
- decision 91 / 92: version from `app/desktop/package.json` (`__APP_VERSION__`): the only cache key if one is needed.
- decision 111: Steam only: Deck check under Steam, no "without Steam" rows.
- decision 106: no new lint tooling; no new dependencies (Vite/Rolldown options only).

## What exists (2026-10-13)
- `app/client/vite.config.ts`: Vite 8 (Rolldown) defaults: `dist/assets/{index,App,engine}-<hash>.js` + two CSS, minified, no maps (≈ 340 KB JS+CSS); `define __APP_VERSION__`; plugins `svelte()`, `credits()`; dev proxy to :8000 (plan 99 step 1 replaces it).
- `main.ts`: `import "./app.css"`, awaits `initLang`, then dynamic `import("./App.svelte")` and `mount`.
- `credits.ts`: `generateBundle` emits `credits.json`, `CREDITS.md` by `fileName` (root of `dist/`); Svelte credited from `node_modules/svelte/`.
- Shell: `resolve.mjs` `makeResolver` serves `dist` files over `app://trivia/`, falls back to `index.html`; `sendFile` headers: content-type, accept-ranges, content-length (no cache headers). `electron-builder.yml`: `dist` → `resources/client/` as `extraResources` (plain files, not in the asar: a mod overlay can sit next to it).
- `authoring/steam/credits_check.mjs` reads `resources/client/{credits.json,CREDITS.md,audio,fonts}`; `smoke.mjs` plays the unpacked build.

## Test build (task 899, throw-away config, 2026-10-13)
`preserveModules` + `preserveModulesRoot: "src"`, `[name].js`, `minify: false`, `sourcemap: true`, `cssCodeSplit: true`:
- builds in < 1 s; 166 JS files (96 of them Svelte runtime under `node_modules/svelte/src/…`), 32 CSS files; JS 713 KB, maps 1.3 MB, CSS 91 KB (was 340 KB JS+CSS). Size is no concern next to media.
- `[name]` drops `.svelte` (`game/Game.js`) and collides `App.svelte` with `app.css` → `App2.js`: hence decision 124''s names via `entryFileNames`/`chunkFileNames`/`assetFileNames` functions.
- Component CSS lands as `game/Game.svelte_svelte_type_style_lang.css`, loaded by Vite''s preload helper (`__vite__mapDeps` in `main.js`) when `App.svelte` is imported: works by URL, so a mod can replace it; rename per decision 124.
- `index.html` got ~110 `<script type="module">` tags (every static dependency of `main.js`) plus `_virtual/_vite/modulepreload-polyfill.js`: must become one `<script type="module" src="/main.js">` + `app.css` (full conversions and part 4 edit `index.html`; the browser follows imports itself).
- Svelte''s `dev/*` modules (hmr, inspect, tracing) are shipped as files (unreachable at runtime): accepted, or dropped if an option removes them cheaply.

## Choices
- **CSS: per-component files**, not one `style.css`. Replace: a theme mod overrides `game/Hud.svelte.css` or the global `app.css` / `game/theme.css` (colour variables) by path. Add: a mod''s own CSS needs a load point: part 4''s `mods.js` (an entry may import CSS) or a theme overriding `theme.css`; this plan only guarantees the paths and stable class names. One `style.css` would force a theme to copy all CSS and break on every update.
- **Not overridable by path:** what has no file of its own: inlined constants (`__APP_VERSION__`), `define`d values, the preload helper''s dependency list inside `main.js` (a mod adding a module imports it itself), files emitted by plugins (`credits.json`, `CREDITS.md`: overridable but meaningless), `/data/` (part 5) and `/media` (pool URLs, part 5). Listed in the docs for part 10.
- **Stale files across updates:** no hashes means a cached old `game/Game.svelte.js` would mix with a new `engine/game.js`. Custom `app://` responses via `protocol.handle` are expected not to go through Chromium''s HTTP cache, and the session lives in `userData` across runs: check it (build A, start, rebuild with a changed module, start: new code runs; also V8''s code cache). Either way the resolver sends `cache-control: no-cache` for `dist` files (cheap, explicit); if the check shows reuse anyway, clear the session''s cache at start when `__APP_VERSION__`/build id changed (`ses.clearCache()`), not version-keyed URLs (they''d break override paths).
- **Start time:** ~200 module requests over `app://` instead of 3. Measured on the Steam Deck (Proton Windows build, decision 92) and the gamemaster''s PC: time from launch to the start menu, hashed vs. readable build. Budget: ≤ +0.5 s on the Deck. Over budget → `pm decision add` with options (e.g. Svelte runtime as one `vendor/svelte.js`, ours still per module; or accept).

## Tasks

### 1. Readable build config
- `vite.config.ts` `build`: `minify: false`, `sourcemap: true`, `cssCodeSplit: true`, `modulePreload` off (or a small plugin) so `index.html` loads only `/main.js` and `/app.css`; `rollupOptions.output`: `preserveModules: true`, `preserveModulesRoot: "src"`, naming functions per decision 124 (`vendor/`, `_virtual/`, `.svelte.js`, `.svelte.css`, assets by path without hash: `audio/`, `fonts/` stay where `public/` puts them).
- `svelte.config.js`: `compilerOptions.cssHash` from the component''s path (e.g. `svelte-game-hud`); unique per component.
- Old `dist/assets/` gone (`emptyOutDir` stays default).
- Done when: `npm run build` gives the layout of decision 124 (spot-check `game/Game.svelte.js`, `game/Game.svelte.css`, `lib/data.js`, `vendor/svelte/…`, `.map` next to each JS, no file with a hash, no `node_modules/` in `dist/`); `dist/index.html` has one module script; the game plays a few levels in a browser on the built client (plan 99''s `serve` if done, else the desktop shell) and in `trivia-desktop`; per-commit checks pass.

### 2. Override by path works (throw-away proof)
- Copy `dist/` to a temp dir, replace one component''s JS (e.g. a changed text in `game/Hud.svelte.js`), one component CSS and `game/theme.css` (a colour), point the shell at it (temp `roots()` edit or env, not committed) and start: changes visible, nothing else broken; source maps resolve in devtools (`TRIVIA_DEVTOOLS=1`) to `src/…`.
- Done when: the three overrides show in the game; result noted in the task''s done note (part 2 builds the real overlay).

### 3. No stale files across updates
- `resolve.mjs` `sendFile`: `cache-control: no-cache` for `dist` responses (media/data: unchanged unless the check below says otherwise).
- Check as in "Choices": two builds in a row, the second start runs the new module; with and without the header if needed to know whether Chromium caches `app://` at all; V8 code cache invalidates on changed bytes.
- If stale code shows up despite the header: clear the session cache at start when the build id changed (version + a build stamp written by `vite.config.ts`), stamp stored in `userData`.
- Done when: the two-build check passes; the finding (cached or not) is one line in `plans/18-steam-integration.md`.

### 4. Impact checks: packaging, credits, smoke, dev server
- `trivia-desktop-build --dir`: `resources/client/` has the readable tree (all `vendor/` files and `.map` files survive electron-builder''s filters; if not, fix the `extraResources` filter).
- `credits_check.mjs` and the `credits` plugin pass unchanged (Svelte now ships as readable files under `vendor/svelte/`: still credited once, MIT; the licence text already in `CREDITS.md`).
- `smoke.mjs` (both runs, with and without packs) passes on the new build; `trivia-steam stage --dry-run` (or `trivia-release-check --skip tag …` steps desktop builds, credits, smoke) passes.
- Plan 99''s dev server: `vite` dev is unaffected (no build); its built-client command (`vite preview`) serves the readable tree; if plan 99 isn''t done yet, add a line to its open step-1 task instead (`pm task set`).
- Done when: those commands pass; build size per depot noted (should be + ~2 MB).

### 5. Start time on the Steam Deck (gamemaster, decision once)
- Raise `pm decision add "Readable build: start time on the Steam Deck ok?"` (kind once) with the steps: install the beta build with the readable client (or copy the unpacked Windows build to the Deck as a non-Steam test only if a beta upload isn''t due), time launch → start menu three times, compare with the previous build; options "ok (≤ +0.5 s)" (recommended) | "too slow: bundle the Svelte runtime" | "too slow: other".
- This task makes itself pending on the decision; on "too slow" it adds a follow-up task in this plan with the chosen fix.
- Done when: the decision is answered and any follow-up added.

### 6. Docs
- `plans/01-architecture.md`: build bullet (readable build, decision 124, why: mods). `plans/18-steam-integration.md` "Layout": `resources/client/` is the readable tree; the cache finding of task 3.
- `directory.md`: `app/client/dist/` entry describes the readable layout.
- Notes for part 10''s guide (no guide file yet): the naming rule, what can''t be overridden by path, stable class names, no stability promise (paths may change; renames go into patch notes, plan 102). Put them in this plan''s done note of the task or a short section in `plans/18-steam-integration.md` ("Modding: module paths") that part 10 links.
- Done when: the docs name decision 124 and the per-commit checks pass.

## Order
1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 6; 5 after 1 (needs a build on the Deck; may wait for the next beta upload, it doesn''t block parts 2–4).', '', '2026-10-13T17:49:39Z', 899);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (112, 15, 'Mod format, local mods/ and the app:// overlay', 700, 'Part 2 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). Mods become folders with a `mod.json`; the shell finds them (local `mods/` now, Workshop folders via part 3), validates them, keeps the player''s on/off and load order per machine, and serves enabled mods'' files over `app://trivia/` before our own client and data: first hit wins. A mod''s `index.html` makes it a full conversion. The shell only reads mod files as bytes; mod code runs only in the page.

## Binding
- Plan 102''s ground rules: page only (no mod file is `require`d, `import`ed or `eval`ed by Node; the shell reads bytes and serves them), no second path (our client = the last layer), offline (the request block in `main.mjs` stays), path safety (`contained()` per layer), per-commit checks.
- decision 111: Steam only: mods load only with a valid user (`validUser`: real SteamID or the dev switch `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`); without one they''re listed as off with reason `no_user`. No "without Steam" work.
- decision 91: game version = `app/desktop/package.json` `version` (semver): what `mod.json`''s `game` is compared with.
- decision 121 (precedent): per-install settings live under `<userData>`, not in the Steam Cloud save.
- decision 124: module paths a mod overrides (plan 111: Readable client build); this plan doesn''t depend on plan 111''s output (the overlay serves any `dist`), so it may run first.
- Not here: Workshop sources (part 3), bridge/IPC to the page and writes of `mods.json` (part 3, part 6), `mods.js` and `window.trivia` (part 4), which `/data/` files merge instead of overlay (part 5), mods screen, crash marker, one-time notice (part 6). The browser `trivia` server (`app/server/`) gets no mods (decision 111, plan 99 retires it).

## `mod.json`
```json
{ "id": "big-text", "name": "Big text", "description": "Larger answer buttons", "version": "1.0.0",
  "game": "0.1.0", "languages": ["es", "en"], "entry": "main.js", "kind": ["theme", "code"] }
```
- `id`: required, slug `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,63}$`, unique among discovered mods; folder name doesn''t matter. Part 3 states how a Workshop item''s published file id relates to it.
- `name` (1–80 chars), `description` (≤ 2000 chars, may be empty), `version` (free string ≤ 32 chars, the modder''s), `game` (semver `x.y.z`): required.
- `languages`: optional, array of our locale tags (`es`, `en`, `de`, `es-ES`, … as `lib/i18n.ts` knows them; unknown tags: warning, not error); missing or `[]` = language-independent.
- `entry`: optional, relative path to a `.js`/`.mjs` file inside the mod (must exist, `contained()`); part 4 loads it.
- `kind`: optional hints for the UI, subset of `bundle`, `theme`, `code`, `conversion`; derived when missing: `index.html` at the root → `conversion`, `entry` → `code`, any `.css` → `theme`, bundle files (rule from part 5; until then `data/bundles/`) → `bundle`.
- Unknown top-level keys: warning (typos), not error. File ≤ 64 KB, UTF-8 JSON object.
- `mod.json` is metadata: the overlay never serves it.
- Version check: `game` ≠ running version → loaded, warning `game_version` (part 6 shows it); invalid semver → error.

## Discovery and sources
- Local folder: `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR` if set, else packaged `<userData>/mods/` (created at start if missing, so the guide can point at it), dev repo `mods/` (gitignored). Each direct subfolder with a `mod.json` is a mod; subfolders without one: ignored (one stderr line).
- Hook for part 3: `discover({ local, workshop })`, `workshop` = list of `{ dir, publishedFileId, state }` from Steam; part 3 fills it. Source order local before Workshop: a duplicate id later in that order is invalid (`duplicate_id`, naming the other folder), so a local copy shadows its Workshop original while developing.
- Each discovered mod: `{ id, dir, source: "local" | "workshop", manifest, kind, warnings[], error | null }`. Errors (codes for part 6''s `t()` texts): `no_manifest`, `bad_json`, `bad_field` (+ field), `duplicate_id`, `entry_missing`, `not_a_folder`. Invalid mods are listed, never served.

## Mod settings: `<userData>/mods.json`
- Per machine, not in the save (the save is Steam Cloud''s and per user; mods are files on this machine; decision 121''s precedent). Dev: `state/desktop/mods.json` (next to the dev save).
- `{ "version": 1, "mods": [{ "id": "big-text", "on": true }, …] }`: one ordered list = load order; index 0 is the top. Part 6 may add its notice flag here.
- Top wins: the overlay looks up the top enabled mod first; part 4 runs entry scripts bottom-up so the top mod also patches last.
- Discovered mods missing from the list: appended at the end, `on: true` (putting a folder in `mods/` or subscribing is the opt-in; part 3 confirms for Workshop, part 6 may hold new ones off until the one-time notice). Listed ids not found: kept in the file (a Workshop item still downloading keeps its place), not loaded.
- Read once at start; the overlay is fixed for the run; changes (part 3''s IPC, part 6''s screen) apply on restart. Unreadable file: treated as empty, one stderr line, left as is until the next write. Writes atomic (temp + rename, like `save.mjs`).

## Overlay
- Layers for a run: enabled valid mods in load order, then ours. Mod root mirrors the URL space: `app://trivia/<rel>` → `<mod>/<rel>`, also `/data/<rel>` → `<mod>/data/<rel>`; ours: `dist/<rel>`, `data/<rel>`.
- First hit wins, per request, whole files (no merging; part 5 adds exceptions for files that must merge, e.g. bundle lists, in `resolve.mjs`).
- Not overlaid: `/media?url=…` (pool URLs → our cache; part 5 decides mod media), `/api/*` (404 as today), `mod.json`.
- SPA fallback: unknown path → `/index.html` through the same layers, so a mod''s `index.html` wins and is a full conversion.
- Mods screen reachable with a full conversion on: our client isn''t reachable while the conversion owns `/index.html`, so the way back is starting without mods: this plan adds the launch flag `--no-mods` (no layers this run, stderr line); part 6 adds the crash marker, a held key/pad button, the Steam launch option and the screen. A conversion may link to nothing of ours: accepted.
- Path safety per layer: `contained()` becomes symlink-safe: the file''s `realpath` must stay inside the layer root''s `realpath` (today a symlink inside `dist`/`data` to outside is followed: fix applies to every root); files only; `..`, `%2e%2e`, absolute paths and drive letters (`/C:/…`, `\\`) stay inside or miss.
- Bytes only: `sendFile` streams the file; MIME by extension from the `MIME` table (add `.mjs`, `.map`, `.ttf`, `.otf`, `.woff`, `.txt`, `.ico`, `.wasm`); unknown → `application/octet-stream` (Chromium won''t run it as a module script: good).
- Paths are case-sensitive as URLs; on Windows the file system isn''t: accepted, the guide says "match case".
- Start cost: discovery reads one `mod.json` per folder; each request tries ≤ (mods + 1) `stat`s: fine for tens of mods.

## Tasks

### 1. `app/desktop/mods.mjs`: manifest validator, discovery, settings
- Pure Node (`node:fs/promises`, `node:path`), no Electron import, like `resolve.mjs` and `save.mjs`: `readManifest(dir, gameVersion)`, `discover({ local, workshop }, gameVersion)`, `readSettings(file)`, `writeSettings(file, settings)`, `arrange(mods, settings, { validUser })` → `{ list, layers }` (list: every mod with `on`, position, state `loaded | off | invalid | no_user | missing`; layers: dirs of loaded mods in load order).
- Field rules and error/warning codes as above; semver compare without a dependency (three integers).
- JSON Schema text of `mod.json` (draft 2020-12) as a section "Modding: `mod.json`" in `plans/18-steam-integration.md` for part 10''s guide; the validator is the source of truth, the section says so.
- Record the choices: `pm decision record "Mod settings: per machine in <userData>/mods.json, one ordered list, top wins" --answer … --body …`.
- Done when: a throw-away script over a temp folder (valid mod, bad JSON, missing field, duplicate id, missing entry, version mismatch, folder without manifest) prints the expected list; `npm run check` etc. pass.

### 2. `resolve.mjs`: layers, symlink-safe `contained()`, MIME, test
- `makeResolver({ dist, data, media, layers = [] })`: `dist` lookups and the `index.html` fallback go through `[...layers, dist]`; `/data/` through `[...layers.map((d) => d + "/data"), data]`; `mod.json` at a layer root skipped; `/media` unchanged.
- `contained()`: `realpath` check against the root''s `realpath` (computed once per root); MIME additions.
- One lasting test, `app/desktop/resolve.test.mjs` (`node --test`, temp dirs, no Electron): `..` and encoded `..`, absolute and drive-letter paths, a symlink in a mod to a file outside it and to a folder outside it, a directory request, layer order (top mod wins, ours last), mod `index.html` as fallback, `mod.json` not served, `/data/` layering. Root `package.json` `test` runs it too (`… && node --test app/desktop/`), so `npm test` covers it (D-73).
- Done when: the test passes; `trivia-desktop` without mods plays as before; per-commit checks pass.

### 3. Wire it into `main.mjs`
- `modsDir()` (`TRIVIA_MODS_DIR` → packaged `<userData>/mods/`, created → dev `REPO/mods/`), `modsSettingsFile()` (packaged `<userData>/mods.json`, dev `state/desktop/mods.json`).
- `parseArgs`: `--no-mods` (extend `OWN` and `USAGE`).
- In `start()` after `checkUser()`: discover (workshop `[]` until part 3), read settings, append new mods and write back, `arrange`, pass `layers` to `makeResolver`; with `--no-mods` or no valid user: no layers. One stderr line per mod (`trivia: mod <id> <state> [warnings]`) and a summary (`trivia: mods: 2 loaded, 1 invalid`).
- Keep the result (`list`) in a module variable for part 3/6''s bridge; no IPC here.
- `.gitignore`: `mods/` (repo root, with a short comment).
- Done when: `trivia-desktop` with a repo `mods/` holding a CSS override mod starts with the changed colour; `--no-mods` starts plain; without the dev switch and without Steam the mod is `no_user`; per-commit checks pass.

### 4. End-to-end check in the shell (throw-away mods)
- Temp `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR` with: a theme mod (`game/theme.css` colour), a JS override of one component (path per decision 124 if plan 111 is done, else the current hashed name), two mods overriding the same file (order in `mods.json` decides; swap and restart), a full conversion (`index.html` "Hello"), an invalid mod, a mod with a symlink to `~/.ssh/…`-style outside file (served 404/fallback, never the file).
- Done when: each behaves as above in `trivia-desktop` (dev switch on), `--no-mods` brings back our game with the conversion installed; one line per case in the done note; temp mods deleted.

### 5. Docs
- `directory.md`: `app/desktop/` row: `mods.mjs`, `resolve.test.mjs`, layers in `resolve.mjs`; new row `mods/` (local mods for development, gitignored; `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`); `state/desktop/mods.json`.
- `plans/18-steam-integration.md`: section "Modding" (sources, `mods.json`, layers and top-wins, what isn''t overlaid, `--no-mods`, symlink rule; the schema from task 1); `plans/01-architecture.md` one bullet (overlay).
- AGENTS.md "Commands": `trivia-desktop` line gains `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`, `--no-mods`.
- Done when: docs name this plan''s paths and the recorded decision; per-commit checks pass.

## Order
1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5. Part 3 (Workshop) fills `discover`''s `workshop` and adds the bridge; part 5 adds `/data/` merge exceptions; part 6 builds on `list`, `--no-mods` and `mods.json`.', '', '2026-10-13T17:52:37Z', 900);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (113, 8, 'Image peek on image questions', 400, 'On a question with an image, holding one button (pad and key) hides every panel and shows only the picture, whole; release brings the UI back unchanged. On the Deck (1280×800) the panels can cover parts of the picture, and decision 125: Steam Deck: best effort, review without a Deck at hand makes the peek part of the S3 Deck bar. Part 10 of masterplan 8; owners `plans/14-input.md` (the peek action, pad and key), `plans/04-ui-tv-display.md` (Question screen), `plans/18-steam-integration.md` (Deck). Client only: no engine, save or shell change.

## Depends on
- Plan 44/45''s action layer (done): `lib/input.svelte.ts` (scopes, `toIntent`, `KEY_LABELS`, `dispatch`), `lib/gamepad.ts` (`BUTTONS`, `step`, edges only), `lib/gamepad.svelte.ts` (loop), `Glyph.svelte`, `Prompt.svelte`.
- Plan 99 "Retire the browser game and the Python game server", task 762: Node dev server for browser checks: the check runs on it if it''s done, else on `trivia-desktop` (`TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`).

## Decisions
- decision 126: Image peek: which pad button and key? → pad R3 (right stick click, standard button 11; glyph RS / R3); key per catalog `screen.question.peekKey`, `V` in es, es-ES, en, de.
- decision 9: Gamepad on the Question screen (Y/X/B/A pick, RT submit, LT unlock, LB/RB tray, Start menu; View = flag): unchanged.
- decision 53: Joker and action keys per UI language (LUI-4): the key lives in the catalog; `check-literals.mjs` collision rule covers it.
- D-51: picture at native size, down to fit, never up: the peek keeps it (the picture is already whole in the backdrop; the panels are what hides it).

## Design
- **Hold intent** (`lib/input.svelte.ts`): new `Intent` `{ type: "peek"; down: boolean }`. Today every action is a press edge; peek is the only hold.
  - Keys: `toIntent` maps the scope key (`question` scope `keys`: `peekKey` → peek) to `down: true` on `keydown` (repeats still ignored); a new `keyup` listener dispatches `down: false` for the key that started it. Not in text fields (none on Question; the flag dialog is modal anyway).
  - Pad: `step` gains release edges, only for buttons whose mapped intent is a hold (`peek`): press → `down: true`, release → `down: false`. Other buttons stay press-only. `gamepad.ts` `BUTTONS` gets nothing; the Question scope''s `pad` map adds `11: peek`.
  - Safety release: window `blur`, `visibilitychange` to hidden, the pad loop''s "not active" branch, and a pad disconnect dispatch `down: false` if a peek is held, so the UI never sticks hidden.
  - `busy` doesn''t drop the release (`down: false` always goes through, like `menu`/`flag`); a press while busy is dropped.
  - `KEY_LABELS.peek` = `t("screen.question.peekKey")`; `keyLabel`/`padButton` match peek regardless of `down` (prompts show the binding).
- **Question screen** (`Question.svelte`):
  - Peek allowed only when `media.type === "image"`, the picture is shown sharp (not `decorative`, not `mediaFailed`), and no modal mode is open (Paso, Francotirador, Cambiazo, flag dialog, overlay: their modal scopes block it anyway). Not on video/audio questions or other screens.
  - `peeking` state: class on `.question-screen`; every panel (`.top`/Hud, header card, answers, joker tray, prompts, «Respuesta final», topic, dial, credit line) fades out (opacity 0, `visibility: hidden` after the fade, ~150 ms) and the backdrop picture stays; dark/dim filters off while peeking. No layout change, so release restores exactly (focus, locked answer, armed joker, aim as before; DOM focus isn''t touched).
  - While peeking, other actions pass through as usual; a pick or submit during a peek is allowed (no special case), but `menu`/`flag` opening a modal ends the peek (`down: false` on scope change).
  - Timed modes: no pause; the clock keeps running while peeking (no pause exploit). If the timed mode (MD-8) has no clock yet at task time, nothing to do; note it in the task.
  - Prompt: `<Prompt action={{ type: "peek", down: true }} />` with a short label (`screen.question.peek`, es «Ver imagen», en «View image», de «Bild ansehen») on image questions only, placed where it doesn''t cover the picture (e.g. next to the credit line or in the HUD row); keyboard `V` chip, pad RS/R3 glyph.
  - Mouse/touch: press-and-hold on the picture area outside the panels (pointerdown on the backdrop → peek, pointerup/pointercancel/leave → release), only if it stays a few lines; else skip and say so in the task note.
- **Glyphs** (`Glyph.svelte`): button 11 in both sets (xbox `RS`, playstation `R3`), same style and label size (13.5 viewBox units, plan 46''s 9 px rule).
- **Catalogs** (`app/client/src/locales/*.ts`): `screen.question.peekKey` (`V`) and `screen.question.peek` in es, en, de (es-ES inherits), xx if it mirrors keys; `check-literals.mjs` `SCREENS.question` gets `screen.question.peekKey`.
- No new dependency.

## Tasks
1. **Image peek: hold action, pad R3, key V, Question screen**
   - Everything in "Design": hold intent with key `keyup` and pad release edges (incl. safety release), Question scope binding (keys + `pad` map 11), `peeking` CSS, prompt, glyph 11, catalog strings, collision rule.
   - Check: `npm run check` (incl. `check:literals`), `npm test`, `npm run build`; throw-away script feeding fake pads to `step` (R3 press → peek down, release → peek up, other buttons unchanged, no repeat).
2. **Peek screen check at 1280×800, 1280×720, 1920×1080; docs**
   - Throw-away script: lists the image questions of `app/data/pool.json` (non-decorative images) and, per size, the ones whose fitted picture rect (native size, D-51) intersects the panels'' rects (Playwright walk with the go-to dialog, `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1` in `trivia-desktop`, or `?q=` on the dev server, whatever exists). For every listed one at the three sizes: peek shows the whole picture, nothing of the UI left; release brings it back as before. Keyboard (`V`) and pad (faked standard pad or forced `device: "gamepad"`, button 11). Also: the prompt doesn''t cover the picture; peek does nothing on video/audio/decorative questions and in Paso/Francotirador/Cambiazo/flag dialog/overlay; holding through a `blur` releases.
   - Runs on the Node dev server (task 762) if done, else `trivia-desktop`.
   - Docs: `14-input.md` (peek in section 2''s action list and the Question pad map: R3 / `V` per catalog, hold), `04-ui-tv-display.md` Question screen (peek behaviour) and "Input" (hold intents: the one exception to press-only), `18-steam-integration.md` "Steam Deck Verified": replace "Test on a real Deck before applying." with decision 125''s rule (fit at 1280×800 + peek + Gamepad template, review without a real-Deck test; the real-Deck test is plan 90 once a Deck is at hand); `plans/project.md` in sync; `directory.md` only if a file is added.
   - Done when: the milestone''s peek line holds ("At 1280×800 every image question''s picture can be seen whole via the peek"); the owning plan files carry the notes.

## Done when
- On every image question, holding R3 or `V` (catalog key) hides every panel and shows the whole picture; release restores the UI unchanged; at 1280×800, 1280×720 and 1920×1080; not on video/audio questions or other screens; the clock (timed mode) doesn''t stop.
- `plans/14-input.md`, `04-ui-tv-display.md`, `18-steam-integration.md` updated (Deck: decision 125''s best-effort rule).

## Out of scope
- The real-Deck test (plan 90, milestone 10); Steam Input templates and action sets (S5); zooming or panning the picture beyond D-51''s fit.', '', '2026-10-13T17:56:05Z', 919);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (114, 15, 'Workshop source: subscribed items in the shell', 690, 'Part 3 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). The shell adds the player''s subscribed Workshop items as mod sources next to local `mods/` (plan 112), and the page gets its first mods bridge: the mod list (source, state, errors) and writes of on/off and load order to `mods.json` over IPC. The overlay stays fixed per run: new subscriptions show up in the list while running and load on the next start. The gamemaster turns Workshop on in Steamworks; workers never log in.

## Binding
- Plan 102''s ground rules: page only (Workshop folders are read as bytes, never `require`d/`import`ed/`eval`ed), offline (the `webRequest` block stays; Steam''s client downloads items, not us), path safety (`contained()` per layer, plan 112), per-commit checks.
- decision 111: Steam only: Workshop sources only with a real Steam user (`hasSteamId()`); no "without Steam" rows. The dev switch `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1` gets a fake Workshop folder for checks (below), nothing else.
- decision 127: Workshop mods: identity and duplicates: key = `mod.json` `id`; published file id beside it as a string; local before Workshop; two Workshop items with the same id: lower published file id wins, the other `duplicate_id`.
- Plan 112 (part 2): `discover({ local, workshop })`, `arrange()`, `readSettings`/`writeSettings`, `<userData>/mods.json` (dev `state/desktop/mods.json`), list states, read-once overlay, `--no-mods`. This plan fills `workshop` and adds the bridge; it doesn''t change the format or the overlay.
- Plan 85 (Steamworks binding): `steamworks-ffi-node` 0.11.3, `steam` in `main.mjs`, `runCallbacks` timer, IPC `handle()` with the sender-origin check (`event.senderFrame.url` starts with `app://trivia/`).
- Not here: the bridge rename `window.trivia` → e.g. `window.triviaShell` (part 4; this plan adds `mods` to whatever the bridge is called then, `lib/desktop.ts` the only reader), mods screen, one-time notice, restart button, crash marker (part 6), publishing (part 8).

## Binding facts (`SteamWorkshopManager`, checked 2026-10-13)
- `getSubscribedItems(): bigint[]`, `getItemState(id): number` (bits `EItemState`: Subscribed 1, Installed 4, NeedsUpdate 8, Downloading 16, DownloadPending 32, DisabledLocally 64), `getItemInstallInfo(id): { folder, sizeOnDisk, timestamp } | null`, `getItemDownloadInfo(id)`, `downloadItem(id, highPriority)`: all synchronous.
- No push callback for `ItemInstalled_t` / `DownloadItemResult_t` in the binding: refresh is a re-query, not an event (below). Don''t add FFI of our own for it.
- Published file ids are `bigint`: convert to decimal strings at the edge (IPC and JSON can''t carry bigint).

## Workshop source at start
- In `start()` after `startSteam()`/`checkUser()`, before plan 112''s `discover`: with `steam` and `hasSteamId()`, `workshopItems(ugc)` →
  - per subscribed id: state bits; `Installed` and a folder from `getItemInstallInfo` → `{ dir, publishedFileId, state: "installed" }` (+ warning `needs_update` when `NeedsUpdate`: the installed files load, Steam updates them later);
  - `Downloading` / `DownloadPending` → `{ dir: null, publishedFileId, state: "downloading" }`;
  - subscribed, neither installed nor downloading → `downloadItem(id, false)` once, then `downloading`;
  - `DisabledLocally` → `{ dir: null, state: "disabled" }`.
- `discover` lists entries without `dir` as such (state `downloading` / `disabled`, not loaded this run, no error); entries with `dir` need `mod.json` at the item''s root (else `no_manifest`, as local).
- `mod.json` `id` is the key (decision 127); the runtime record carries `publishedFileId`; a `mod.json` that names a different published file id (if part 8 writes one) → warning `workshop_id_mismatch`, still loads.
- Pure module `app/desktop/workshop.mjs` (no Electron, no binding import): `workshopItems(ugc)` takes an object with the four methods, so the real `steam.workshop` and the dev fake share it.
- Dev fake: env `TRIVIA_DEV_WORKSHOP_DIR` (honoured only with `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1`, one stderr line): each subfolder named by a fake numeric id is an installed item; a subfolder ending in `.downloading` is a downloading one. Lets agents check Workshop flows without logging in (decision 111''s dev switch precedent).
- Steam updating an item''s files while the game runs: accepted (Steam usually holds UGC updates for running games); the overlay reads files per request, so at worst a mixed version until restart. The guide says "restart after updates".
- Stderr: one line per Workshop item like plan 112''s (`trivia: mod <id> workshop <publishedFileId> <state>`).

## Newly subscribed items: default
- Confirmed: appended at the end of the load order, `on: true` (plan 112''s rule; subscribing is the opt-in). Part 6 may hold new mods off until its one-time notice; it does that in `arrange`, not here.
- Unsubscribed items: their row stays in `mods.json` (plan 112: missing ids kept), listed `missing` only if part 6 wants it; the list from `mods:list` omits missing ids by default.

## Refresh while running
- No event (binding facts): `mods:list` re-queries Workshop and re-runs discovery + `arrange` on every call (cheap: one `mod.json` read per folder), so part 6''s screen sees new subscriptions and finished downloads when it opens or the player presses refresh.
- The overlay layers stay this run''s (plan 112); each list entry carries `active` (in this run''s layers) beside `on` (setting), so the page can show «restart to apply» where they differ.
- No timer polling in main.

## Bridge additions (page)
- IPC channels via the existing `handle()` (origin check), in `main.mjs`:
