Plans 111 (task 1), 112 (tasks 1–3) first; then 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6; task 1 may run any time (no dependency); task 5 after plan 116 if it runs (it renames the screens). Part 6 (mods screen) builds on `modErrors()`, `loadedMods()` and `ready`; part 10''s examples (CSS theme, rules tweak) use `style.css` and `actions.before`.', '', '2026-10-13T18:14:43Z', 904);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (118, 15, 'Mods screen and safe start', 400, 'Part 6 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). Players see their mods in one screen, reachable from the start screen and the Esc menu: what each mod is, where it comes from, whether it loaded and why not; they turn mods on and off, change the load order and restart the game to apply. Before the first mod runs, a one-time notice says that mods run code with the game''s access. A start that crashes with mods on is followed by a start without mods that opens this screen with the reason; `--no-mods` (plan 112) is the manual way.

## Binding
- Plan 102''s ground rules: page only (the screen is page code; the shell only reads/writes `mods.json` and relaunches), strings via `t()` in es, en, de (+ es-ES where it differs), `check:literals`, per-commit checks.
- decision 111: Steam only: no "without Steam" rows; without a valid user the list shows `no_user` states (plan 112) and toggles still write (plan 114); the dev switch `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1` for checks.
- decision 127 (Workshop identity, duplicates), decision 130 (bridge = `window.triviaShell`), decision 131 (`/mods.js`, `ready` event after mount).
- Plan 112: `mods.mjs` (`discover`, `arrange`, list states `loaded | off | invalid | no_user | missing`, error/warning codes), `<userData>/mods.json` (dev `state/desktop/mods.json`; "part 6 may add its notice flag here"; new mods appended `on: true`, "part 6 may hold new ones off until the one-time notice"), overlay fixed per run, `--no-mods`.
- Plan 114: `mods:list` → `{ mods: ModEntry[], noMods, validUser }` (re-queries Workshop and discovery on each call), `mods:set`, `ModEntry` (`on` vs `active`), states `downloading`, `disabled`, warnings `needs_update`, `workshop_id_mismatch`, `bridge.mods` in `lib/desktop.ts` (null outside the shell).
- Plan 117: `modErrors()` (`load_failed`, `setup_failed`, `timeout`, `hook_failed`), `loadedMods()`, `ready` event after mount.
- IN-1..IN-5 (`plans/04-ui-tv-display.md` "Input"): every action a clickable control; screens listen to actions via `pushScope`, `data-focus-default`, `root`; key hints only via `<Prompt>`.
- Plan 100: style guides `locales/style/<locale>.md` (task 799) read before writing strings; screen gallery (task 802) gets the new screen.
- Not here: achievements line with mods on (part 7, decision 123), publishing (part 8), guide text (part 10).

## Choices (record as decisions, task 1)
- Notice flag in `mods.json` (`"notice": true` + date), per install like the settings (decision 121''s precedent): every Steam user on the machine shares mods, so one notice per machine.
- Until the notice is accepted, newly discovered mods are appended `on: false` (in `arrange`''s append step) and nothing loads: no mod code ever runs before the player saw the notice. After accepting, plan 112''s rule applies (new = on, at the end). Existing rows aren''t touched by accepting.
- Manual safe start: `--no-mods` only (plan 112), entered in Steam''s per-game launch options (Properties → Launch options) and documented in the guide. No held key or pad button: the page starts after the overlay is fixed, so a key check would cost a relaunch, and a pad isn''t readable before the page; no second Steamworks launch option either (Steam would ask on every start).
- Crash marker: `<userData>/mods-start.json` (dev `state/desktop/`), written only when the run has layers, cleared on page ready or after 20 s alive; found at start → this run has no layers.

## Shell (`main.mjs`, `mods.mjs`, `preload.cjs`)
- Marker: before `win.loadURL` with layers ≠ []: write `{ at, mods: [ids of active mods] }` (atomic, like `writeSettings`). Cleared by:
  - `mods:ready` from the page (our client after plan 117''s `ready`; a full conversion may call `window.triviaShell.mods.ready()`, the guide says so);
  - 20 s after `did-finish-load` without `render-process-gone` or `unresponsive` (covers conversions that never call ready).
  - Not cleared on quit: a player who closes a broken start before that gets a safe next start (intended).
- `render-process-gone` / page load failure while the marker is set: `app.relaunch()` (args as below) + `app.exit(0)`; the next start finds the marker → safe. No loop: a safe start has no layers, so writes no marker.
- Start: marker found → delete it, no layers this run, `safeStart = { reason: "crashed", mods: marker.mods }`; `--no-mods` → `safeStart = { reason: "flag" }`. Stderr line each. `mods:list` returns `safeStart` (null on a normal start) beside plan 114''s fields.
- Notice: `mods:notice` → sets `notice` in `mods.json`, returns the list; `arrange` holds new mods off while it''s missing (above).
- Restart: `mods:restart` → wait for queued save writes (`writing`), `app.relaunch({ args })` with our own flags `--no-mods` and `--import-save` dropped (Electron/Steam args kept), `app.exit(0)`. Under Steam the relaunched process keeps `SteamAppId` from the environment; the check task confirms the Steam overlay and presence still work after a relaunch on Linux and Windows (else a follow-up task).
- `preload.cjs`: `mods: { list, set, notice, restart, ready }`; `lib/desktop.ts`: `ModsBridge` type grows (`safeStart`, `notice`, `restart`, `ready`).
- `main.ts` (or `lib/mods.ts`): on plan 117''s `ready` event, `desktop?.mods?.ready()`.

## Screen: `ModsScreen.svelte`
- Path: `game/screens/Mods.svelte` if plan 116 has run, else `game/ModsScreen.svelte`; full-screen layer like `Credits.svelte` (opened over the start screen or from the Esc menu, own input scope, `data-focus-default`).
- Entry points, only when `desktop?.mods` (shell):
  - start screen: a `secondary` button «Mods» under play/resume (count of loaded mods in it when > 0);
  - Esc menu general column: row «Mods» after Credits (`Overlay.svelte`);
  - safe start after a crash: opens by itself before the start screen.
- Rows (one per `ModEntry`, in load order; invalid and pending ones at the end, without move controls):
  - name, version, source (`Local` / `Workshop` + published file id), kind badges (bundle, theme, code, conversion), languages (endonyms; none = «all languages»);
  - state line: loaded / off / «on after restart» / «off after restart» (`on ≠ active`) / invalid + reason / downloading / disabled in Steam / needs a Steam user;
  - warnings: `game_version` («made for version x.y.z»), `needs_update`, `workshop_id_mismatch`, unknown language or key (plan 112''s exact codes), page failures from `modErrors()` (load failed, setup failed, timed out, hook failed);
  - detail of the focused row: description, folder name (local) for modders.
- Controls (IN-1: each a visible button with `<Prompt>`): on/off per row (confirm on the row), move up / move down on the focused row (buttons ▲▼; keyboard and pad via existing directional/shoulder intents, the task picks ones `input.svelte.ts` has without new keys if possible), «Refresh» (re-runs `mods:list`: new subscriptions, finished downloads), «All off», «Restart to apply» (enabled when any `on ≠ active` or the order differs from this run''s), «Back» (cancel: Esc / Backspace / B).
- Every change calls `mods:set` at once and shows the returned list; no save button.
- Safe start line at the top: crashed → «The last start with mods didn''t finish. Started without mods. Active then: A, B.» + «All off» focused; flag → «Started without mods (--no-mods).».
- Without a valid user: a line that mods need Steam (decision 111), rows still toggle.
- Empty list: a line where to put mods (Workshop, local folder) and that the guide explains it (no link: offline page; the guide is in the store page and install folder per part 10).
- Start screen hint: when the notice isn''t accepted and the list has mods held off, one line under the buttons «N mods found, off until you allow them» with the Mods button focused next.

## One-time notice
- Shown the first time the player turns a mod on (or presses «Restart to apply» with any on) while `notice` is missing: dialog over the screen: mods are made by other players and run code with the game''s access (save, Steam achievements and features); only enable mods you trust; a crashed start turns them off for one start; buttons «Allow mods» / «Cancel». Allow → `mods:notice`, then the toggle applies. Cancel → nothing changes.
- Dialog follows the confirm-dialog pattern (own scope, focus default on Cancel, Esc/B cancels).

## Strings
- Keys `mods.*` in `es`, `en`, `de`, `es-ES` only where it differs (plan 69, D-59: the family''s `es` game gains strings, changes none); terms per plan 100''s style guides (task 799: «Mods», «Steam Workshop» / «Steam-Workshop» as Steam writes it per language; de without plural address).
- Error, warning and state codes map to `mods.error.*`, `mods.warning.*`, `mods.state.*`; an unknown code shows the code itself (forward-compatible), not a blank.

## Tasks

### 1. Shell: notice, marker, safe start, restart, ready (after plans 112 tasks 1–3, 114 task 2)
- `mods.mjs`: `notice` in settings, hold-off in `arrange`, marker read/write/clear helpers (pure Node); `main.mjs`: marker around `loadURL`, `render-process-gone` relaunch, `safeStart`, IPC `mods:notice`, `mods:restart`, `mods:ready`, `safeStart` in `mods:list`; `preload.cjs`, `lib/desktop.ts`; page sends `ready` (plan 117 task 3''s event; if plan 117 isn''t done yet, after `App` mounts in `main.ts`).
- `pm decision record` the four choices above (notice per install + hold-off, `--no-mods` only, marker file and 20 s, restart drops own flags).
- Done when: throw-away checks under `trivia-desktop` (dev switch, temp `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`): a fresh `mods.json` holds a new mod off until `mods.notice()` from devtools, then new mods come on; a mod whose `style.css` is fine clears the marker (file gone after start); a mod whose entry runs `process.crash`-style code (`while(true){}` in `setup` blocking the page, or a renderer crash via `window.location = "chrome://crash"` if not blocked; the task picks one that works) → next start is safe, `mods.list().safeStart.reason === "crashed"`, the start after it loads mods again; `mods.restart()` relaunches with the new order active and without `--no-mods`; per-commit checks pass.

### 2. Mods screen, entry points, notice dialog, strings (after task 1; after plan 117 task 3 for `modErrors()`)
- Screen as above, start button + hint, Esc menu row, safe-start auto-open, notice dialog, `mods.*` keys in four catalogs (read the style guides first if task 799 is done).
- Done when: under `trivia-desktop` with a temp mods dir (one theme, one code mod, one invalid, one with a `game` mismatch, one `TRIVIA_DEV_WORKSHOP_DIR` item and one `.downloading`) every row shows its state and warning; on/off, up/down, refresh, all off, restart work with keyboard only, pad only (fake pad as in task 352, or a real one) and mouse only; Back returns to where it came from; the dev server (`npm run dev -w app/client`) shows no Mods entry; `check:literals` and per-commit checks pass.

### 3. TV-check and Deck size
- Screen at 1920×1080 and 1280×800 (Deck), es, en, de; long names and 20 mods scroll with the focus ring visible; add the screen and the notice dialog to plan 100''s gallery step list (task 802 if open: `pm task set 802` adds them; if done: add the steps in `authoring/tools/gallery.py`, bridge stub with a sample `mods` list).
- Restart under Steam (Spacewar 480 is enough for this): overlay (Shift+Tab) and rich presence work after «Restart to apply» on Linux; Windows if a Windows machine is at hand, else a manual row for plan 104''s release checklist (`pm task add 104 …`).
- Done when: shots for es, en, de look right (no overflow, no plural address in de); restart under Steam checked or the follow-up exists.

### 4. Docs
- `plans/04-ui-tv-display.md`: Mods screen, start button, Esc menu row; `plans/18-steam-integration.md` "Modding": notice flag, hold-off, crash marker and 20 s, `safeStart`, IPC `mods:notice`/`mods:restart`/`mods:ready`, restart args, "play without mods" via Steam launch options; `directory.md`: the screen file, `mods-start.json`; AGENTS.md "Commands" `trivia-desktop` line: `--no-mods` safe start (if plan 112 hasn''t added it).
- Done when: docs name the recorded decisions and paths; per-commit checks pass.

## Order
Plans 112 (tasks 1–3), 114 (task 2), 117 (task 3) first; then 1 → 2 → 3 → 4. Part 7 adds its achievements line to this screen; part 10''s guide describes the screen, the notice, safe start and `--no-mods`.', '', '2026-10-13T18:16:58Z', 905);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (119, 15, 'Publishing: upload a mod folder as a Workshop item', 670, 'Part 8 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). Modders publish a mod folder as a Workshop item with the game itself: `trivia --publish-mod <folder>` runs headless in the main process, validates `mod.json` with part 2''s validator, creates the item on first publish, uploads a clean copy of the folder with title, description, preview and tags, and prints progress. The command reads the folder as bytes and never runs it. `trivia-steam` stays for our own builds.

## Binding
- Plan 102''s ground rules: page only. Publishing is our code in main; no mod file is `require`d, `import`ed or `eval`ed, the folder is only read and copied. Offline: Steam''s client uploads, we make no network calls of our own.
- decision 111: Steam only: publishing needs the Steam client and a real Steam user (`hasSteamId()`). The dev switch `TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER=1` doesn''t count here (no fake upload). `--dry-run` needs no Steam: it only validates and stages.
- decision 127: Workshop mods: identity and duplicates: `mod.json` `id` is the key; the published file id travels beside it as a string.
- Plan 112 (part 2): `readManifest(dir, gameVersion)` and its error/warning codes, kind derivation, `contained()` symlink rule, semver compare. Plan 114 (part 3): Workshop tags and setup (gamemaster decision), warning `workshop_id_mismatch`.
- Plan 85: `startSteam()`, `hasSteamId()`, `STEAM_APP_ID` / `TRIVIA_STEAM_APPID` / `SteamAppId`, `steamworks-ffi-node` 0.11.3.
- Not here: in-game publish button (backlog row, task 4), `--check-mod` for players (part 9 decides; it may reuse `--dry-run`), the modding guide itself (part 10 copies this plan''s docs section).

## Binding facts (`SteamWorkshopManager`, checked 2026-10-13)
- `createItem(appId, EWorkshopFileType.Community) → Promise<bigint | null>`, `startItemUpdate(appId, id) → handle`, `setItemTitle/Description/Visibility/Tags/Content/Preview(handle, …) → boolean`, `submitItemUpdate(handle, changeNote) → Promise<boolean>`, `getItemUpdateProgress(handle) → { status, bytesProcessed, bytesTotal, percentComplete }` (`EItemUpdateStatus` 0 Invalid … 5 CommittingChanges). Visibility enum: Public 0, FriendsOnly 1, Private 2, Unlisted 3.
- No `SetItemUpdateLanguage`, no metadata or key-value tags: languages go into tags.
- The legal agreement flag (`userNeedsToAcceptWorkshopLegalAgreement`) isn''t returned: the binding only logs a warn with `https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/workshoplegalagreement`. So the command prints that URL itself after every successful create/submit ("the item stays hidden until you accept it"), and passes the binding''s warn line through if seen (capture like `startSteam()` does). No FFI of our own (plan 114''s rule).
- `submitItemUpdate` polls its result for 30 s only; a bigger upload may return `false` while Steam still uploads. Task 1 measures with a ~50 MB folder; if it times out, keep polling `getItemUpdateProgress` until status 0 and report "submitted, check the item page" instead of failure.
- Title max 128 chars (`MAX_TITLE_LENGTH`), description max 8000; plan 112''s limits (name ≤ 80, description ≤ 2000) are stricter: fine.

## Command
```
trivia --publish-mod <folder> [--changenote <text>] [--visibility public|friends|unlisted|private] [--content-only] [--dry-run]
```
- Dev: `trivia-desktop --publish-mod …`; packaged: the game executable (Linux `trivia`, Windows `Trivia.exe`), from a terminal with the Steam client running.
- `parseArgs`: `OWN` gains `publish`, `changenote`, `visibility`, `content-only`, `dry-run`; the three value flags need a value; `--changenote`/`--visibility`/`--content-only`/`--dry-run` without `--publish-mod` → error. `USAGE` lists them.
- Headless: the publish branch runs before `requestSingleInstanceLock()` (works with the game closed; running beside the game isn''t checked), opens no window, no `protocol.handle`; `app.whenReady()` only for the Windows dialog; exits with `app.exit(code)`: 0 ok, 1 failed, 2 usage.
- Steps (`app/desktop/publish.mjs`, pure Node: takes `ugc`, `appId`, an output function; no Electron, no binding import, like `workshop.mjs`):
  1. `readManifest(folder, gameVersion)`: errors stop (codes printed with field); warnings printed (`game_version`: "made for 0.1.0, running 0.2.0", unknown keys).
  2. Preview: `preview.png` / `preview.jpg` / `preview.gif` at the root (first found); Steam''s limit 1 MB → error above it; none → warning on first publish (Steam shows a placeholder).
  3. Stage: copy into a temp dir (`os.tmpdir()`) the folder''s files: skip dot files and dot folders (`.git`, `.DS_Store`), the preview file stays (subscribers may use it), symlinks per `contained()` (escaping ones skipped with a warning, never followed out); print file count and total size. The upload is the staged copy, never the folder itself. Temp dir removed at the end (also on failure).
  4. Tags: kinds (manifest `kind` or plan 112''s derivation) + `languages` locale tags (`es`, `en`, …), only names from the tag list the gamemaster entered (plan 114''s setup decision): one constant `WORKSHOP_TAGS` in `publish.mjs`; others dropped with a warning.
  5. `--dry-run` stops here: prints the item it would create/update (id or "new"), title, description length, visibility, tags, preview, staged file list summary; exit 0.
  6. Steam: `startSteam()`; no Steam or `!hasSteamId()` → "needs the Steam client running and logged in" exit 1.
  7. First publish (no `workshopId` in `mod.json`): `createItem` → write `workshopId` (decimal string) into the folder''s `mod.json` (only that key changed, key order and 2-space indent kept, atomic temp + rename) and into the staged copy, print "created item <id>, https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=<id>"; visibility defaults to private (the modder checks the page first).
  8. Update: `startItemUpdate(appId, workshopId)`; `setItemContent(staged)`; unless `--content-only`: title (`name`), description (`description`), preview, tags; visibility only on first publish or when `--visibility` is given (an update doesn''t flip a public item back). `submitItemUpdate(handle, changenote ?? "")`.
  9. Progress: while submitting, a 500 ms timer prints `getItemUpdateProgress` lines on stdout when status or the percent step (10 %) changes: `preparing config`, `preparing content`, `uploading 40 % (12.3 / 30.1 MB)`, `uploading preview`, `committing`.
  10. Result: ok → item URL, legal agreement line; failure → the binding''s captured reason (e.g. not the owner of the item, Workshop not enabled for the app), exit 1. A failed first publish after `createItem` keeps `workshopId` (the next run updates the empty item).
- Windows output: a packaged Windows Electron app has no console, so stdout may be invisible. Packaged on win32: every line also goes to `<folder>/../<id>-publish.log` (beside the mod, not inside it) and the end result shows in `dialog.showMessageBox` (ok: URL; failed: reason + log path). Linux/Deck: stdout/stderr only.

## Published file id: `mod.json` `workshopId`
- Picked over a sidecar: `setItemContent` uploads a whole folder, so a sidecar would ship anyway (or need its own exclusion rule); in `mod.json` it travels to subscribers, where plan 114''s `workshop_id_mismatch` warning checks it against the item''s real id (a copied mod re-uploaded under another item shows the warning).
- Validator (plan 112''s `mods.mjs`): optional `workshopId`, string `^[1-9]\d{0,19}$`; known key, not an unknown-key warning. Recorded: decision 132: Workshop publishing: where the published file id lives.
- A modder who wants a fresh item: delete `workshopId`.

## Tasks

### 1. `publish.mjs`: validate, stage, upload
- `app/desktop/publish.mjs` as above: `preparePublish(folder, gameVersion)` (steps 1–5, returns the plan + staged dir) and `publish(ugc, appId, prepared, opts, out)` (steps 7–10); `WORKSHOP_TAGS`; `mod.json` rewrite of `workshopId`; progress formatter.
- `mods.mjs` (plan 112): `workshopId` field; plan 114''s `workshop_id_mismatch` reads it (if plan 114''s task 1 is done; else it picks up the field name from here).
- Done when: a throw-away script with a fake `ugc` (records calls, scripted progress, `createItem` → 123) shows: first publish creates, writes `workshopId`, sets private, uploads the staged dir (no `.git`, no escaping symlink); second run updates without visibility; `--content-only` sets content only; a mod with a bad field, a preview > 1 MB, an unknown tag each give their message; per-commit checks pass.

### 2. `main.mjs`: flags, headless branch, Windows output
- `parseArgs` and `USAGE` as above; publish branch before the instance lock; `startSteam()` reuse (its stderr lines stay); exit codes; win32 packaged log file + message box; `stopSteam()` before exit.
- Spike inside the task: one real upload isn''t possible for workers (decision 111, workers never log in): check the 30 s `submitItemUpdate` behaviour in the binding''s source and implement the progress-poll fallback either way; the gamemaster check (task 3) confirms with a big folder.
- Done when: `trivia-desktop --publish-mod <tmp mod> --dry-run` prints the summary and exits 0 without Steam; without `--dry-run` and without Steam it prints the Steam line and exits 1; `--changenote x` alone → usage error exit 2; a packaged Linux build (`trivia-desktop-build --dir`) runs the dry run the same; per-commit checks pass.

### 3. Gamemaster check under Steam (pm decision, `--once`)
- After plan 114''s setup decision ("Steamworks: enable Workshop for ¡Trivia! and a second Steam account?") is answered and tasks 1–2 are done: a decision with the checklist (workers never log in): publish a tiny CSS mod (`trivia-desktop --publish-mod mods/<x>`) → item created private, `workshopId` in `mod.json`, legal agreement accepted at the printed URL; update with `--changenote "test"` and a ~50 MB folder → progress lines, change note on the item page; `--visibility public` → visible to the second account, which subscribes and sees it load (plan 114''s check); on Windows the log file and message box appear.
- Options "all ok" (recommended) / "problems: …"; make this task pending on it (`pm task-pending-decision`) and close with the answer; failures → follow-up tasks in this plan. May share the session with plan 114''s Workshop check.
- Done when: the gamemaster answers; follow-ups added for any failure.

### 4. Docs
- `plans/18-steam-integration.md` "Modding": subsection "Publishing" (command, flags, steps, staging and what''s skipped, `workshopId`, visibility default, tags, legal agreement, Windows log file, `trivia-steam` is for our builds only); part 10''s guide copies it.
- `README.md`: how-to "Publish a mod to the Workshop" (three lines + the command).
- AGENTS.md "Commands": `trivia-desktop` line gains `--publish-mod <folder>` (`--dry-run` without Steam).
- `directory.md`: `app/desktop/` row names `publish.mjs`.
- `plans/backlog.md`: new row "In-game publish button on the mods screen (part 6), calls `publish.mjs` through a bridge channel; needs a pm decision (page triggering an upload)" | plan 102 part 8 | new.
- Done when: the docs name the command, `workshopId` and the recorded decision; per-commit checks pass.

## Order
Plan 112''s tasks 1–3 first (validator, `contained()`); then 1 → 2 → 4; 3 after 2 and plan 114''s setup decision. Part 9 may reuse `--dry-run` for players'' checks; part 12 publishes the four example mods with this command.', '', '2026-10-13T18:19:27Z', 907);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (120, 15, 'Player bundle kit: format doc, template, AI prompt', 350, 'Part 9 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). Players make their own question bundles, with or without their own AI: a format doc for players, a template mod folder that loads as is, and a standalone AI prompt that writes the pool in our format. They check the result with `trivia --check-mod <folder>`, which runs the same validators as the loader and lists every dropped question with its reason. The kit lives in the repo''s `modding/` folder (part 10''s guide folder) and ships in the game''s install folder.

## Binding
- Plan 102''s ground rules: page only (the check reads mod files as data in main, never runs them), no second path (one validator for loader, check and publish), our data stays ours (template and player pools never enter `authoring/`, `qgen` or our pool), Steam only, strings via `t()`, per-commit checks.
- decision 133: Player bundle kit: check with `trivia --check-mod`, kit in `modding/` shipped in the install folder (this plan''s choices).
- decision 128: Bundles from mods: the shell merges `bundles.json`; mod ids namespaced `<mod id>:<id>` (the format documented here); decision 129: Credits for mod media (runtime section; the doc tells modders to fill `credit`).
- decision 38: one base bundle per game, offered per `languages`; decision 33 / decision 51: non-base off on the configure screen, on in solo; decision 112: a missing pool file = not offered in that locale.
- decision 124: output paths (`lib/data.ts` → `lib/data.js`): the check imports our built `engine/pool.js`.
- decision 132: `workshopId` in `mod.json` (the doc names it, the template leaves it out).
- Plan 112: `readManifest`, error/warning codes, `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`, `<userData>/mods/`. Plan 115: mod bundle format, `bundleRows`, `modPool` (checks, media rewrite), `modIssues()`. Plan 119: headless branch before the instance lock, exit codes, Windows log file + message box. Plan 118: mods screen shows `modIssues()` counts.
- `app/data/age-groups.json`: scale 1–15, groups kids 1–6, young_teens 1–10, young_adults 4–13, adults 5–15 (windows), 12 levels each.
- House style: `authoring/tools/prompts/house-style.md` (adapted, not linked: the prompt is standalone).
- Not here: the modding guide, example mods and store link (part 10, task 909), in-game publish button (backlog), achievements with mods (part 7).

## Format doc: `modding/bundles.md`
- English, AGENTS.md doc style; for players, no repo knowledge assumed.
- Folder layout: `mod.json` (fields of plan 112, `kind: ["bundle"]` optional), `data/bundles.json`, `data/bundles/<bundle>/categories.json`, `names.<locale>.json` (optional), `pool.json` (es) / `pool.<locale>.json`, media below the bundle folder.
- `bundles.json` row: `id`, `kind` (`base` replaces ours for that game, anything else adds to ours; when to pick which), `languages`, `name`, `description` (player text, no translation keys).
- `categories.json`: categories with slug, name, subcategories; every question''s `subcategory` must be listed; `names.<locale>.json` for category names in other languages.
- Pool file: `version: 1`, `questions[]` with the `PoolQuestion` fields: `id` (rule from task 1, unique in the mod, the game prefixes `<mod id>:`), `difficulty`, `subcategory`, `description`, `question`, `answer`, exactly 3 `wrong_answers`, exactly 3 `hints` (vague → strong), `fun_fact`, `media` (optional), `background` (audio only); `status` optional (`approved`). One file per language, same ids across languages (a translation = same id).
- Difficulty 1–15 "who can answer it" (the house style''s anchors) and the age-group windows table from `age-groups.json`; what to cover for which audience (e.g. a kids bundle: 1–6 spread evenly).
- Media: image/audio/video files in the bundle folder, relative `file_url`, `role` (essential: the question needs it; illustrative; decorative), `credit` required for files the modder didn''t make (decision 129); size advice (images ≤ 1920 px, ≤ 500 KB); no web URLs (dropped). No media: leave `media` out (task 1).
- Writing advice: short house-style rules (one breath, four options of the same form, no giveaways, real-world superlatives, family-friendly).
- Where it goes: `mods/` / `<userData>/mods/`, restart, mods screen; checking with `--check-mod`; publishing (plan 119''s command, one line).
- No stability promise (plan 102).

## Template: `modding/template-bundle/`
- `mod.json`: id `template-bundle`, languages `es`, `en`; a one-line note in `bundles.md` to change the id before publishing (decision 127: the id is the key).
- One non-base bundle (e.g. `solar-system`, «El sistema solar» / "The solar system"), 1 category, 2–3 subcategories, `names.en.json`.
- `pool.json` (es) and `pool.en.json`: 15 questions, one per difficulty 1–15, same ids; written by the worker in house style from facts it is sure of (no `qgen`: player content, never our pool); one image question (`img/<name>.png`, made by us, credit «¡Trivia! template · CC0»), the rest text-only.
- Loads as is: copy into `mods/`, restart, the bundle shows on the configure screen.

## AI prompt: `modding/bundle-prompt.md`
- One file: a short "how to use" header for the player (paste into your AI, answer its questions, save its files, run `--check-mod`), then the prompt in a fenced block.
- Prompt (English instructions; the AI talks with the player in the player''s language):
  1. Asks first, one message: topic, audience (age group → difficulty window, table inline), question language(s) from ours (es, en, de), how many questions (suggest 30–60), bundle name, base or add-on (default add-on).
  2. Writes: `bundles.json` row, `categories.json`, `names.<locale>.json` if more than one language, `pool[.<locale>].json`, each in its own fenced JSON block with its file path above it.
  3. Rules inline: our pool fields, house-style digest (options, hints, short, no giveaways, real-world clues, family-friendly), difficulty anchors, spread over the window, no media (the player adds images by hand per `bundles.md`), unique ids `<topic>-001`.
  4. No web search assumed: only facts it is sure of; drop a question rather than guess; no numbers it can''t vouch for.
  5. Big sets in parts ("say ''next'' for the next 20"), same ids continue.
- Tested once by the worker (task 4).

## Check: `trivia --check-mod <folder>`
- Headless, before the instance lock, no window, no Steam (like plan 119''s `--dry-run`); exit 0 no errors, 1 errors, 2 usage. Windows packaged: plan 119''s log file beside the folder + message box.
- `app/desktop/check-mod.mjs` (pure Node, no Electron): `readManifest` (plan 112), `bundleRows` (plan 115), per bundle and language: `categories.json`, pool file, `modPool` imported from our built `engine/pool.js` (dev `app/client/dist/`, packaged `resources/client/`; our file, never the mod''s), media files present and inside the folder (`contained()`).
- Output: errors and warnings with codes and plain text; per bundle and language: questions ok, dropped (id + reason); difficulty coverage per age group (questions in its window, levels with none); "loads as is" / "fix the errors above".
- Plan 119''s `preparePublish` may call it (task 5 adds that if plan 119 task 1 is done, else a line in plan 119''s docs task).

## Tasks

### 1. Text-only questions and per-question reasons (client, after plan 115 task 2)
- `modPool` (`engine/pool.ts`): `media` missing or null → normalized to a decorative image with `file_url: null`; returns dropped questions with reason codes (`no_text`, `wrong_answers`, `hints`, `difficulty`, `subcategory`, `status`, `bad_id`, `duplicate_id`, `bad_media`); raw id rule `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`, unique within the mod; `modIssues()` keeps its counts.
- `Question.svelte`: the «media missing» line only when a file was set and failed or the role is essential (ours unchanged: they always have media).
- Done when: a `pool.test.ts` case covers a text-only question and each reason code; a text-only mod question plays in `trivia-desktop` (dev switch) with no missing-media line; per-commit checks pass.

### 2. `--check-mod` (shell, after task 1, plan 111 and plan 119 task 2)
- `check-mod.mjs` as above; `main.mjs` `parseArgs` (`OWN`, `USAGE`), headless branch, output helper shared with plan 119.
- Done when: `trivia-desktop --check-mod <tmp mod>` on a throw-away mod (bad `mod.json` field, a bad bundle row, two bad questions, a missing image, an absolute URL) lists each with its code and exits 1; fixed, it exits 0; a packaged Linux build (`trivia-desktop-build --dir`) runs the same; per-commit checks pass.

### 3. Format doc and template (after task 2)
- `modding/bundles.md` and `modding/template-bundle/` as above; the image made by a throw-away script or by hand (PNG, CC0, ours).
- Done when: `--check-mod modding/template-bundle` exits 0 with 15 ok per language; a copy in a temp `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR` loads in `trivia-desktop`: the bundle shows marked player-made, a game with it on plays its image question, the Credits screen lists the image; temp copy deleted; per-commit checks pass.

### 4. AI prompt (after task 3)
- `modding/bundle-prompt.md` as above.
- Done when: the worker runs the prompt once through an AI at hand (e.g. `claude -p`, answering its questions with a sample: "dinosaurs, kids, en + de, 20 questions"), saves the files into a temp mod with a `mod.json`, and `--check-mod` passes with ≤ 2 dropped (else the prompt is fixed and rerun); coverage line shows the kids window filled; temp mod deleted; one line on the result in the done note.

### 5. Ship in the install folder, release check
- `app/desktop/electron-builder.yml` `extraFiles`: `../../modding` → `modding` (beside the executable); `credits_check.mjs`: the template image either listed as ours (CC0) or `modding/` excluded with a comment; `trivia-steam stage` copies it with the unpacked builds.
- `trivia-release-check`: a step `modkit`: the packaged executable''s `--check-mod <unpacked>/modding/template-bundle` exits 0 (keeps the template valid across updates).
- Plan 119''s `preparePublish` calls `checkMod` (warnings only) if plan 119 task 1 is done.
- Done when: `trivia-desktop-build --dir` has `modding/` in `linux-unpacked/` and `win-unpacked/`; `trivia-release-check --skip tag` passes its `modkit` and `credits` steps; per-commit checks pass.

### 6. Docs
- `directory.md`: rows `modding/` (player kit: `bundles.md`, `bundle-prompt.md`, `template-bundle/`; shipped in the install folder), `app/desktop/check-mod.mjs`.
- `README.md`: how-to "Make your own question bundle" (three lines: copy the template or use the prompt, `--check-mod`, `mods/`).
- AGENTS.md "Commands": `trivia-desktop` line gains `--check-mod <folder>`.
- `plans/22-bundles.md` "Player bundles (mods)" (plan 115): link to `modding/bundles.md`; `plans/18-steam-integration.md` "Modding": `--check-mod`, kit shipped in the install folder.
- `pm decision consequences 133 "…"`.
- Done when: docs name decision 133 and the paths; part 10 (task 909) can link `modding/bundles.md` and `bundle-prompt.md`; per-commit checks pass.

## Order
After plan 115 tasks 1–2, plan 111 and plan 119 tasks 1–2. 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6. Part 10''s guide links this kit; part 12 may publish the template as the bundle example.', '', '2026-10-13T18:22:27Z', 908);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, intro, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (121, 15, 'Modding guide, examples and the store link', 300, 'Part 10 of masterplan 102 (milestone 15: Modding and Steam Workshop). Modders get one guide, `modding/README.md`, that explains everything parts 1–9 built (format, sources and load order, overlay rules, `window.trivia`, mods screen and safe start, publishing, player bundles) plus four example mods that load from `mods/` as is: a bundle, a CSS theme, a rules tweak and a minimal full conversion. The guide ships in the install folder and is linked from the store page and the Workshop page. Runs after parts 1–9 (and 11): it documents their final state, it doesn''t design anything.

## Binding
- decision 134: Modding guide: path, language, examples (recorded by task 909): `modding/README.md`, English only, shipped in the install folder, online at `https://github.com/rubenmoor/trivia/blob/main/modding/README.md`; examples `modding/examples/{theme,rules-tweak,conversion}/`; bundle example = `modding/template-bundle/` (plan 120); examples without media, MIT (decision 107).
- decision 133: Player bundle kit: `modding/` holds `bundles.md`, `bundle-prompt.md`, `template-bundle/`, shipped via electron-builder `extraFiles`; `trivia --check-mod <folder>`; release-check step `modkit`.
- decision 124 (module paths), decision 127 (Workshop identity, duplicates), decision 128 (bundles merge, `<mod id>:<id>`), decision 129 (mod media credits), decision 130 (`window.triviaShell` vs `window.trivia`), decision 131 (`/mods.js`, `setup(trivia)`, run order, 5 s timeout), decision 132 (`workshopId`, first publish private), decision 111 (Steam only; dev switch), decision 108 (repo public), decision 107 (code MIT, questions reserved).
- decision 123 (achievements with mods on): pending; the guide states part 7''s result (plan from task 906), whatever it is.
- Plan 102''s ground rules: page only, no stability promise (module paths and `window.trivia` may change; renames go in patch notes), offline (mod code gets no network), docs per AGENTS.md style.
- Sources the guide condenses (each plan''s docs task writes them; the guide is the player-facing text, they keep internals): `plans/18-steam-integration.md` "Modding" sections (plan 111 task 6 "module paths", plan 112 task 5 incl. the `mod.json` JSON Schema, plan 114 task 5, plan 115 task 5, plan 117 task 6 "`window.trivia`", plan 118 task 4, plan 119 task 4 "Publishing"), `plans/22-bundles.md` "Player bundles (mods)", `modding/bundles.md`.
- Plan 88 (store page): texts live in `plans/17a-store-page.md`, every text set approved by a pm decision, workers never log into Steamworks; task 666 (store texts per language, open) and task 672 (entry checklist, open).
- Not here: the end-to-end check from the Workshop on a second account, release-check mods rows (part 12, task 910), the player bundle format itself (plan 120), in-game publish button (backlog).

## Guide: `modding/README.md`
Intro paragraph in full sentences (what mods can do, that they''re files laid over the game), then bullets per AGENTS.md style. Sections:
- **Quick start:** copy an example into the local mods folder (packaged `<userData>/mods/` with the real path per OS: Windows `%APPDATA%\<app name>\mods`, Linux/Deck `~/.config/<app name>/mods`, as the shell resolves it; dev `mods/`, `TRIVIA_MODS_DIR`), restart, Mods screen, allow mods (one-time notice).
- **Mod format:** `mod.json` fields (`id`, `name`, `description`, `version`, `game`, `languages`, `entry`, `kind`, `workshopId`), limits, error and warning codes as the mods screen shows them; the JSON Schema text (from plan 112''s section; "the validator is the source of truth"); `--check-mod`.
- **Sources and load order:** local folder, Workshop subscriptions (restart after updates; downloading items load next start), local shadows Workshop (decision 127), `mods.json` per machine, top wins, new mods appended on.
- **Overlay rules:** URL ↔ file path (`app://trivia/<rel>` → `<mod>/<rel>`), naming rule (decision 124: `.svelte.js`, `.svelte.css`, `vendor/`, `_virtual/`), what can''t be overridden (plan 111''s list, `/data/` except bundles, `/media`, `mod.json`), match case, source maps and how to see our source (devtools via `TRIVIA_DEVTOOLS=1` if plan 111/117 keep it player-usable, else the public repo at the game''s version tag `v<version>`), screen list (`game/screens/*`, `game/question/*` per plan 116), full conversion = own `index.html`.
- **Code add-ons:** `entry`, `export default async function setup(trivia)`, root `style.css` auto-linked, run order (bottom first, top patches last), 5 s timeout and error codes, every `window.trivia` member with a one-line example (plan 117''s list as built: `version`, `mods`, `mod`, `modules`, `data`, `actions.before/after`, `i18n.add`, `screens.replace/dom/show`, `menu.add`, `on`, `css`), importing our modules by path (read-only exports), Svelte components must use `/vendor/svelte/…`; `window.triviaShell` exists but is ours (no promise).
- **Mods screen and safe start:** on/off, order, restart to apply, one-time notice, crashed start → next start without mods, `--no-mods` in Steam''s launch options; a full conversion calls `window.triviaShell.mods.ready()` (plan 118).
- **Player bundles:** short summary + links to `bundles.md`, `bundle-prompt.md`, `template-bundle/`; base vs add-on, ids namespaced, credits (decision 129), never in our pool or reports.
