INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web, model, worktree) VALUES (1131, 17, 'qgen output as a plain log: resume notes and one line per Claude or media call', 'work', 'done', 500, '2026-10-15, authoring/tools/progress.py (plain log: waiting() start/result lines with time, step, item; resumed(); fail(); bar, \r and minute status line gone), qgen.py (claude() result lines incl. retry n/2 and usage limit; parallel() names items; resume line per step; run_steps repeats the failed step''s FAILED lines before exit, so RUNBOOK''s last 30 lines show the cause), media.py, providers/__init__.py, directory.md, plans/07 QG-18. Checked with a fake claude on PATH (ok, failure+retry, slow, all retries failed, usage limit) against a resumed copy of work/batch-8 and fake provider searches: no \r or escape codes. A first check run picked up the real claude (dev shell PATH): 5 rate calls, ~$0.77, written only to the temp copy.', '`qgen` output is built for a person at a terminal: a live progress bar, `\r` rewrites of the last line, and a status line every minute when piped (QG-18, QG-20 in `plans/07-question-generation.md`). Mostly an agent reads it now, through `pm run` and the RUNBOOK. That reader needs a log that only appends: what was skipped because a step resumed, and every slow or failing outside call.

What
- Drop the bar: in `authoring/tools/progress.py`, remove the `LIVE` mode (`_Stream`, `_draw`, `_clear`, `\r\033[K`), the per-`Bar` redraw/heartbeat thread and the minute status line. Keep the one summary line per step (`label: n/total in 3m10s, k failed`).
- Resume: when a step skips work that is already done, print one line, not one per item: `draft: 14/20 done earlier, skipped; 6 to do` or `rate: all done earlier, skipped`. `qgen batch` says `Resuming batch-N` already (`cmd_batch`); keep it. `run_steps`''s `== [i/n] step` header stays.
- One line per outside call, in `qgen batch` and in single steps (not hidden by `progress.verbose`), with HH:MM:SS, the step and the item:
  - `claude()` in `qgen.py`: before the call `12:01:07 claude opus draft geografía/ríos`; after it `… ok 2m19s`, or `… failed (exit 1): <short reason>, retry 1/2`, or `… usage limit`
  - media providers: each search (`providers/__init__.py`, `progress.waiting(... search)`), each preview download and each sync/download host call in `media.py`: start line + `ok <n> results 3s` / `failed: <reason>`
  - Simplest route: have `progress.waiting()` log the start and end itself (it already wraps exactly these calls) and give it the item name; ImageMagick doesn''t need a line.
- The output must stay readable when the calls run in parallel (`--jobs`): every line names its item, each line is written whole (under the lock), never a half line.

Docs
- `authoring/RUNBOOK.md` asks for "the last 30 lines" on a failure: those must still show the cause. Before the exit, `run_steps` prints a closing block that repeats the step''s `FAILED …` lines, or the RUNBOOK asks for that block instead.
- Update the docstring of `progress.py` (or rename the module if it''s now only a logger) and its row in `directory.md`.

Done when
- `qgen batch` piped to a file: no `\r` or escape codes; a resumed run shows one skip line per step it resumes; every `claude -p` call and provider call has a start line and a result line.
- Same on a terminal (no bar).
- Check with a throw-away fake `claude` on `PATH` (one success, one failure, one slow call) and a resumed `work/<run>`; don''t spend real Claude calls.', '2026-10-15T17:26:09Z', '2026-10-15T18:47:04Z', NULL, 0, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web, model, worktree) VALUES (1132, NULL, 'write an executive summary', 'exec', 'done', 10000, '2026-10-15, executive summary 1 stored via pm summary add; no files', 'Write an executive summary for the owner of this project: a busy person who doesn''t read code, tasks or plans. Write
it for an interested outsider who knows nothing about the project: plain, good English in full sentences, no jargon.
Change nothing in the project; stage nothing.

Read first: the previous summary (`pm summary show`), the milestones (`pm milestone list`, `pm show m<id>`), and what
was done since the previous summary (`git log --stat <its commit>..HEAD`, `pm show <id>` where you need more).

Write, in this order:
1. **What the project is**, in one or two sentences: what it makes, for whom, and why. Every summary starts this way.
2. **Where it stands**, in one sentence: how far along it is toward the current goal (the open milestone), and
   whether that is on track, in terms of what the product can do.
3. **What''s new** since the previous summary (for the first one: everything so far): what the product can do now
   that it couldn''t before, and why that matters. Explain it to the outsider; don''t go into detail.
4. **What comes next**, plus a risk only if it could change the outcome or the timing.

Leave out everything about how the work is managed: decisions, sign-offs, tasks, plans, runs, failures, cost, tests
and pm itself; never ids, file names or counts of these. One screen at most, about 250 words, few headings.
Store it: `pm summary add < summary.md`, delete summary.md, then close your task.', '2026-10-15T18:39:31Z', '2026-10-15T18:47:43Z', NULL, 0, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web, model, worktree) VALUES (1133, NULL, 'write a resource use report', 'exec', 'done', 10000, '2026-10-15, resource use report 1 via pm report add (975 done tasks in 12 categories); no files', 'Write a resource use report: sort every done task of this project into categories of the work itself, so the owner
sees where time and tokens went. Change nothing in the project; stage nothing.

`pm report tasks` lists the done tasks: id, kind, the commits that did the work, subject, note.

1. First pass: read every task''s diff (`git show --stat <commit>`, then `git show <commit> -- . '':!tasks.sql''`;
   skim large ones) and, where the diff doesn''t tell, `pm show <id>`. Note in notes.md, one line per task: what it
   actually built or changed, and loose candidate categories. Name them in the project''s own terms: what the product
   is made of and what making it takes, e.g. for a game 3D assets, question authoring, store publishing, UI,
   translations; never pm''s task kinds. Planning and upkeep tasks are classified like the rest, by what they plan
   or touch.
2. Decide on a set of mutually exclusive categories that together cover every task, each with one sentence of
   criteria that decides which tasks belong in it. Start from scratch; don''t look at earlier reports.
3. Second pass: apply the criteria and put every task in exactly one category; a task that spans several goes
   where most of its work went.

Store it: write report.json, `{"categories": [{"name": "…", "criteria": "…", "tasks": [3, 7]}]}`, then
`pm report add < report.json` (it refuses a report that misses a done task or has one twice), delete notes.md and
