     M authoring/data/questions.json
    ?? authoring/data/spot-checks.json

Look: `git show pm/task-1144-run-1144-left`. Keep: `git merge pm/task-1144-run-1144-left`. Then `git branch -D pm/task-1144-run-1144-left`.', '', NULL, 'branch merged into main in 27c7988, then deleted', '2026-10-15T21:16:51Z', '2026-10-16T02:36:55Z', NULL, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (226, 'QC batch-8: which changes?', 'answered', '["recommended set", "all", "none"]', '`qgen report --qc batch-8` (authoring/reports/batch-8.md "## Quality control"): spot check 30 of 30; agreement with the LLM review 26/30 (87 %) ✗ below `quality.min_agreement` 90 % → release bar ✗ (D-79: only QC step 4''s proposals, no full audit, no second spot check). False approve 3 (10 %, audit flagged 1), false needs_work 1 (q-1854), difficulty |Δ| ≥ 2: 2, media changed 0, audit/human agreement 28/30 (93 %). Audit: 1 major among 57 approved (q-1868, giveaway). Trend: batch-8 is the first batch with a spot check, so no two-batch audit-vs-review trend proposal.

You marked no problem kinds, so all 4 disagreements sit under "no problem given"; by your feedback I read them as: ambiguity 2 (q-1868, q-1733), regional usage 1 (q-1691), false needs_work 1 (q-1854, no change needed). QC.md threshold (≥ 2 per kind, ≥ 2 major audit findings, any `fact` false approve): only ambiguity passes.

Proposals (each becomes a task in `plan 16: Quality control after every batch`, its priority):

A. Ambiguity, recommended (q-1868, q-1733).
   - `review.md` reason 2: for a "¿Quién soy?"/riddle with a generic answer (a kind of person, not a named one), check each wrong answer against every event described: if a wrong answer could also have done it ("un arqueólogo británico" could have thrown a stone into a cave), it''s needs_work; the `fix` adds a detail only the answer has.
   - `house-style.md` "Keep it short" or "Format": the question asks exactly one thing; a rhetorical question or quoted thought inside a riddle goes in quotes («…») so the players don''t hear two questions (q-1733: «¿Con qué grano dejé de merecer mi nombre?» then «¿Qué paradoja soy?»). `review.md`: two questions without quotes → needs_work, `fix` adds the quotes.

B. Regional premise, below the threshold (1 disagreement, q-1691), only in "all".
   - `locale-es.md` / `review.md` reason 8: a `base` question must not rest on a word, brand-as-generic or usage known in one country only, Spain included ("cinta Scotch" is Spain''s name for adhesive tape; Latin America says "cinta adhesiva", "diurex", …); needs_work, feedback names it as regional. `neutralize.md` gets Spain examples next to the Colombian ones.
   - Not proposed here: a "Spain" regional bundle (your q-1691 feedback); that is a new bundle, a decision of its own if you want it.

Options: "recommended set" = A; "all" = A + B; "none".', '', 'all', NULL, '2026-10-15T21:41:52Z', '2026-10-15T21:45:10Z', 53, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-15, task 53: proposals A and B became tasks in plan 17 at plan 16''s priority 650 (plan 16 is closed): task 1182: Ambiguity in riddles: generic answers and one question per text (QC batch-8 A) (review.md reason 2, house-style.md one question per text), task 1183: Regional premise: no Spain-only words or brands in base (QC batch-8 B) (review.md reason 8, locale-es.md, neutralize.md Spain examples); authoring/reports/batch-8.md ''Changes from this QC: 1182, 1183''. No Spain bundle (would need its own decision).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (227, 'Media share below 15 %: lower the none weight, and which residual-miss fixes?', 'answered', '["Weight only: none 7 → 5, no residual-miss fixes (rework covers them)", "Weight + query hints (blank map, no logos/franchise characters as essential)", "All: weight + query hints + second research round + role change at record", "Only lower the media share bound to 10 % in targets.json (changes decision 13''s set), no weight change", "None: keep weight and bound, ack the ✗"]', '`plan 146: Media yield` task B (`task 1189: Diagnose media share below 15 % (batches 6–8) and propose fixes`). Media share (merged with `axes.stimulus` ≠ none) fell 25.6 % → 13.8 % → 12.2 %, ✗ against `decision 13: Batch health thresholds and stop rule?`''s 15–50 % twice. Read-only script over `work/batch-{6,7,8}/` and the pool.

**Diagnosis: the slot plan explains the whole fall; every later stage is flat.**

| stage | b6 | b7 | b8 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| slots (subcats × slots each) | 120 (30 × 4) | 1026 (57 × 18) | 1656 (92 × 18) |
| options with a stimulus | 45.3 % | 28.4 % | 20.8 % |
| slots with ≥ 1 media option | 73 % | 58 % | 45 % |
| subcats with no media value fitting (`axis_fit` ≤ 2) | 2/30 | 5/57 | 16/92 |
| drafter picks media when offered (drafted slots) | 37 % | 27 % | 28 % |
| skip rate: media-offering / text-only slots | 26 / 19 % | 33 / 27 % | 33 / 30 % |
| skips naming media | 16/29 | 78/313 | 65/519 |
| media share of drafts | 26.4 % | 14.9 % | 12.4 % |
| drop rate media / text | 12.5 / 9.0 % | 19.8 / 12.4 % | 14.2 / 12.5 % |
| needs_work at record media / text | 24 / 18 % | 22 / 18 % | 6.6 / 4.2 % |
| media share of merged | 25.6 % | 13.8 % | 12.2 % |

- Drafts → merged: share almost unchanged (26.4 → 25.6, 14.9 → 13.8, 12.4 → 12.2). Media drafts drop a bit more (giveaway: the photo shows the answer) but it costs ≤ 1 point.
- Options → drafts: steady ratio ≈ 0.55–0.6 (drafter takes the text option ~ 7 of 10 times it can choose).
- Options: 45 → 21 %. Two causes:
  - Subcategory mix: batches 7–8 move into the long tail of non-visual subcats (pop music, musicals, fiction, Harry Potter, reality shows, slogans, sayings, numbers…; free providers have no media for franchises and logos). Fit-only expected option share 20.4 → 18.7 → 14.7 %; 16 of 92 batch-8 subcats can''t offer media at all.
  - Slots per subcat 4 → 18: with 4 slots, `OFFERED_FACTOR` (0.3, `concepts.py`) penalises `none` after slot 1 while the media values are still fresh, a strong boost; over 18 slots every value has been offered, the boost fades back to the base weights (`none` 7 vs. 4.2 for all media values together, `authoring/data/question-axes.json`).
- Not the cause: drafter prompt, drops, record, review. Per subcategory: media concentrates in animals, places, art, religion, pharaohs (Reptiles, Peces: 7 each in b8).
- Context: every approved question already shows a picture (illustrative/decorative); media share counts only essential stimuli. Pool base-bundle share now 15.0 %.

**Fix for the cause: lower the `none` weight.** Simulated slot draw for batch-8''s subcats on today''s pool (18 slots, seed 1):

| `none` weight | options with stimulus | slots with media | expected merged share (× 0.59) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 7 (now) | 27.4 % | 58 % | ≈ 16 % |
| 5 | 39.2 % | 69 % | ≈ 23 % |
| 4 | 46.6 % | 75 % | ≈ 27 % |

- `none` 7 → 5: lands mid-range (15–50 %), still text-first; non-visual subcats stay text-only via `axis_fit`. Cost: media drafts drop and needs_work slightly more (b8: +1.7 / +2.4 points on the media part), so ≈ +0.5 point overall.
- At 7 the usage factor alone may lift batch-9 to ≈ 16 % (the pool is now under media''s expected share), but only just above the bound.
- Alternative: lower the bound to 10 % in `targets.json` (decision 13''s looser set had 10–60 %), changing decision 13''s set; accepts fewer picture questions.

**Residual misses** (plan 146 "What would raise it"), counted from batch-7/8 "## Needs work" (batch-7: ~31 media lines, batch-8: 9 no-pick + 12 licence `replace`):
- Second `research` round: off-target/no candidates = most misses (b7 ~17, b8 6: q-1331, q-1452, q-1485, q-1840, q-2053, q-2187). Maybe half recover → ≈ 3–8 per batch at record; but `qgen rework` (`decision 3: How are needs_work questions (33 now, more per batch) resolved?`) already recovers them (4 media needs_work left in the pool) for ≈ $0.12 each, so the gain is < $1 per batch and a little rework time.
- Query hints at draft ("blank map" for map stimuli, no logos/franchise characters as essential): labelled maps b7 2 (q-0654, q-0804), b8 1 (q-1423); logos/franchises b7 ~6 (q-0658, q-0983, q-1027, q-1204, q-1206, q-1209), b8 1 (q-1811). ≈ 2–8 per batch; a prompt line, cheap.
- Role change `essential` → `illustrative` at record: 1 case in two batches (q-1583). Not worth code.
- Contact sheet not matching candidates: b7 ~5 (q-0614, q-0656, q-0896, q-1100, q-0787), b8 0: likely gone, no fix.
- Licence `replace` (12 in b8) is `decision 47: Replace the files on the licence list`, not this decision.

Options: recommended first. "Weight" = `none` 7 → 5 in `authoring/data/question-axes.json`, bound stays 15 %.', '', 'Weight + query hints: none 7 → 5 in question-axes.json, bound stays 15 %; drafter query hints: blank map for map stimuli, no logos/franchise characters as essential. No second research round, no role change at record (rework covers them).', NULL, '2026-10-15T22:01:27Z', '2026-10-16T00:32:30Z', 1189, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1190: 2026-10-15, task 1190: authoring/data/question-axes.json weights.stimulus.none 7 → 5 (base media share of weights ≈ 46 %, simulated merged ≈ 23 % on batch-8''s subcats: a guide for batch-9, not a forecast); media share bound stays 15 % in targets.json, decision 13''s set unchanged; authoring/tools/prompts/draft.md (drafter, English from batch-9): map stimuli query a blank map, logos/trademarks/franchise characters never essential; no second research round, no role change at record (qgen rework covers them); plans/20-pipeline-efficiency.md weight figure updated.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (228, 'batch-9: health stop, ack?', 'answered', '[]', '`qgen batch` for `task 1185: run batch-9` exited 2 (RUNBOOK exit-2 row); no new batch started, no claude calls (no `claude:` line printed). Output verbatim:

```
batch-8 fails the batch health thresholds: overlap rate, max final−target gap 11–15, media share.
See `qgen report --batches` or authoring/reports/batches.md. The developer looks at it and clears the stop with: qgen health ack batch-8 "<note>"
```

Only the developer clears it: `qgen health ack batch-8 "<note>"`; then task 1185 reruns plain `qgen batch`.', '', 'before continuing with batch-9 we need an extensive review of batch 8: quadruple the sample size for human review to 120 and have me review those first. incorporate my feedback and we see from there.', NULL, '2026-10-15T22:02:05Z', '2026-10-15T22:07:18Z', 1185, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-15, task 1185: task 1191: Extended review of batch-8: 120-question spot check (decision 228) added to plan 17 at priority 650; task 1185 waits on decision 229 (continue after the review and the developer''s health ack).

2026-10-17, task 1186: the 120-question extended review of batch-8 happened (task 1191, decision 231 -> tasks 1248–1252); batch-9 then ran (decisions 229, 257). batch-9''s QC reverted to the normal spot-check size of 30 (decision 234): 28/30 agreement (93 %). The size-120 option stays available per batch via ''size'' in authoring/data/spot-checks.json (QC.md).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (229, 'Continue batch-9 after the extended batch-8 review?', 'answered', '["go: acked, run batch-9", "not yet"]', '`task 1185: run batch-9` waits per `decision 228: batch-9: health stop, ack?`: first `task 1191: Extended review of batch-8: 120-question spot check (decision 228)` (120 spot-checked, feedback incorporated as tasks). Then the developer clears the health stop with `qgen health ack batch-8 "<note>"` and answers here; task 1185 reruns plain `qgen batch`.', '', 'go: acked, run batch-9', NULL, '2026-10-15T22:07:51Z', '2026-10-16T01:38:50Z', 1185, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (230, 'Spot check batch-8 (120) done?', 'answered', '["done"]', '`decision 228: batch-9: health stop, ack?`: the extended spot check of batch-8, 120 questions.

- Run `trivia-authoring`, open http://127.0.0.1:8001/review?batch=batch-8&sample=1
- 120 questions (batch-8''s `size` in `authoring/data/spot-checks.json`); the first 30 are already decided (`decision 215: Spot check batch-8 done?`), 90 to go (~40 min).
- Blind as before; mark the problem kinds in the feedback box.
- The audit now covers all 120 (60 extra, opus, $2.67): `authoring/reports/batch-8.md` "## Audit" (7 major among 115 approved, mostly giveaway hints).
- Then `task 1191: Extended review of batch-8: 120-question spot check (decision 228)` runs `qgen report --qc batch-8` and proposes changes.', '', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15T22:13:23Z', '2026-10-15T23:05:45Z', 1191, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-15, task 1191: all 120 of batch-8 spot-checked; qgen report --qc batch-8 counts 120 (agreement 108/120, 90 %, release bar ✓); authoring/reports/batch-8.md QC section rewritten; proposals in decision 231: QC batch-8 (120): which changes?');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (231, 'QC batch-8 (120): which changes?', 'answered', '["recommended set: A, B, C, D", "all: A–E", "none"]', '`task 1191: Extended review of batch-8: 120-question spot check (decision 228)`: `qgen report --qc batch-8` with all 120 (`authoring/reports/batch-8.md` "## Quality control").

## Numbers
- Agreement with the LLM review: 108/120 (90 %) ✓; release bar ✓
- False approve 9 (8 %), false needs_work 3 (2 %); difficulty |Δ| ≥ 2: 6 (4 of them rated too low)
- Audit/human agreement 105/120 (88 %), below the review''s 90 %: no "audit as a gate" proposal (QC.md step 4 trend rule)
- Audit majors on approved: 7; you approved 6 of them (q-1316, q-1378, q-1531, q-1774, q-2033, q-2199), only q-1868 not

## Proposals (prompts only unless said; each a task in `plan 17: Batch runs` at 650)
- **A. No "Tres pistas:" label** (q-1581 "should be purged", q-1973 rejected; both open with it). `house-style.md`: the question never announces "Tres pistas:"/"Pistas:" (clashes with the Soplo hints, count often wrong); `three_facts` stays, written as plain facts. `review.md` reason 6/style: such a label → needs_work, `fix` drops it. `audit.md` check 7: retired style.
- **B. Strip the label from the pool** (with A): ~20 approved `base` questions open with "Tres pistas…"/"Pistas:" (batch-7: q-0904, q-0852, q-0944, …; batch-8: q-1577, q-1747, q-1750). Edit the question text by hand (drop the label, minimal rewording), `qgen validate`, `qgen export`; translations follow the usual stale mechanism.
- **C. Media must show what the question names** (q-1897 rover instead of the Blue Marble, q-1246 a Yucatán note instead of a peso bill, q-1331 landscape instead of rock art, audit q-2199 boutonniere instead of an ascot). `house-style.md` media + `review.md` media pick: when the question names something showable that isn''t the answer (a photo, an artwork, a banknote, "como esta"), the query and the picked image show that very thing; a merely nearby subject → another candidate or needs_work (media).
- **D. Audit: hint giveaways are minor** (6 of 7 audit majors overruled; q-2035: "the first hint gives away the answer, BUT … no real problem"). Hints come only through the Soplo joker. `audit.md` check 3 / severity: hint 1 or 2 narrowing the options is `minor`; `major` only when it names, translates or defines the answer. `review.md`, `house-style.md` unchanged.
- **E. Translatable premise** (not recommended: one case, you were unsure). q-1950: the clue is the Spanish meaning of an English title, which collapses in `en`. `review.md`: a `base` question whose clue is a translation of the answer''s own words → needs_work.

Not proposed (one case each, below QC.md''s threshold of 2): q-1646 overgeneralization ("every bill has a thread"); q-1452/q-2043 false needs_work (no pattern).', '', 'all: A–E', NULL, '2026-10-15T23:08:12Z', '2026-10-16T00:27:58Z', 1191, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1191: tasks in plan 17: task 1248 (A: no clue label, prompts/axes), task 1249 (B: strip the label from 21 approved base questions, by hand), task 1250 (C: media shows what the question names), task 1251 (D: audit hint giveaways minor unless they name the answer), task 1252 (E: no clue that translates the answer''s own words); authoring/reports/batch-8.md ''Changes from this QC'' lists them');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (232, 'Overlap of English drafts against the es-sourced base pool: which keys?', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 147: English-first batches: calibration, overlap and QC across languages, part 3. overlap_candidates/_overlap_keys/duplicates match normalized Spanish answers and stems, so an English draft barely matches the es pool. The en entries alone depend on milestone 9''s es → en timing; Wikidata ids cover few answers (dates, phrases, concepts) and need a lookup step; an LLM pass per draft costs every batch. A one-time key translation of ~1100 short strings is cheap and independent of that timing.', '', 'English keys per pool question: its en entry where milestone 9 made one, else a cached English answer + concept per es-sourced question (one batched claude call, refreshed for new or changed es questions); no Wikidata ids, no per-draft LLM pass', NULL, '2026-10-16T00:19:36Z', '2026-10-16T00:19:36Z', 1198, NULL, 'rule', 1198, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (233, 'Batch health trend rule across the switch to English drafts?', 'answered', '["Trend only within one source locale: batch-9 has no trend check, batch-10 compares with batch-9 alone, from batch-11 the mean of the previous two en batches; absolute thresholds always", "Trend across the switch as today (batch-9 vs. the mean of batch-7/8)", "No trend rule for batch-9 and batch-10; from batch-11 as today"]', '`plan 147: English-first batches: calibration, overlap and QC across languages`, part 4. `decision 13: Batch health thresholds and stop rule?` stops the next `qgen batch` when a batch fails an absolute threshold or the trend rule: no metric worse than the mean of the previous two batches by more than half its threshold''s margin (`authoring/tools/health.py`, `targets.py`). batch-9 is the first `en`-sourced batch: its drop, needs_work, overlap, calibration and fun numbers come from a new pipeline, not from drift, and batch-7/8 are es batches. The absolute thresholds stay either way. The answer decides how batch-9''s and batch-10''s health is read (task 1186: QC batch-9, and the stop before batch-10).', '', 'Trend across the switch as today (batch-9 vs. the mean of batch-7/8)', NULL, '2026-10-16T00:23:31Z', '2026-10-16T00:26:19Z', 1199, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1207: no code change to the check: decision 13 has no trend stop (trends side by side only), and batch-9''s report table already shows batch-7/8; authoring/tools/health.py health_section now names the compared batches and decision 233; batches.md already said so (task 1206).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (234, 'Spot-check size for batch-9 and batch-10 (first English batches)?', 'answered', '["batch-9: 120, batch-10: 60, then 30 again if both reach ≥ 90 %", "120 for both", "60 for both", "30 (decision 2 unchanged)"]', '`plan 147: English-first batches: calibration, overlap and QC across languages`, part 5. `decision 2: When is batch quality "fine": human spot-check size and agreement rate with the LLM review?` sets 30 per batch, ≥ 90 % agreement; batch-8 got 120 (`decision 228: batch-9: health stop, ack?`; 108/120 = 90 %, decision 230). batch-9 is the first batch drafted in English with a new chain (drafts, rating, review in English). The size is per run (`spot-checks.json` "size", `spot_check_size()` in `qgen.py`) and also sets how many questions the batch''s audit reads (`audit_size()`), so it must be known before batch-9 runs. 30 take ~10–15 min, 120 ~45–60 min.', '', '30 (decision 2 unchanged)', NULL, '2026-10-16T00:23:31Z', '2026-10-16T00:26:51Z', 1199, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1208: 30 is the default (`quality.spot_check_size`), so `spot_check_size("batch-9")`/`("batch-10")` already return 30 and `audit_size()` 60 before the runs; no pre-run size option or spot-checks.json entry needed, no code change. task 1186: QC batch-9 phase 1 now cites decision 234 directly (its grep "spot check" missed "Spot-check"); batch-10''s QC task inherits the body.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (235, 'Source locale per question and translations from either source (English-first base)', 'answered', '[]', 'Milestone 9 ''English-first drafting for base'' (developer, 2026-10-15). Keeps D-81''s one-source-file layout and shared ids (seen counters, flags, media stay per id); the family''s es pool keeps its es-sourced questions as they are (D-59) and gains es translations of en sources. Alternative rejected: a second source file per source locale (two readers for every step). Plan: English-first drafting for base (milestone 9).', '', 'questions.json holds every source question; field locale (absent = es) names its language: base from batch-9 en, base ≤ batch-8 es, regional bundles their region''s language. questions.<L>.json holds the translations into L of sources whose locale ≠ L (new: questions.es.json, questions.es-ES.json); ids shared. pool for L = approved sources with locale L + playing translations into L (pool.json = es, plus pool.es-ES.json). A flag in the source''s locale lands on the source, else on that locale''s translation.', NULL, '2026-10-16T00:29:24Z', '2026-10-16T00:29:24Z', 1217, NULL, 'rule', 1217, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (236, 'English-first drafting for base: what drives drafting and which locales steer it', 'answered', '[]', 'Milestone 9, section ''English-first drafting for base'' (developer, 2026-10-15): «from batch-9 on, qgen batch for base drafts in English: en is the source locale of every new base question»; «target: 5000 approved per question locale (decision 21) for en, es, es-ES, de; English drafting aims at the locales'' deficits»; «replaces: decision 27 for base (es-419 comes from translation, not from Spanish drafts); decision 37''s last clause and D-84 (the en/de source shortfall driving es drafting); decision 219 (DRIVES_DRAFTING: English is the source, no locale steers es drafting)». Source locale per question: `decision 235: Source locale per question and translations from either source (English-first base)`. Implemented by `plan 148: English-first drafting for base` (targets: task 1236). plans/decisions.md D-107.', '', 'base drafts in English from batch-9 on; the launch question locales en, es, es-ES, de steer English drafting by their deficits (draft gap per level = largest over the four of deficit / yield − approved translatable sources not yet in that locale); es drafting for base stops, so no en/de source shortfall drives es drafting and translations.DRIVES_DRAFTING goes; milestone 12''s locales (zh-Hans, ru, pt-BR) steer only by plan 60''s own decision later; regional bundles keep their own deficit (D-101)', NULL, '2026-10-16T00:45:53Z', '2026-10-16T00:45:53Z', 1231, NULL, 'rule', 1231, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (237, 'Supersede decision 27: Spanish variety (OQ-31): which Spanish does base speak, and which Steam store languages to tick? by English-first drafting for base?', 'answered', '["Supersede decision 27 for base as the milestone says: es-419 from translation of English sources (es-sourced ≤ batch-8 stay), es idiomatic Latin American, es-ES its own question pool; colombia and the store languages unchanged", "Keep decision 27 as is (the milestone''s section changes instead)"]', 'Decision 27 (your answer, 2026-10-12): base in neutral Latin American Spanish (es-419), es-ES a variant only where the wording differs. Milestone 9, section ''English-first drafting for base'' (you, 2026-10-15), replaces it for base: «replaces: decision 27 for base (es-419 comes from translation, not from Spanish drafts)»; «two Spanish question locales, like the UI: es (es-419) and es-ES, each its own pool»; «es is idiomatic Latin American Spanish, no longer a compromise with Spain»; «es-ES is Spain''s Spanish». Workers may supersede only a worker''s choice, so this needs your answer. Unchanged: colombia keeps its Colombian voice; Steam store languages Spanish – Latin America and Spain. `plan 148: English-first drafting for base` goes ahead on the milestone''s text; no task waits for this. Related: `decision 236: English-first drafting for base: what drives drafting and which locales steer it`, `decision 235: Source locale per question and translations from either source (English-first base)`, plans/decisions.md D-107.', '', 'Supersede decision 27 for base as the milestone says: es-419 from translation of English sources (es-sourced ≤ batch-8 stay), es idiomatic Latin American, es-ES its own question pool; colombia and the store languages unchanged', NULL, '2026-10-16T00:46:00Z', '2026-10-16T00:49:56Z', 1231, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (238, 'Draft gap for the drafting locale itself: 1:1, not deficit / yield', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 236: English-first drafting for base: what drives drafting and which locales steer it gives one formula for all four locales. An approved en draft counts 1:1 toward en''s deficit (no translation step), while es sources reach en only through es → en translation at en''s yield; dividing en''s deficit by the es → en yield would over-ask drafts. Both coincide while yields are 1.0 (no pilots yet). targets.locale_gap, D-107, task 1236.', '', 'for en (base''s drafting locale) the gap is deficit − untranslated × en yield; the other three locales keep decision 236''s deficit / yield − untranslated', NULL, '2026-10-16T01:13:26Z', '2026-10-16T01:13:26Z', 1236, NULL, 'rule', 1236, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (239, 'English-first drafting prompts: apply?', 'answered', '["apply", "apply with changes (list them)", "keep"]', '`task 1238: English-first prompt drafts for the developer` (`plan 148: English-first drafting for base`, part 8; milestone 9 "English-first drafting for `base`"). From batch-9 on, `base` drafts in English (`task 1234: qgen batch drafts en for base: the whole chain on the question''s locale`); every step reads `house-style.md` + `locale-<L>.md` of the question''s own locale, and the translation prompts already get `{source_language}` filled (`qgen.py` `prompt(…, source_locale=)`, task 1235). Today the prompts still assume Spanish text and Spanish sources. Below: each change as verbatim old → new, in `authoring/tools/prompts/`. Nothing applied yet.

Summary:
- `house-style.md`: language-neutral; examples in English; new section "International angle" (the one place for the rule; drafting, rating, review, audit, revise read it through the house style; `task 1203: International angle in the concept prompts` refers to it).
- `locale-en.md`: a drafting voice (decision 167''s wording kept); "where this house style says Spanish" gone.
- `locale-es.md`: idiomatic Latin American Spanish, no "understood … in Spain"; Colombian references only for `colombia`.
- New `locale-es-ES.md`: Spain''s Spanish, `tú`, Spain''s words and titles (`prompt()` already picks `locale-es-ES.md` once it exists, else `locale-es.md`).
- `locale-de.md`: "the Spanish"/"than Spanish" → the source; "where this house style says Spanish" gone.
- `draft.md`, `rate.md`, `factcheck.md`, `review.md`, `audit.md`: Spanish examples and Spanish-only rules → per locale. `revise.md`: no Spanish assumption, unchanged.
- `neutralize.md`: Spain-oriented rules gone (no "understood in Spain" compromise words); only on es-sourced `base` questions.
- `translate.md`, `translate-review.md`, `translate-region.md`: from `{source_language}`; travel reason `about_spanish` → `about_language` (code: `TRAVEL_REASONS` in `qgen.py`; no stored entry uses it today).
- Not here: the calibration examples (house style "Difficulty", `task 1202: International calibration examples for the 1–15 scale`), the concept prompts (task 1203), `base.rule` / `base.voice` in `app/data/bundles.json` (`task 1245: base bundle rule and voice for English-first drafting`; `locale-es.md`''s `{voices}` reads `base.voice`, so its "and in Spain" goes with 1245), `tighten.md`, `bundles.md` (Spanish examples only illustrate).
- One judgement call to check: `review.md` `feedback` "(Spanish, …)" → English, like `fix`, `reasons` and `notes`; say "keep Spanish" under "apply with changes" if you read feedback better in Spanish.

---

## 1. `house-style.md`

### 1a. Use the four options honestly
old:
```
- A superlative or comparison asks about the **real world**: "¿Cuál es el dinosaurio más largo que ha existido?", and the answer is the real record holder. Never "¿Cuál de estos es el más largo?", where the answer is only the biggest among the four options: the options are not a meaningful set, and the question teaches nothing.
- The same goes for "¿Cuál de estas ciudades está a mayor altura?": ask "¿Cuál es la capital departamental más alta de Colombia?" instead.
- The clue in the question must point to the answer **in the real world**, not just among the four options. Before writing, ask: "What else in the world fits this clue?" If anything does, use a clue that only the answer has.
  - Bad: "¿Qué ritmo del Caribe colombiano se baila con velas en la mano?" Other dances use candles too.
  - Bad: "¿En qué deporte se usa una raqueta y una pelota amarilla?" Pádel and frontenis do too, and tennis doesn''t depend on the ball''s colour.
```
new:
```
- A superlative or comparison asks about the **real world**: "What is the longest dinosaur that ever lived?", and the answer is the real record holder. Never "Which of these is the longest?", where the answer is only the biggest among the four options: the options are not a meaningful set, and the question teaches nothing.
- The same goes for "Which of these mountains is the highest?": ask "What is the highest mountain in Africa?" instead.
- The clue in the question must point to the answer **in the real world**, not just among the four options. Before writing, ask: "What else in the world fits this clue?" If anything does, use a clue that only the answer has.
  - Bad: "Which dance is performed with candles in the dancers'' hands?" Many dances use candles.
  - Bad: "Which sport is played with a racket and a yellow ball?" Padel and squash do too, and tennis doesn''t depend on the ball''s colour.
```

### 1b. Keep it short
old:
```
- It asks exactly one thing. A rhetorical question or thought inside a riddle goes in quotes, so players don''t hear two questions: "Me preguntaron «¿Con qué grano dejé de merecer mi nombre?». ¿Qué paradoja soy?"
```
new:
```
- It asks exactly one thing. A rhetorical question or thought inside a riddle goes in quotes, so players don''t hear two questions: "The Sphinx asked “What walks on four legs in the morning, two at noon and three in the evening?”. Who solved the riddle?"
```
old:
```
- No invented story around the question: no relatives, friends, guides or influencers telling you something ("Tu tío…", "Tu prima te reta…", "Un amigo…"), no "Imagina que…", no backstory. A situation ("Estás en la cocina y…") only when it is the clue itself, in one short clause.
```
new:
```
- No invented story around the question: no relatives, friends, guides or influencers telling you something ("Your uncle…", "Your cousin dares you…", "A friend…"), no "Imagine that…", no backstory. A situation ("You''re in the kitchen and…") only when it is the clue itself, in one short clause.
```

### 1c. New section, after "## Keep it short" (before "## No giveaways")
new:
```
## International angle
A `base` question is played in every language of the game (English, Spanish for Latin America and for Spain, German, more later), by families anywhere. Write it so it travels:
- No focus on the Spanish-speaking world: Latin American or Spanish places, people, food and customs only in the share any other region gets, and only when families everywhere know them.
- No Anglo or US bias: no US or UK school knowledge, holidays, money, sports leagues, TV or brands treated as everyone''s; the world is the default, not one country.
- Spread places, people, customs, food, music and history across the world; a famous fact from anywhere is welcome.
- No question that lives on one language: no wordplay, pun, rhyme or sound joke, and no question about one language''s spelling, plurals, letters or words, unless it works the same in every language.
- A regional bundle (e.g. `colombia`) follows its own rule (under "Bundle") instead.
```

### 1d. Description
old:
```
Every question has a `description`: one full, humorous Spanish sentence shown *before* the question, instead of the category, in the style of "You Don''t Know Jack". It relates to the question''s content, can be absurd, and must not give the answer away or rule out options (e.g. don''t say "un nevado" when only two options are nevados). Examples from the pool:
- Café: "Los papás no funcionan por la mañana hasta que les echas este combustible."
- Capybara (chigüiro): "Un ratón al que se le fue la mano en el almuerzo."
- Light bulb: "Una idea tan buena que hasta tiene forma de idea."
- Saturno: "Un planeta que nunca se quita sus joyas."
- Año luz: "Una pregunta engañosa, como cuando tu mamá dice ''ya casi llegamos''."
- Hipérbole: "Esta es la mejor pregunta de la historia del universo, sin exagerar."
```
new:
```
Every question has a `description`: one full, humorous sentence in the question''s language, shown *before* the question, instead of the category, in the style of "You Don''t Know Jack". It relates to the question''s content, can be absurd, and must not give the answer away or rule out options (e.g. don''t say "a volcano" when only two options are volcanoes). Examples from the pool, in English:
- Coffee: "Parents don''t start in the morning until you fill them up with this fuel."
- Capybara: "A mouse that went a little overboard at lunch."
- Light bulb: "An idea so good it''s even shaped like one."
- Saturn: "A planet that never takes off its jewelry."
- Light-year: "A trick question, like when your mom says ''we''re almost there''."
- Hyperbole: "This is the best question in the history of the universe, no exaggeration."
```

### 1e. Media
old:
```
Examples: "¿Quién escribió Don Quijote?" → "Consuegra windmills"; "¿Cuál es la capital de Australia?" → "kangaroo" (Australia, but no city that could be an option).
```
new:
```
Examples: "Who wrote Don Quixote?" → "Consuegra windmills"; "What is the capital of Australia?" → "kangaroo" (Australia, but no city that could be an option).
```
old:
```
(e.g. "¿Qué planeta tiene anillos enormes?": any space picture might show Saturn).
  - "essential": the media is part of the question, e.g. "Escucha: ¿qué instrumento suena?".
```
new:
```
(e.g. "Which planet has huge rings?": any space picture might show Saturn).
  - "essential": the media is part of the question, e.g. "Listen: which instrument is this?".
```
old:
```
Not for rare or regional objects (e.g. a gaita),
```
new:
```
Not for rare or regional objects (e.g. a Colombian gaita flute),
```
old:
```
(a question about a Colombian fruit → "Colombian fruit market"; about a Greek god → "Greek temple ruins")
```
new:
```
(a question about a tropical fruit → "tropical fruit market"; about a Greek god → "Greek temple ruins")
```

### 1f. Fun fact
old:
```
`fun_fact`: one short Spanish sentence shown after the answer is revealed. True, surprising, family-friendly.
```
new:
```
`fun_fact`: one short sentence in the question''s language, shown after the answer is revealed. True, surprising, family-friendly.
```

### 1g. Difficulty
Unchanged here: the calibration examples are `task 1202: International calibration examples for the 1–15 scale`''s.

## 2. `locale-en.md` (drafting voice and translation target)

old (whole file):
```
- All content is in English.
- US spelling ("color", "center"). Words all English speakers understand; where US and UK English differ ("soccer"/"football", "fries"/"chips", "cookie"/"biscuit"), reword around it if that stays natural, else the US word.
- Units: metric first; add imperial in brackets in the question text and fun fact where English speakers think in it ("8849 m (29,032 ft)", "30 °C (86 °F)"). The four options use one unit, all in the same form.
- Numbers: comma as thousands separator from 1,000 ("42,195 people"), decimal point ("42.195 km"), years without separator; large round numbers in words ("3 million"). Dates: "July 20, 1969".
- Names and titles as English speakers know them: "Christopher Columbus", "Joan of Arc", the English title of a film or book ("Inside Out", "One Hundred Years of Solitude"). A name without an English form stays as it is.
- Address (UI style guide `en`): "you", for one player or the whole family; never "you all", "you guys", "yourselves".
- Quotes in the text fields: “…” (curly double quotes).
- Watch "a"/"an": an article in the question must fit all four options.
- Difficulty and distractors on the same scale 1–15 (the calibration examples are about Spanish speakers, the scale isn''t).
- Where this house style says Spanish (the description, the fun fact), read English. Its examples stay in Spanish: they show the style, not the language.
```
new (whole file):
```
- All content is in English, whether you write the question or translate it.
- Plain, natural English that every English speaker reads easily: short common words, no slang, no idioms of one country.
- US spelling ("color", "center"). Words all English speakers understand; where US and UK English differ ("soccer"/"football", "fries"/"chips", "cookie"/"biscuit"), reword around it if that stays natural, else the US word. US spelling is about the words, not the topics (house style "International angle").
- Units: metric first; add imperial in brackets in the question text and fun fact where English speakers think in it ("8849 m (29,032 ft)", "30 °C (86 °F)"). The four options use one unit, all in the same form.
- Numbers: comma as thousands separator from 1,000 ("42,195 people"), decimal point ("42.195 km"), years without separator; large round numbers in words ("3 million"). Dates: "July 20, 1969".
- Names and titles as English speakers know them: "Christopher Columbus", "Joan of Arc", the English title of a film or book ("Inside Out", "One Hundred Years of Solitude"). A name without an English form stays as it is.
- Address (UI style guide `en`): "you", for one player or the whole family; never "you all", "you guys", "yourselves".
- Quotes in the text fields: “…” (curly double quotes).
- Watch "a"/"an": an article in the question must fit all four options.
- Difficulty and distractors on the same scale 1–15 (the calibration examples are about Spanish speakers, the scale isn''t).
```
(The difficulty bullet keeps decision 167''s wording; task 1202 updates its parenthesis with the examples.)

## 3. `locale-es.md` (idiomatic Latin American Spanish)

old (whole file):
```
- All content is in Spanish. The voice depends on the question''s bundle:
{voices}
- Neutral means: a word understood across Latin America and in Spain where one exists ("bombilla", "palomitas", "gripe", "portero", "café"); no regional diminutives ("pelota", not "pelotica"); no word, brand-as-generic or usage known in one country only, Spain included ("cinta adhesiva", not "cinta Scotch"; "computadora", not "ordenador"). Where no word is shared ("carro"/"coche"/"auto", "celular"/"móvil"), reword around it if that stays natural ("vehículo", "teléfono"); else take the most widespread Latin American form.
- Address: one generic "tú" where a question speaks to the players ("Escucha: …", "si estornudas…"), as the pool does; never "vos", "vosotros", "usted". (The UI''s "ustedes" is for the game''s own prompts, not question texts.)
- Use Colombian references where they fit naturally (places, food, music, sport, people, Encanto), but don''t force them. Each batch writes for one bundle (D-41): a `base` question may use Colombian references anyone in the Spanish-speaking world knows, but never needs a connection to Colombia to answer; that is the `colombia` bundle''s job. A neutral voice is about the words, not the topics.
- Numbers (all bundles): no thousands separator up to 9999 ("2640 metros"), a space from 10 000 ("42 195 personas"), a decimal comma ("42,195 km"); large round numbers in words ("3 millones").
```
new (whole file):
```
- All content is in Spanish, whether you write the question or translate it. The voice depends on the question''s bundle:
{voices}
- `base` is idiomatic Latin American Spanish: natural words a family anywhere in Latin America uses ("computadora", "jugo", "papa", "celular", "palomitas", "lentes"). It doesn''t need to read as Spain''s Spanish: Spain gets its own `es-ES` text. No Spain-only word or usage ("ordenador", "zumo", "patata", "móvil", "vale"), no regional diminutives ("pelota", not "pelotica"), no word, brand-as-generic or usage known in one Latin American country only ("cinta adhesiva", not "cinta Scotch"; "gripe", not "gripa"). Where Latin American countries differ ("carro"/"auto", "piscina"/"alberca"/"pileta"), reword around it if that stays natural ("vehículo"); else take the most widespread Latin American form.
- Names and titles as Latin American Spanish speakers know them: "Cristóbal Colón", "Juana de Arco", the Latin American release title of a film or book ("Intensa-Mente", "Buscando a Nemo"). A name without a Spanish form stays as it is.
- Address: one generic "tú" where a question speaks to the players ("Escucha: …", "si estornudas…"), as the pool does; never "vos", "vosotros", "usted". (The UI''s "ustedes" is for the game''s own prompts, not question texts.)
- `colombia` only: Colombian references where they fit naturally (places, food, music, sport, people, Encanto); don''t force them. A `base` question never needs a connection to Colombia or any one country to answer (house style "International angle"); a Colombian voice is the `colombia` bundle''s.
- Units: metric only. Numbers (all bundles): no thousands separator up to 9999 ("2640 metros"), a space from 10 000 ("42 195 personas"), a decimal comma ("42,195 km"); large round numbers in words ("3 millones"). Dates: "20 de julio de 1969".
- Articles and gender (el/la, un/una) must fit all four options.
- Difficulty and distractors on the same scale 1–15.
```

## 4. New `locale-es-ES.md` (Spain''s Spanish)

new (whole file):
```
- All content is in Spain''s Spanish (castellano de España), whether you adapt a Spanish question from Latin America or translate one: the words, spelling and usage a family in Spain uses.
- Spain''s common words: "ordenador", "móvil", "coche", "zumo", "patata", "gafas", "piscina", "palomitas", "vídeo". No word known in one part of Spain only (Canarian "guagua", Andalusian or Catalan-only words), no slang, no brand-as-generic ("cinta adhesiva", not "celo"). Latin American words where Spain has its own ("computadora", "jugo", "carro", "celular", "lentes") become Spain''s.
- Names and titles as they are known in Spain: "Cristóbal Colón", "Juana de Arco", the Spanish release title of a film or book in Spain ("Del revés" for "Inside Out", "Buscando a Nemo"). A name without a Spanish form stays as it is.
- Address: one generic "tú" where a question speaks to the players ("Escucha: …", "si estornudas…"); never "vosotros", "usted", "vos". (The UI''s "vosotros" is for the game''s own prompts, not question texts.)
- Quotes in the text fields: «…» (angle quotes).
- Units: metric only. Numbers: no thousands separator up to 9999 ("2640 metros"), a space from 10 000 ("42 195 personas"), a decimal comma ("42,195 km"); large round numbers in words ("3 millones"). Dates: "20 de julio de 1969".
- Articles and gender (el/la, un/una) must fit all four options.
- Difficulty and distractors on the same scale 1–15.
```

## 5. `locale-de.md`

old:
```
