INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (240, 'base bundle rule and voice for English-first drafting: apply?', 'answered', '["apply", "apply with changes (list them)", "keep"]', '`task 1245: base bundle rule and voice for English-first drafting` (`plan 148: English-first drafting for base`; milestone 9 "English-first drafting for `base`": international angle, `es` idiomatic Latin American). Companion of `decision 239: English-first drafting prompts: apply?` (prompt files); this one covers `app/data/bundles.json`, which isn''t a prompt file. Nothing applied yet.

Where they are read (`authoring/tools/qgen.py`):
- `rule`: `bundle_text()` puts it into every draft call''s user message ("Every question you write is in the `base` bundle: …"); `bundles_text()` feeds `qgen bundles` (membership, with `house-style` + `bundles.md`). Not shipped to the game (stripped on export; `pool.test.ts`).
- `voice`: `bundle_text()` for Spanish drafts only (base now drafts en, so: none); `voices_text()` fills `{voices}` in `prompts/locale-es.md`, read by every `es` step (translate en → es, rate, factcheck, review on `es`, `neutralize`). `locale-es-ES.md` (decision 239 draft) has no `{voices}`.

## 1. `base.rule` (`app/data/bundles.json`)
old:
```
Everything that isn''t in another bundle: answerable by the players anywhere in the Spanish-speaking world, including well-known facts about Colombia (its capital, its flag, coffee, García Márquez).
```
new:
```
Everything that isn''t in another bundle: answerable by families anywhere in the world, whatever their language; well-known facts about any country belong here (Colombia''s capital, Japan''s flag, Italian pasta, García Márquez), knowledge only one country''s or one language''s players have doesn''t.
```
- Self-contained (no reference to the house style''s "International angle"), so `qgen bundles` reads the same rule with or without decision 239.
- Boundary to `colombia` kept: famous facts about Colombia stay `base`; `colombia`''s rule ("…that the players outside Colombia mostly wouldn''t know") unchanged.
- es-sourced questions (≤ batch-8) stay as they are (`decision 237: Supersede decision 27…`); the new rule only steers new drafts and future `qgen bundles` runs (where "When in doubt, choose `base`" still holds).

## 2. `base.voice` (`app/data/bundles.json`)
old:
```
neutral Latin American Spanish (es-419): words understood across Latin America and in Spain
```
new:
```
idiomatic Latin American Spanish (es-419): words a family anywhere in Latin America uses; Spain has its own es-ES text
```
- Matches decision 239''s draft of `locale-es.md` (its `base` bullet says the same at length).

## 3. Fallback voice in `qgen.py` (`VOICE`, for a bundle without `voice`)
old:
```
VOICE = "neutral Latin American Spanish (es-419)"
```
new:
```
VOICE = "idiomatic Latin American Spanish (es-419)"
```

## Applying (task 1239, together with decision 239)
- The three edits above; `qgen validate`, `npm test` (bundles.json shape unchanged).
- `rg` "Spanish-speaking world", "understood across Latin America and in Spain", "neutral Latin American Spanish" over `.md` and fix the hits that describe `base` (decision 200).', '', 'apply', NULL, '2026-10-16T01:25:13Z', '2026-10-16T01:40:16Z', 1245, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1239: Applied by task 1239 (2026-10-15): base.rule and base.voice in app/data/bundles.json, VOICE in qgen.py as drafted; plans/22-bundles.md''s base row and house-style bullet follow. plans/15-i18n.md''s "neutral Latin American Spanish" left to task 1240: Docs for English-first drafting; gate for batch-9.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (241, 'Calibration examples for an international English pool: apply?', 'answered', '["apply", "apply with changes (list them)", "keep"]', '`task 1202: International calibration examples for the 1–15 scale` (`plan 147: English-first batches: calibration, overlap and QC across languages`, part 1). From batch-9 on, `base` is drafted in English for families anywhere; the house style''s difficulty examples still assume Colombian/Spanish-speaking teens (empanada, Cartagena, Tunja, «lápiz», Cervantes, García Márquez). `decision 239: English-first drafting prompts: apply?` leaves the house style''s "Difficulty" section and the locale files'' difficulty bullet to this task; this draft applies on top of it (after `task 1239: Apply the English-first prompts`). Nothing applied yet.

Summary:
- Scale, "who can answer it" rules, "Hard questions" rules, anchors 9–15 (`decision 11: Difficulty anchors 9–15: are these levels right?`): unchanged, except names in English spelling.
- Calibration lists: examples in English; the international ones kept with their observed guesses (trumpet, WALL·E, microwave, horse/neigh analogy, antonym, ants, stegosaurus, Link, Pac-Man, Greek gods, Saturn, Columbus, the seven notes).
- Six local examples replaced by international analogues that teach the same lesson; they **keep the old example''s guess** (the lesson: "AI said N, teens showed M"), they are not new observations:
  - 1: Colombian food photo → empanada (guessed 4) → everyday food photo → pizza (guessed 4)
  - 6: Cartagena''s clock tower photo (guessed 4); walls against pirates (guessed 5) → Sydney Opera House from a photo, which city (guessed 4); what the Great Wall of China was built against (guessed 5)
  - 9: highest Colombian city → Tunja (guessed 3) → in which country Kilimanjaro stands → Tanzania (guessed 3)
  - 2: plural of «lápiz» (guessed 5) → in which country the Eiffel Tower stands → France (guessed 5) (a plural question breaks decision 239''s "International angle")
  - 5: who wrote Don Quijote → Cervantes (guessed 8) → who wrote "Romeo and Juliet" → Shakespeare (guessed 8)
  - 7: Colombian Nobel 1982 → García Márquez (lowered to 3) → the only person with Nobel Prizes in two sciences → Marie Curie (lowered to 3)
- New: a level-4 pool example (only level without one: q-1655 jazz), and one rule line: one country''s home knowledge is not easy for the others.
- `locale-en.md`, `locale-de.md`: the difficulty bullet loses "(the calibration examples are about Spanish speakers, the scale isn''t)", like decision 239''s `locale-es.md` / `locale-es-ES.md`.
- `draft.md`, `rate.md`, `revise.md`, `audit.md`: no change; they point to the house style''s calibration, and no calibration line in them assumes Spanish speakers (decision 239 covers their Spanish examples).
- Not here: Bonmatí (anchor 12) stays: a world champion and Ballon d''Or, confirmed in decision 11; say "replace" under "apply with changes" if you want a non-Spanish anchor. `concepts-list.md`''s "(Sol)" is `task 1203: International angle in the concept prompts`''.
- Judgement to check: the analogues'' levels (pizza 1, Sydney Opera House 6, Great Wall 6, Kilimanjaro 9, Eiffel Tower 2, Shakespeare 5, Marie Curie 7) are mine for an international family; correct any under "apply with changes".

