Options: "apply" applies 1–4 as drafted (task 1316); "apply with changes": say which.', '', 'apply', NULL, '2026-10-16T14:03:54Z', '2026-10-16T14:16:06Z', 1315, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1316: Applied 1–4 as drafted (task 1316): taxonomy.md, taxonomy-names.md, remap.md, regional-categories.json; qgen''s guard that waited for them removed. Checked on a scratch copy: fake en region ireland, qgen taxonomy → 14 categories, 54 subcategories with slugs + en names; --apply wrote categories.json without names and names.{en,es,de}.json (claude: opus 1 call $0.48, sonnet 1 call $0.06, total $0.54).

2026-10-16, task 1275: Task 1275 (last waiter) redrafted the locale prompts on the slug taxonomy prompts: no further changes to the taxonomy prompts; regional taxonomy proposals use slugs + en names for en and de regions (checked on scratch bundles).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (263, 'Bundle screen reviewed in /gallery?', 'answered', '["approved", "problems (listed in the answer)"]', 'Part 3 of `plan 151: Bundle selection on its own full screen` (`task 1340: Gallery run of the bundle screen and developer review`). Shots in `/gallery` (`trivia-authoring`, from `work/gallery/<locale>/`), es, es-ES, en, de, 1920×1080 (no `--size` yet: `task 1330: Gallery size option` open):
- `bundles`: new screen between Player and Choice, both modes (`task 1338: Bundle screen between Player and Choice; Choice without the selector`); title «¿Qué preguntas juega {name}?», large tiles with descriptions, «Atrás» / «Seguir»
- `choice-solo`, `choice-gamemaster`: no bundle selector any more; read-only line «Paquetes de preguntas: …» above the buttons
- `choice-supply-report`: supply report stays on Choice (`decision 260: Bundle screen: where it sits, supply report, in-game settings`)
- base bundle names (`decision 261: Name of the base bundle per UI language (not «Basics»)`): «Paquete básico», «Base bundle», «Grundpaket»; letter keys es P, en B, de G
Worker''s check: no overflow or cut text, 0 unmatched catalog texts in `manifest.json`.', 'Picking which sets of questions to play now has its own full screen, shown before the game options, and the main question set has a clearer name in each language. Before this counts as finished, the developer looks at screenshots of the new screen and the changed options screen in all four languages. The answer is either a go-ahead or a list of problems, each of which becomes its own fix.', 'problems: layout, scrolling, order, country flags and no descriptions, now in milestone 8''s bundle-screen line (2026-10-16 edit); plan 151''s revision covers them, no separate fix tasks', NULL, '2026-10-16T14:53:07Z', '2026-10-16T15:00:15Z', 1340, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1340: Bundle screen redone as two columns (base bundle left, optional bundles right, scrolling with focus follow), names only, no descriptions; optional order: regional bundle of the player''s region, then non-regional, then other regions (task 1351). Regional bundles carry a required ISO `country` (bundles.json, qgen validate, mods, --check-mod, modding docs) and always show that country''s flag as icon, no pin (task 1350). Plan 151 revised instead of separate fix tasks; reviewed again in task 1340.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (264, 'Bundle screen (two columns) reviewed in /gallery?', 'answered', '["approved", "problems (listed in the answer)"]', 'Shots in work/gallery/<locale>/ for es, es-ES, en, de: bundles, choice-solo, choice-gamemaster, choice-supply-report (1920×1080; no unmatched catalog text, no overflow or cut text seen). Changed since decision 263: left column base bundle, right column optional bundles, names only, regional bundle shows its country''s flag, region-first order, scrolling right column with fade. Not covered by the gallery: ~15 fake mod bundles (it cannot load a mods dir) and 1280×800 (no --size option yet); scrolling is only checked on the dev server per task 1351.', 'The screen where players choose which question bundles to play was rebuilt after your last review: two columns, country flags, and a list that scrolls when there are many bundles. Please look at the new screenshots and say whether it is good enough or what still needs fixing. Approving closes the earlier gallery flag on this screen.', 'problems: base bundle info only (no «Elijan uno»), narrow, vertical ruler; optional bundles fixed-width tiles flowing in rows and columns; obvious hover and selected states; counts «5000+» / «2000+»; flow Player → Choice («Seguir») → Bundles → Level, supply report on the bundle screen; all in milestone 8''s bundle-screen line (2026-10-16 edit), plan 151''s revision covers them', NULL, '2026-10-16T15:34:10Z', '2026-10-16T16:03:11Z', 1340, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-17, task 1340: 2026-10-16, task 1340: Flow Player → Choice («Seguir», no bundle line, no supply) → Bundles («¡A jugar!») → Level in both modes; supply report, disabled start and running-out notice on Bundles (task 1360). Narrow base column with toggle tiles, vertical ruler, flowing fixed-width tiles with scroll and focus follow, clear hover and selected states, counts «5000+» / «2000+» (task 1361). Any bundle combination: no base_bundle_count, base may be off, start disabled with a reason when the selection has no questions (task 1379). Reviewed again in the next review decision.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (265, 'Start horizon: approve the image picks?', 'answered', '["Approve all", "Approve with changes (list numbers to drop or replace)"]', 'Contact sheet: `work/horizon/contact-sheet.png` (16 tiles on `--night-800`, number + name). Originals: `work/horizon/candidates/NN-<slug>.svg`; the slug becomes the shipped `<slug>.webp`.

All 16 are CC0 or PD vector cut-outs (SVG, so real alpha) from Wikimedia Commons, mostly Open Clip Art imports. No photos: Commons has almost no usable photo cut-outs with alpha for these subjects. Style is flat clipart, not photographic; the plan said "natural colours", the vectors are the usable reading of it. All File pages: `https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:<title>`.

| # | Slug | File title | Author | Licence | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | eiffel-tower | Landmark in city of Paris - Eiffel Tower.svg | shokunin | CC0 | Europe, landmark, tall |
| 2 | toucan | Toucan by gnokii.svg | gnokii | CC0 | S. America, animal, bright colours |
| 3 | royal-palace | Royal Palace Phnom Penh.svg | gnokii | CC0 | Asia, landmark, wide, gold |
| 4 | teapot | Tea pot.svg | donchico | CC0 | object, small |
| 5 | red-panda | Red panda.svg | revzack | CC0 | Asia, animal, warm orange |
| 6 | banjo | Banjo.svg | gnokii | CC0 | N. America, instrument |
| 7 | drum | African Drum 2.svg | Benji Park / Anonymous | CC0 | Africa, instrument, small |
| 8 | penguin | Lemmling-Cartoon-penguin-1.svg | lemmling | CC0 | animal, wide-bodied, high contrast |
| 9 | lighthouse | Lighthouse.svg | matthewg42 | Public domain | landmark, tall, red/white |
| 10 | ukulele | Ukulele.svg | Deluge | CC0 | Oceania (Pacific), instrument, tall |
| 11 | castle | Neuschwanstein Castle 1.svg | gurica | CC0 | Europe, landmark, wide (the only German item) |
| 12 | volcano | Simple erupting volcano.svg | Madison Kingsford | CC0 | nature, orange/red |
| 13 | orca | Orca-matthew-gates-r.svg | Totodu74 | CC0 | animal, wide, all oceans |
| 14 | toy-train | Toy train.svg | gnokii | CC0 | vehicle, small, primary colours |
| 15 | airplane | Plane in the sky mo 01.svg | OpenClipart "mo" | CC0 | vehicle, blue |
| 16 | cactus | Vector cactus illustration (7).svg | Clker-Free-Vector-Images (Pixabay import) | CC0 | N. America, plant, tall, green |

Known gaps (Commons search found nothing usable): no bridge, no bus (the Commons buses are cropped), no kangaroo/koala (Oceania only via the ukulele), no giraffe in colour, Africa only via the drum. 7 is a plain djembe drawing, 4 has a thin string above it. Name replacements by number if you want them; the build task picks them under the same rules.', 'The start screen of the game is getting a colourful row of small illustrations along the bottom, like a skyline: landmarks, animals, instruments and vehicles from around the world. Sixteen free-to-use drawings have been collected and put on one overview picture. You decide whether they go into the game as they are, or whether some should be dropped or swapped first; nothing is built until you answer.', 'i approve all of them, but i can only actually decide when i see the rendered start screen. so please only have me decide on the final start screen and don''t have me decide on anything relating to the choice of pictures before that', NULL, '2026-10-16T15:41:40Z', '2026-10-16T15:44:41Z', 1329, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (266, 'Regional drafting in en, de, es-ES: prompts apply? (after slug keys)', 'answered', '["apply", "apply with changes", "don''t apply"]', '`task 1275: Regional drafting in any launch language (en US/UK, de, es-ES, es)` (`plan 98: Regional packs (RG-3..RG-5)`, part 10). The bundles `usa` (en, US English), `germany` (de), `uk` (en, British English), `spain` (es-ES), `mexico` (es, Mexican Spanish) draft in their region''s language and variety. After `decision 262: Taxonomy prompts on slug keys: apply?` (taxonomy prompts and the general list are applied) this redraft brings back the locale-prompt parts of `decision 250: Regional drafting in en, de, es-ES: prompts apply?` (its parts 4–8), unchanged, as verbatim old → new in `authoring/tools/prompts/`. The old texts below match the files today. Nothing applied yet.

Already in code (staged): `qgen bundle new --kind region --language <L> --voice "…"`; `bundle_text()` gives a bundle''s voice to drafts in its own locale whatever the language; `{voices}` lists the voices of the prompt''s locale only (es: base, colombia, later mexico; en: later usa, uk; de: germany; es-ES: spain); `qgen taxonomy --apply` gives the voice to the names call of its language. `concepts.key()` (concept dedupe) maps «ß» to «ss» and drops «der/die/das» like Spanish articles, so German concepts don''t collapse («Straße» was «strae»).

Throw-away check (scratch copy, removed): a `de` region and an `en` region with a British voice: `qgen taxonomy --bundle` proposed slugs + English names + the bundle''s language names (`germany` 16 categories, 50 subcategories; `uk` 14, 59), `--apply` wrote slug-keyed `categories.json` and `names.en|es|de.json`, concept lists and `qgen draft` wrote German and British text with the right draft locale, `--translate en|es-ES --bundle germany --dry-run` picked a `de` source. The game offers a pack when its `languages` list the save''s language (`pool.ts`): an en-only pack to en, a de pack to de, and to en once `languages` lists both. Cost of the check: about $2.4.

How variety inside one question locale works: **the voice wins over the locale prompt''s spelling/word lines, for the bundle''s own questions only**; no `locale-<L>-<region>.md` files. Every step (draft, rate, review, audit, fix) reads `locale-<L>.md` with its `{voices}`, so a reviewer sees uk''s British voice next to the US line and won''t flag "colour"; translations into en of other bundles (colombia, germany) aren''t listed there and keep US English.

Not changed: `house-style.md`, `draft.md`, `rate.md`, `review.md`, `audit.md`, `translate.md`.

---

## 1. `locale-en.md`
old (first two bullets):
```
- All content is in English, whether you write the question or translate it.
- Plain, natural English that every English speaker reads easily: short common words, no slang, no idioms of one country.
```
new:
```
- All content is in English, whether you write the question or translate it.
- A bundle with a voice below writes its own questions in that variety; for them, the voice wins over this section''s spelling, words, idioms and date format (a British voice: "colour", "football", "biscuit", "20 July 1969"). Every other question, translations into English included, follows this section as written:
{voices}
- Plain, natural English that every English speaker reads easily: short common words, no slang, no idioms of one country.
```

## 2. `locale-es.md`
old:
```
- `colombia` only: Colombian references where they fit naturally (places, food, music, sport, people, Encanto); don''t force them. A `base` question never needs a connection to Colombia or any one country to answer (house style "International angle"); a Colombian voice is the `colombia` bundle''s.
```
new:
```
- A regional bundle (its voice above, e.g. `colombia`): its region''s references where they fit naturally (`colombia`: places, food, music, sport, people, Encanto); don''t force them. For its own questions its voice''s words win over the `base` line above. A `base` question never needs a connection to one country to answer (house style "International angle"); a national voice is a regional bundle''s.
```

## 3. `locale-es-ES.md`
old:
```
- All content is in Spain''s Spanish (castellano de España), whether you adapt a Spanish question from Latin America or translate one: the words, spelling and usage a family in Spain uses.
```
new:
```
- All content is in Spain''s Spanish (castellano de España), whether you write the question, adapt a Spanish question from Latin America or translate one: the words, spelling and usage a family in Spain uses.
- A bundle with a voice below writes its own questions in that voice, with its region''s references where they fit naturally; every other question follows this section:
{voices}
```

## 4. `locale-de.md`
old:
```
- All content is in German: standard German, spelled as in Germany (with "ß").
```
new:
```
- All content is in German, whether you write the question or translate it: standard German, spelled as in Germany (with "ß").
- A bundle with a voice below writes its own questions in that voice, with its region''s references where they fit naturally; every other question follows this section:
{voices}
```
old:
```
- Address (UI style guide `de`): never assume a plural: no "ihr", "euch", "euer", no plural imperatives ("Hört zu", "Schaut"). Where the source speaks to the players ("you", "tú"), use a generic "du" ("Hör zu: …", "wenn du niest") or an impersonal form ("Zu hören ist …"); never "Sie".
```
new:
```
- Address (UI style guide `de`): never assume a plural: no "ihr", "euch", "euer", no plural imperatives ("Hört zu", "Schaut"). Where a question speaks to the players (yours, or the source''s "you", "tú"), use a generic "du" ("Hör zu: …", "wenn du niest") or an impersonal form ("Zu hören ist …"); never "Sie".
```

## 5. `translate-region.md` (any source language; `{source_language}` is filled since plan 148)
old:
```
- **The region''s own things keep their own names:** dishes, festivals, places, people, music, sayings ("arepa", "bandeja paisa", "Feria de las Flores", "vallenato"). Add a short gloss in the target language only where the question can''t be understood without it ("tinto (black coffee)"), never one that gives the answer away.
- **Regional words travel:** a question about a regional Spanish word or saying (what a «tinto» is, what «parcero» means) is asked in the target language about the Spanish word; these players know such words. `about_language` stays for questions on Spanish spelling, grammar or wordplay.
```
new:
```
- **The region''s own things keep their own names:** dishes, festivals, places, people, music, sayings ("arepa", "Feria de las Flores", "vallenato"; "Spätzle", "Schützenfest"). Add a short gloss in the target language only where the question can''t be understood without it ("tinto (black coffee)"), never one that gives the answer away.
- **Regional words travel:** a question about a word or saying of the region (what a «tinto» is, what a „Schultüte“ is) is asked in the target language about the original word; these players know such words. `about_language` stays for questions on the spelling, grammar or wordplay of {source_language}.
```


---
Options: "apply" applies 1–5 as drafted; "apply with changes": say which.', 'The regional question packs for the United States, Germany, the United Kingdom, Spain and Mexico must be written in each region''s own language and variety, for example British rather than American English. This is the wording of the instructions the question writer and translator read to do that: where a regional pack has its own voice, it wins over the general spelling and word rules, and a translation from German or Spanish keeps the region''s own words. Applying it lets those packs be set up; not applying leaves them writing in the general style.', 'apply', NULL, '2026-10-16T16:01:11Z', '2026-10-16T16:38:11Z', 1275, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1275: Applied as drafted: locale-en.md, locale-es.md, locale-es-ES.md, locale-de.md take {voices} and let a bundle''s voice win over the locale''s spelling/word lines for its own questions only; locale-de.md address rule covers drafts; translate-region.md examples and ''regional words'' rule cover de/es-ES sources via {source_language}. Code (bundle new --language, voices per locale, concepts.key ß/der/die/das) was already committed.

2026-10-16, task 1280: Regional drafting prompts applied; mexico was set up with --language es and a Mexican Spanish voice on top of them.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (267, 'Bundle tiles: question count strings', 'answered', '[]', 'Masterplan part 11 leaves ''well away from 2000'' and the rounding to plan 151. Shipped bundles are built to targets.json (base 5000, regional 2000, D-99, D-101), so a fixed string per kind is what players will see at release and needs no runtime count; mods have no target, so ''well away'' is simply ''below the fixed string'', counted from the pool the client already loaded (no new data field, works for every mod). Rounding down keeps «+» honest.', 'Each question set on the bundle screen shows how many questions it holds. The developer asked for round promises rather than live numbers: «5000+» for the general set and «2000+» for every extra set, with player-made sets that are much smaller showing their own number instead. This settles exactly when a set shows its own number and how it is rounded.', 'Our shipped bundles show their target as a fixed catalog string: base «5000+», every other shipped bundle «2000+» (targets.json totals; the release bar, not the tile, makes them true). Mod bundles show their own count: questions of the bundle in the UI language''s pool as loaded, rounded down (≥ 100 to the hundred, ≥ 10 to the ten, else exact) with «+» when rounded; capped at the fixed string of their kind (a mod base bundle with ≥ 5000 «5000+», any other mod bundle with ≥ 2000 «2000+»). Numbers formatted per UI language.', NULL, '2026-10-16T16:08:58Z', '2026-10-16T16:08:58Z', 1357, NULL, 'rule', 1357, '2026-10-16, task 1357: written into plan 151: Bundle selection on its own full screen (Layout: counts).

2026-10-18, task 1610: the strings carry the noun: bundles.countPlus «{n}+ questions» (es «preguntas», de «Fragen»), bundles.count plural forms («1 question» / «{n} questions»); rules (which number, rounding) unchanged.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (268, 'Short-group marks after the flow change', 'answered', '[]', 'Milestone 8''s bundle-screen line moves the supply report, short-group marks and the running-out notice to the bundle screen (decision 264). The marks label age-group tiles, which only Choice shows, and on Choice the bundles aren''t picked yet, so marks there would be guesswork; the bundle screen has no age-group tiles to mark. The report on the bundle screen carries the same information for the group actually chosen; Back to Choice changes the group.', 'The game marks age groups that don''t have enough fresh questions left for a full game. Until now the marks sat on the screen where players pick the age group. With the question sets now picked after that screen, the shortage can only be known once the sets are chosen, so this settles where that warning appears.', 'Choice shows no supply information any more: no per-group «short» marks, no report. The bundle screen computes the supply of the full choice (mode, age group, levels, cards, bundles) and shows the supply report and the disabled start there; when the chosen group is short the report says so; no marks on other groups.', NULL, '2026-10-16T16:08:59Z', '2026-10-16T16:08:59Z', 1357, NULL, 'rule', 1357, '2026-10-16, task 1357: written into plan 151: Bundle selection on its own full screen (Flow: Choice without supply).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (269, 'Bundle screen: a mod''s extra base bundle', 'answered', '["narrow column: one base bundle → info only, not focusable; two or more → the same narrow column lists them as compact radio tiles (focusable, letter keys, no heading or prompt), preset per decision 158", "among the optional tiles: a mod base bundle is a tile in the flowing area marked as replacement; switching it on swaps it into the left column and ours out", "no mod base bundles: mods offer add-on bundles only; check-mod refuses kind base; modding docs change"]', 'Milestone 8''s bundle-screen line (decision 264): base column info only, narrow, no «Elijan uno», exactly one base bundle per session; ''a mod''s extra base bundle: open question for the plan''. Today modding/README.md and modding/bundles.md offer kind "base" as ''a second choice beside ours'', ours the default (decision 158''s preset). Answer picks how the left column behaves; the layout work of plan 151: Bundle selection on its own full screen waits for it.', 'Players can install player-made question sets, and such a set may replace the game''s general set instead of adding to it. The redesigned bundle screen shows the general set in a narrow column for information only, with nothing to pick. This asks how a player picks a player-made general set there: inside that narrow column, among the extra sets, or not at all.', 'narrow column: one base bundle → info only, not focusable; two or more → the same narrow column lists them as compact radio tiles (focusable, letter keys, no heading or prompt), preset per decision 158', NULL, '2026-10-16T16:09:04Z', '2026-10-16T16:37:49Z', 1357, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1379: superseded by milestone 8''s any-combination line: every base bundle, a mod''s too, is a toggle tile (role switch), no radio tiles, no info-only tile; BundleSelector.toggled toggles every bundle.

2026-10-17, task 1340: 2026-10-16, task 1340: Superseded by milestone 8''s any-combination line: every base bundle, a mod''s extra ones too, is a focusable toggle tile (letter key) in the narrow base column; no info-only tile, no radio tiles (tasks 1361, 1379).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (270, 'Start horizon reviewed in /gallery?', 'answered', '["Approved", "Flags in /gallery"]', 'How to review: run `trivia-gallery --only start,start-resume,start-save-notice,language-from-start,credits` (and again with `--size 1280x800`), then `trivia-authoring` → `/gallery` (folders per language, `@1280x800` for the small size). Check: title, buttons, notice and language button stay readable over/above the band; no image looks wrong, cut off badly or clustered; the credits screen lists the images. Flag what to change per shot and image. Worker run found no plain breakage.', 'The start screen now shows a colourful row of pictures along its bottom edge, like a skyline of landmarks, animals and objects from around the world. Before it is final, it has to be looked at on screen in every language and at two window sizes. Either it is fine as it is, or some pictures, their order or the layout need changes.', 'we need more images, about twice as many maybe three times, the horizon can''t be a flat line: it should curve up towards the left and right. the z-index should get sorted: landmarks/nature in the background, stuff in the foreground. consider shrinking things a bit to fit more, obivously no need to shrinkg landmarks/nature/buildings that go into the background anyway', NULL, '2026-10-16T16:26:07Z', '2026-10-16T16:37:13Z', 1331, NULL, 'once', NULL, '2026-10-17, task 1362: Horizon rebuilt (task 1362): 35 images (19 new CC0 Commons cut-outs: landmarks, Akuka Africa set, gnokii objects) in two layers, landmarks/nature behind and objects/animals in front, on a bowl-shaped ground rising to both edges; shots re-run at 1920x1080 and 1280x800 in all four locales; credits and VD-11 updated');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (271, 'QC neutralize-1: which changes?', 'answered', '["A only: no changes, no second pass (recommended)", "A and B: add run/QC neutralize-2 for the 11 left questions", "Other"]', 'Report: authoring/reports/neutralize-1.md. Spot check 30 of 30; agreement 24/30 (80 %, bar 90 %); false approve 4 (13 %), false needs-work 2; audit agreement 87 %. None of the 6 disagreements is about neutral wording or changed meaning: q-0473 (image doesn''t match), q-0285 (video doesn''t fit), q-1134 (answer ambiguous), q-1009 (image replaced by the developer) are pre-existing media/ambiguity issues; q-1077, q-0202 were approved by the developer against the LLM. Decision 214 left 11 questions un-neutralized (q-0476, q-0590, q-0641, q-0768, q-0896, q-0931, q-0941, q-1100, q-1112, q-1178, q-1194). Proposals: A) no prompt change and no second pass: the 80 % comes from media and ambiguity, covered by plan 17''s tasks 1182 and the review tool; the 6 questions already carry the developer''s verdicts. B) second pass run neutralize-2 / QC neutralize-2 only for the 11 un-neutralized questions.', 'A recent pass reworded Spanish quiz questions so they read naturally to Spanish speakers everywhere instead of only in Colombia. A 30-question spot check by the developer found 24 agreeing with the automatic review, below the 90 % bar. We need to choose whether any follow-up work is warranted.', 'A only: no changes, no second pass (recommended)', NULL, '2026-10-16T16:27:27Z', '2026-10-16T16:33:48Z', 633, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (272, 'Right stick: what it does beyond switching prompts to the pad', 'answered', '[]', 'Every screen is already fully drivable with the D-pad / left stick (focus moves, scroll areas follow focus, Credits scroll by up/down). A second meaning (scrolling) would add per-screen rules and checks, and pushing the stick while holding R3 for the peek would scroll under the peek. Revisit with local multiplayer (milestone 16) or if a screen needs free scrolling. Keeps decision 126: Image peek: which pad button and key?', 'A game controller has two thumbsticks; the left one already moves the highlight between buttons, the right one has done nothing so far. Moving it should now at least show the controller''s button pictures on screen. The choice here is whether it also does something in the game, such as scrolling, or only switches the pictures.', 'Only switches prompts to pad glyphs; no action of its own. R3 (its click) stays the image peek.', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:22:50Z', '2026-10-16T19:22:50Z', 1375, NULL, 'rule', 1375, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (273, 'Keyboard-only turn-based sessions: what does a player''s own key do?', 'answered', '["nothing: keyboard and mouse act for whoever''s turn it is; keys are picked only for timed sessions", "handover: the turn''s player presses their key first, then keyboard and mouse act for them until the turn ends"]', 'Milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls, open question 1. `decision 24: Multiplayer input and couch co-op (OQ-37)?` had a handover (the key gives that player keyboard and mouse until their turn is done); milestone 16 replaces it where it differs. In round-robin co-op and competitive the turn order is fixed (join order), so whoever''s turn it is is already known.
- Option 1: the key does nothing in turn-based modes; keyboard and mouse act for whoever''s turn it is; keyboard-only players pick a key only for Competitive (timed), or pick one anyway as their identity on the join screen
- Option 2: handover as in decision 24: the turn''s player must press their key before keyboard and mouse act (a ''ready'' step each turn)', 'When a family plays the turn-based multiplayer modes without controllers, everyone shares one keyboard and mouse, and each player has picked their own key on the join screen. In these modes the game already says whose turn it is, so the key may do nothing, or it may be a handover: pressing it gives that player the keyboard and mouse. The answer decides whether the join screen asks for keys in turn-based modes at all and whether turns can be passed by hand.', 'nothing: keyboard and mouse act for whoever''s turn it is; keys are picked only for timed sessions

note: "timed sessions" is bad wording, you probably mean competitive multiplayer with keyboard only', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:34:49Z', '2026-10-16T20:22:19Z', 1353, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1397: written into plan 162: Mode row, session setup and join screen (decision 292): keyboard-only round-robin sessions add any number of keyboard seats on the Join screen with the shared binding and no key; only keyboard-only timed sessions capture a claim key per seat (claimKeyOk, decision 290).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (274, 'Pads plus keyboard: one keyboard player, or several key players?', 'answered', '["keyboard and mouse always exactly one player when a pad has joined", "mixed: pads plus several key players sharing the keyboard"]', 'Milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls, open question 2. The milestone''s rule: each pad is one player; keyboard and mouse together are one player (4 pads + keyboard and mouse = 5 players); a keyboard-only session (no pads) is a special kind where every player has one key.
- Option 1: as soon as one pad has joined, keyboard and mouse are exactly one player; several key players only in sessions without pads
- Option 2: mixed: pads plus several key players; in Competitive (timed) each key player claims with their key; in the turn-based modes keyboard and mouse act for the key player whose turn it is', 'In a session where some players have controllers, the rest could still share the keyboard, each with their own key, or the keyboard and mouse could always count as exactly one player. Allowing several keyboard players next to controllers lets bigger families play with few pads, but makes the join screen and the claim keys more complex.', 'keyboard and mouse always exactly one player when a pad has joined', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:34:50Z', '2026-10-16T20:22:42Z', 1353, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1397: written into plan 162: Mode row, session setup and join screen (decision 292): once a pad is seated the Join screen allows one keyboard-and-mouse seat only; a pad''s A is refused with a notice while two or more keyboard seats exist; a lone keyboard seat becomes the keyboard-and-mouse seat (Space claims) when a pad joins.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (275, 'Timed mode, keyboard-only: how does the claimer answer in the 6 seconds?', 'answered', '["both: mouse click or the answer keys", "keys only", "mouse only"]', 'Milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls, open question 3. After a claim only the claimer may act until they answer or the 6 s run out; other claim keys are ignored meanwhile.
- Option 1: both: the claimer clicks an answer or presses its key (A–D / 1–4 per catalog)
- Option 2: keys only (no mouse during a keyboard-only timed game)
- Option 3: mouse only (the claimer takes the mouse)', 'In the timed competitive mode a player claims a question and then has six seconds to answer. Without controllers, everyone shares one keyboard and mouse, so the claimer could answer with the mouse, with the answer keys, or with either. The answer changes how fast and how fair the answering is when several people sit at one keyboard.', 'both: mouse click or the answer keys', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:34:50Z', '2026-10-16T20:23:11Z', 1353, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1399: written into plan 164: Timed game screens, drama and sound: after a claim the claimer answers with pad face buttons, A–D/1–4 or a click; with decision 295 the first pick is the answer (no lock-in, no «Respuesta final», no drum roll), reveal after TIMED_REVEAL_MS. No longer holds: ''answers as on the Question screen'' in decision 290 read as the two-step lock-in.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (276, 'Timed mode: after a drop-out, how many questions does a round have?', 'answered', '["players still in at the round''s start", "players at the session''s start"]', 'Milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls says "each round has as many questions as there are players"; `plans/13-game-modes.md` "Competitive timed" lists it as an open detail. `qgen report --simulate`''s stand-in already uses the players still in; backlog B-15 (`outlook.seenPerLevel` counts 1 per round) waits for this.
- Option 1: questions per round = players still in at the round''s start
- Option 2: questions per round = players at the session''s start, for the whole game', 'In the timed competitive mode each round has as many questions as there are players. Once players drop out, a round could keep its starting number of questions or shrink to the players still in. Shrinking keeps the game tight and uses fewer questions; keeping the count gives the remaining players more chances per round to avoid losing a life.', 'players still in at the round''s start', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:34:50Z', '2026-10-16T20:23:40Z', 1353, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1394: written into plan 159: Competitive (timed) in the engine.

2026-10-16, task 1395: plan 160: Engine consumers of multiplayer sessions: outlook.ts seenPerLevel for timed = players (one question per player still in, worst case no drop-out) and per = players, replacing the 1-per-round count (backlog B-15 closed by its task 3); qgen report --simulate drops runTimed for the engine''s timed sessions (round size from the engine). No longer holds: B-15''s ''timed counts 1 question per round''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (277, 'Multiplayer sessions in the save: one games[] row per session, with seats', 'answered', '[]', 'One running row keeps latest()/running()/newest(), the seen rows'' game_id, telemetry''s one game key and achievements'' ended-game check working unchanged; one row per player would mean several running rows at once (every action''s running() lookup changes) and rr-coop''s shared level/jokers and timed''s shared question copied into every player''s row. Readers that need every player or every game state of a row go through two helpers in engine/state.ts (the row''s players, the row''s game states), so stats and achievements don''t special-case modes. player_id null keeps sessions out of a solo player''s preset (playerChoice). The seat index, not a device or a name, names the actor, so milestone 17''s remote guests are just more seats. Version bump so an older build backs the save up as newer instead of reading a session row as a gamemaster game.', 'A local multiplayer game has several players at once, and the game file has to remember them: who sat down in which order, whose turn it is, and each player''s own progress where the mode gives them one. It could store one game entry per session with a list of players, or one entry per player tied together by a session number. The choice decides how simple the player statistics, achievements, usage reports and the ''find the running game'' logic stay.', 'One games[] row per session: GameRow.seats (join order: player_id, per-seat state where the mode needs it: rr-competitive''s own GameState, timed''s lives/out, a per-seat result) and GameRow.turn; row.state is the shared game (rr-coop''s team game, timed''s question flow; rr-competitive: the shared settings only); player_id null on session rows; history entries carry the answering seat; actions name the acting seat by index (device-agnostic); SAVE_VERSION 5', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:40:00Z', '2026-10-16T19:40:00Z', 1392, NULL, 'rule', 1392, '2026-10-16, task 1392: written into plan 157: Session model and save format (Shape, Helpers, Version 5).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (278, 'Round-robin competitive: the last player standing wins at once', 'answered', '[]', 'Milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls specs ''first to level 12 wins'' for rr-competitive and ''one player left: they win'' for timed; plan 152 part 2 asks to settle rr-competitive''s last player standing.
- Same rule as timed: one rule for both competitive modes, easy to explain at the table
- Playing on alone turns the end into a solo game the others watch, and burns up to 12 more questions per session (D-82: seen per save)
- Turns are one at a time, so exactly one player is left when the second-to-last drops out; nobody-left can''t happen
- The winner may be at a lower level than a player who dropped out: outlasting is the competition', 'In the turn-based competitive mode every player climbs their own ladder of questions, and a wrong answer knocks a player out. When everyone but one player has been knocked out, the game could either declare that player the winner straight away or let them play on alone until they reach the top. Winning at once matches the timed competitive mode, ends the evening''s game sooner and uses fewer questions.', 'The last player still in wins at once (their seat won, the session ends), whatever their level; first to level 12 still wins too', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:42:42Z', '2026-10-16T19:42:42Z', 1393, NULL, 'rule', 1393, '2026-10-16, task 1393: written into plan 158: Round-robin modes in the engine (rr-competitive ends).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (279, 'Round-robin screens: the Level screen is the turn handover, no screen of its own', 'answered', '[]', 'One extra screen per turn would add a click per question for the whole session (12 × N); the Level screen is already the pause between questions and carries the tower. The out moment matters for the family (the crumbling tower), so it keeps the Wrong screen, which leads on instead of home.', 'In the turn-based multiplayer modes the TV has to tell the room whose turn comes next. This choice puts that announcement on the existing level screen, which already sits between one question and the next, instead of adding one more screen to click through every turn. A wrong answer that knocks a player out still gets its own moment before the game moves on.', 'In rr-coop and rr-competitive the Level screen, shown after every final answer, names the next turn''s player (name, seat colour, their device glyph) and, in rr-competitive, shows every player''s track; no separate turn screen. A wrong answer in rr-competitive that only knocks that player out shows the Wrong screen with «Siguiente» (not «Inicio»), then the Level screen of the next turn, or the Victory screen when it left one player standing (decision 278).', NULL, '2026-10-16T19:44:48Z', '2026-10-16T19:44:48Z', 1398, NULL, 'rule', 1398, '2026-10-16, task 1398: written into plan 163: Round-robin game screens (Binding, Turn display, rr-competitive Wrong/Victory).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (280, 'Timed mode: everyone out at one round end means nobody wins', 'answered', '[]', 'Sparing everyone could loop without end (round 12 repeats, see the round-12 rule) and needs a second exception; a shared win would hand WIN_TIMED to several seats for not answering. The milestone''s red frame ''when every player is at risk at once'' is exactly this warning, so the threat has to be real. Terminates every session.', 'In the timed competitive mode a player who gets no question right in a round loses a life, and the third lost life knocks them out. If every remaining player is on their last life and nobody answers correctly in a round, they would all go out at once. The game could then declare nobody the winner, spare them all, or let them share the win; this rule picks one.', 'Every seat that loses its last life at the same round end goes out; when that leaves nobody in, the session ends with no winner: every such seat lost, the row''s result lost (the family loses to the game, as a lost solo game does)', NULL, '2026-10-16T20:59:08Z', '2026-10-16T20:59:08Z', 1394, NULL, 'rule', 1394, '2026-10-16, task 1394: written into plan 159: Competitive (timed) in the engine.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (281, 'Timed mode: round 12 without a correct answer repeats as another sudden-death round', 'answered', '[]', 'Reuses the round-end rule unchanged, needs no extra counter beyond the round number, and always terminates because every repeat without a correct answer costs every remaining seat a life. Extra level-12 rounds draw outside the session''s already shown questions; if none are left, the draw widens to the age group''s whole range, and if even that is empty the session ends with no winner (never level_empty mid-session).', 'The last round of the timed competitive mode is sudden death: the first correct answer wins the game. If every question of that round passes without anyone answering correctly, the game needs a rule for what comes next. This rule makes the round end as any other round (lives lost, players out) and, if two or more are still in, plays another sudden-death round at the same difficulty.', 'Round 12 exhausted with no correct answer: the normal round end (no correct answer = minus one life, third lost = out, everyone out = no winner); two or more seats left: another round at level 12, sudden death, size = seats still in; one left: they win. Ends after at most three such rounds (three lives)', NULL, '2026-10-16T20:59:08Z', '2026-10-16T20:59:08Z', 1394, NULL, 'rule', 1394, '2026-10-16, task 1394: written into plan 159: Competitive (timed) in the engine.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (282, 'Timed mode: the Esc menu and the Steam overlay pause the clock and hide the question', 'answered', '[]', 'A family game on a TV gets interrupted (doorbell, a child); losing a life to an interruption would feel unfair, and with the question hidden the pause gives no thinking time. Deadlines live in the save as timestamps, so the shift is one write and a reload while paused stays paused. A crash without a pause write is not caught: on resume the passed deadlines apply (claim timeout = wrong); rare, and anything else would let a reload reset the clock.', 'The timed competitive mode is the only mode with a clock: 30 seconds per question and 6 seconds after a player claims it. When someone opens the in-game menu, the Steam overlay appears, or the window is hidden, the clock could keep running or stop. This rule stops it and hides the question, so a pause neither costs anyone the question nor gives anyone extra thinking time.', 'Opening the Esc menu, the Steam overlay (where the shell reports it) or hiding the window sends pause: the engine stores paused_at, refuses claim and answer (paused), and view() hides the question and answers; resume shifts every running deadline (question shown_at, claim) by the paused time. A claimer''s remaining seconds are kept. No pause outside timed (D-92 unchanged)', NULL, '2026-10-16T20:59:08Z', '2026-10-16T20:59:08Z', 1394, NULL, 'rule', 1394, '2026-10-16, task 1394: written into plan 159: Competitive (timed) in the engine.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (283, 'Remote Play Together: what happens when a remote guest leaves mid-game?', 'answered', '["same as a local pad lost (milestone 16''s rule)", "out at once, no pause", "pause until back or the host continues without them"]', '`milestone 17: Remote Play Together`, Outcome "Session awareness": "a guest who drops mid-game is handled per mode (decision for the masterplan)". A remote guest''s pad is one more virtual pad (milestone 16''s binding); when the stream ends, Steam removes it, and the shell also gets a session-disconnected callback (ISteamRemotePlay), so the game knows it was a remote guest, not a loose cable.

`milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls` settles a local pad lost mid-session in its per-player controls plan (task 1396: "pause + «reconnect» or skip the player").
- Option 1: same rule as a local pad lost (whatever task 1396 records); a guest who reconnects gets a new virtual pad and takes their seat back through that rule''s «reconnect» path. One rule, no remote-only code
- Option 2: no pause: the guest is out at once (rr-coop: their turns are skipped; rr-competitive, timed: out, as a wrong answer); the session goes on
- Option 3: pause for everyone until the guest is back or the host picks «seguir sin él/ella»; then as option 2', 'With Steam Remote Play Together, friends far away play along over the internet; if one of them loses the connection or closes the stream, their controller vanishes from the game in the middle of a turn. The game can treat that exactly like a controller unplugged in the living room, or handle it its own way: drop the guest right away, or wait for them to come back. The answer decides whether a lost guest pauses the game for everyone and whether they lose their place.', 'pause until back or the host continues without them', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:01:01Z', '2026-10-16T21:22:32Z', 1365, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (284, 'Remote Play Together gate: single player, controller input and local multiplayer playtested?', 'pending', '["passed", "failed (list what)"]', 'Gate of `milestone 17: Remote Play Together` (developer, 2026-10-16). Every implementation task of the milestone waits for this answer; investigation and planning tasks don''t.

Checklist (packaged build or `trivia-desktop`, TV, real pads):
- A: single player: Gamemaster mode and «Solo o en grupo», start to victory and to a wrong answer
- B: controller input per milestone 8: pads, keyboard and mouse interchangeable in one game; prompts follow the last input (`plan 156: Prompts follow the last input`)
- C: local multiplayer per `milestone 16: Local multiplayer: game modes and controls`: its three modes with pads and with keyboard (its close-out playtest counts)

Failures: list them (A/B/C, screen, what happened); they become follow-up tasks in the milestone they belong to.', 'Before any work starts on letting friends join over the internet through Steam, the parts it builds on must work at home: playing alone or as one group against the gamemaster, playing with controllers, and the new modes where several people in the room play against each other or together. This asks the developer to play those three on the TV and say whether they pass. Until the answer is «passed», only research and planning for internet play goes ahead.', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-16T21:01:07Z', NULL, 1365, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (285, 'Achievements from multiplayer sessions: every seat earns for the save; tower achievements need a win at the top level', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 73: Achievement list (SW-11)? and decision 139: Achievement triggers derived without a save change (SW-11) keep everything derived from the save, for one Steam user per machine. A last-one-standing win (decision 278: Round-robin competitive: the last player standing wins at once; timed''s drop-outs) can come at level 1 with two players, so letting it count as a tower win would make WINS_50 farmable; the mode win stays any win because the mode''s achievement is about winning that mode. No save field, no SAVE_VERSION change.', 'The game''s Steam achievements belong to the one Steam account on the machine, while a multiplayer game has several people playing. This settles which of a multiplayer game''s events count towards those achievements, and guards against cheap wins: in two of the new modes a player can win just because everyone else dropped out early, which should earn the mode''s own trophy but not the ones about climbing all twelve levels.', 'Every seat''s play counts for the save''s account, as solo games of different family members already do; a session''s own win (row result won, any way) earns its mode''s achievement (WIN_RR_COOP, WIN_RR_COMPETITIVE, WIN_TIMED) and nothing else by itself; the win-based ones (FIRST_WIN, WINS_10, WINS_50, WIN_ADULTS, WIN_ALL_AGES, WIN_NO_JOKERS, ALL_JOKERS_WIN, SOPLO_SAVE, NIGHT_OWL, POLYGLOT) count a session only when its winning game answered the last level right (rr-coop: the team''s game; rr-competitive: the winning seat''s game; timed: the round-12 sudden-death answer), one win per session, age group and jokers of that winning game; joker, knowledge and habit ones (FIRST_*, ALL_HINTS, PURGE, SUPER_PICKY, SNIPER_TOP, ALL_CATEGORIES, SPECIALIST, QUESTIONS_1000, GAME_NIGHTS, CHRISTMAS) read every game of the session (rowStates); qualifies per mode: timed''s standard is 0 cards and no jokers, the others as solo, every state of the session checked', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:02:26Z', '2026-10-16T21:02:26Z', 1395, NULL, 'rule', 1395, '2026-10-16, task 1395: written into plan 160: Engine consumers of multiplayer sessions (Achievements, task 1).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (286, 'Telemetry of multiplayer sessions: one game per played game, a player count, timed answers not clean', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 119: Telemetry recording: derived from the save into a per-install side file: still derived, no save field. One record per actual game keeps played.py''s per-age-group curves right for rr-competitive''s mixed age groups. The server keeps known fields only, so an older server simply drops players until the developer deploys.', 'With the player''s consent the game sends anonymous play statistics, used to check how hard questions really are. Multiplayer games need a shape in those statistics: whether a session is one record or one per player, what is said about the number of players, and whether answers given under the timed mode''s six-second pressure should count when judging a question''s difficulty. Nothing personal is sent either way: no names and no order of players.', 'rr-coop and timed: one telemetry game per session; rr-competitive: one per seat (its own age group, level reached, jokers, result; key <id>@<started_on>#<seat>); every game gets players = seats in the session (1 for solo and gamemaster, optional on the server, missing = 1); timed: reached = round, jokers 0, answers = claimed questions only, result the session''s; qgen telemetry folds players into a per-mode count and treats timed answers as not clean (self-selected claims under a 6 s clock), so --played curves don''t take them', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:02:33Z', '2026-10-16T21:02:33Z', 1395, NULL, 'rule', 1395, '2026-10-16, task 1395: written into plan 160: Engine consumers of multiplayer sessions (Telemetry, task 2).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (287, 'usa taxonomy: categories and subcategories', 'answered', '["apply as proposed (14 categories, 50 subcategories)", "apply with changes: say which", "redo the proposal"]', 'Bundle `usa` (US English, `languages: ["en"]`, country US, target 2000 ≈ 40 per subcategory).

Rule: Answering needs a connection to the United States: American places, food, customs, holidays, people, music, sport, media, words, sayings or history that players outside the US mostly wouldn''t know; topics shared across the English-speaking world, and British-only ones (`uk`), are not here.

Voice: American English: US spelling, US words ("soccer", "cookie", "truck"), dates as "July 20, 1969", imperial units.

Proposal (`qgen taxonomy --bundle usa`, from the general list of decision 213: General category list for regional bundles): 14 categories, 50 subcategories. Slug, English name (the bundle''s language is English; es/de names are drafted on apply). Full proposal with scopes and samples: `work/taxonomy/usa/proposal.md`.

- `geography` Geography of the United States (4, general)
