- `myths-and-legends` American myths and legends (3, general)
  - `folk-heroes-and-tall-tales` Folk heroes and tall tales
  - `cryptids-and-ufos` Cryptids and UFO lore
  - `ghosts-and-superstitions` Ghost stories and superstitions
- `music-and-dance` American music and dance (3, general)
  - `country-and-folk` Country and folk
  - `roots-and-soul` Blues, jazz, soul and hip-hop scenes
  - `traditional-songs` American traditional and patriotic songs
- `art-and-literature` American art and literature (3, general)
  - `american-literature` American literature
  - `childrens-books` American children''s books
  - `comics-and-art` American comics and art
- `film-and-television` American film and television (4, general)
  - `tv-shows` American TV classics
  - `kids-tv` American kids'' TV
  - `hosts-and-game-shows` Game shows and TV hosts
  - `movie-favorites` American movie favorites
- `sports` Sports in the United States (4, general)
  - `american-football` Football
  - `baseball` Baseball
  - `college-sports` College and high school sports
  - `more-sports` Hockey, racing and more
- `how-people-speak` How America speaks (3, general)
  - `words-and-slang` American words and slang
  - `sayings-and-idioms` American sayings and idioms
  - `place-names` Saying American place names
- `daily-life` Daily life in the United States (4, general)
  - `money-and-shopping` Money and shopping
  - `school-life` School in America
  - `household-brands` American household brands and toys
  - `getting-around` Getting around and public services
- `symbols` Symbols of the United States (2, general)
  - `national-symbols` National symbols
  - `state-symbols` State nicknames and symbols', 'The new United States question pack needs its own list of topics, so its questions cover the American side of life evenly. The proposal below keeps all 14 topics of the general list and splits them into 50 subtopics, about 40 questions each at 2000 questions. Applying it fixes the pack''s structure; the Spanish and German topic names are drafted at that point.', 'apply as proposed (14 categories, 50 subcategories)', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:03:29Z', '2026-10-16T21:20:41Z', 1276, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-16, task 1276: usa taxonomy applied: app/data/bundles/usa/categories.json holds 14 categories and 50 subcategories keyed by English slugs; names.en/es/de.json written (64 names each, es/de drafted on apply). The en default bundles now offer usa and uk next to base, so engine tests (pool, choice) no longer hardcode base + colombia.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (288, 'Local multiplayer: who opens the Esc menu in a session, and who drives it', 'answered', '[]', 'Families pause for real-world reasons that don''t wait for a turn; restricting it to the turn''s player would leave others unable to stop the game, and a pause can''t cheat: nothing in the menu changes the game in multiplayer modes but ending it, which already asks to confirm. Timed pause semantics are decision 282.', 'During a multiplayer game on the TV, someone has to be able to open the pause menu, for example to stop for dinner or end the game. The choice was between letting only the player on turn open it, or letting anyone do so. Anyone can open it, and once it is open every controller, the keyboard and the mouse can use it, as in the menus before a game.', 'Any player''s menu input (Esc, Start, the corner menu button) opens the Esc menu in a session; while it is open it is shared UI: every device drives it, as before the session. In timed it pauses the clock (decision 282). Its items stay as in the mode (skip, undo, level, settings are gamemaster only); restart ends the whole session, with the existing confirm.', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:05:44Z', '2026-10-16T21:05:44Z', 1396, NULL, 'rule', 1396, '2026-10-16, task 1396: written into plan 161: Per-player controls.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (289, 'Local multiplayer: a controller lost mid-session waits for a controller to take the seat', 'answered', '[]', 'Skipping would need a new engine rule (an absent seat) and would cost the player a turn or a life for a flat battery; waiting is fair and needs no engine change. Binding by any free pad keeps the binding device-agnostic for Remote Play Together (a remote guest is one more pad). Pads reconnect at an index Chromium picks, so index identity is not relied on.', 'If a player''s controller runs out of battery or disconnects during a multiplayer game, the game has to decide what happens to that player. The options were to skip them and play on, or to wait until a controller takes their place. The game waits: a dialog asks for that player''s controller, and any free controller can take over the seat.', 'No skip. When a seat''s pad disconnects, the seat is unbound; a modal «Mando de <name> desconectado» dialog shows when that seat is needed (round-robin: when the turn reaches it; timed: at once, with the clock paused through the engine''s pause). A press of A on the reconnected pad or on any pad bound to no seat binds it to that seat and closes the dialog; the Esc menu stays reachable to end the session. The same dialog rebinds pad seats after a page reload when the stored binding is gone.', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:05:44Z', '2026-10-16T21:05:44Z', 1396, NULL, 'rule', 1396, '2026-10-16, task 1396: written into plan 161: Per-player controls.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (290, 'Timed mode: the claim input per kind of player', 'answered', '[]', 'Bumpers can be hit without looking and don''t collide with the face buttons that answer; Space is the biggest free key for the one keyboard player. Excluding every key the Question screen binds keeps a claim key from also answering.', 'In the timed competitive mode, players race to grab each question before answering it, so each player needs one obvious button to grab with. The choice is which button that is on a controller, which key the keyboard player uses, and which keys players may pick when everyone shares one keyboard. A shoulder button on controllers, the space bar for the keyboard player, and any free letter for shared-keyboard players.', 'Pad player: LB or RB (no face button: those pick answers; no jokers in timed, so the bumpers are free). Keyboard-and-mouse player: Space or a click on the claim control. Keyboard-only timed session: each player''s picked key (decision 273); allowed: a single letter or digit the Question screen doesn''t bind (not the answer letters or 1–9, the peek, replay or flag keys) and not Enter, Esc, Space, Backspace, arrows; distinct per player. After a claim, the claimer answers as on the Question screen: pad face buttons; keys A–D/1–4 or a click for keyboard players (decision 275).', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:05:44Z', '2026-10-16T21:05:44Z', 1396, NULL, 'rule', 1396, '2026-10-16, task 1396: written into plan 161: Per-player controls.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (291, 'Local multiplayer setup: flow and where each setting lives', 'answered', '[]', 'Keeps the existing Choice and Bundles screens (decision 158, decision 264, decision 268) and adds one screen; the Join screen must come before Bundles because the supply check needs the player count and per-seat groups; per-seat groups need seats, so rr-competitive''s groups sit on Join.', 'Before a multiplayer game starts, the family picks a game mode, says who plays, and sets difficulty and question sets. This settles the order of those screens and on which screen each setting sits, so every mode is set up the same way and nobody has to set the same thing twice.', 'Player → Choice (mode row with the multiplayer modes) → Join (multiplayer modes only) → Bundles → Level. The name picked on the Player screen is the first seat''s preset name. Choice: one age group for rr-coop and timed; rr-competitive has no group line there, each seat sets its own on the Join screen (preset: that player''s newest game''s group, else the default). «Personalizar partida» (joker counts, same for every seat) in rr-coop and rr-competitive, not in timed (no jokers). Bundles: one list per session, preset from the Player-screen player, supply checked for the whole session (player count, per-seat groups). Back: Bundles → Join → Choice, picks kept.', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:08:28Z', '2026-10-16T21:08:28Z', 1397, NULL, 'rule', 1397, '2026-10-16, task 1397: written into plan 162: Mode row, session setup and join screen.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (292, 'Join screen: joining, leaving, names and claim keys', 'answered', '[]', 'Plan 161''s join helpers (bind an unbound pad''s A, the kbm join, claimKeyOk) do the binding; the screen only adds names, groups and removal; refusing pads instead of silently dropping keyboard seats keeps names nobody asked to lose.', 'On the join screen each person in the room signs in with their own controller, or the keyboard joins for those without one. This settles how someone joins and leaves, how names are chosen, and how keyboard-only players pick their personal key for the timed race, so the screen works the same with controllers, keyboard only, or both.', 'An unbound pad''s A joins it as a new seat; a bound pad''s B leaves its own seat (A again rejoins); every bound pad, keyboard and mouse drive the shared UI otherwise. The keyboard joins through a «join with keyboard» control (Enter or click); with no pad seated each press adds another keyboard seat (rr-*: they share keyboard and mouse, decision 273; timed: each picks its claim key right away, claimKeyOk, decision 290), once a pad is seated only one keyboard-and-mouse seat (decision 274) and a pad''s A is refused with a notice while two or more keyboard seats exist; a single keyboard seat becomes the keyboard-and-mouse seat when a pad joins (its picked key dropped, Space claims). Every seat row has a remove control. Names: each seat needs one before start, distinct by casefold; a name chooser per seat (known players not yet seated, keys 1–9, plus a new name with the field, LetterPicker for pads or Steam''s floating keyboard); the first seat preset to the Player screen''s name. Seat colour by join order. No cap of our own, start needs at least 2 seats.', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:08:29Z', '2026-10-16T21:08:29Z', 1397, NULL, 'rule', 1397, '2026-10-16, task 1397: written into plan 162: Mode row, session setup and join screen.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (293, 'Unbuilt multiplayer modes: on the mode row in dev mode only', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 158 moves mode_not_built to api.ts new; the gallery and the checks of parts 6–8 and 11 need the modes before part 10 ships them (decision 168: Store texts name only the built game modes; BUILT_MODES gate).', 'The new multiplayer modes are built screen by screen long before they ship, and they only appear for players once their Steam achievement exists. Until then the developer and the automated screenshots still need to reach them. This settles that they show up only in the game''s developer mode.', 'While a mode isn''t in BUILT_MODES, the Choice screen''s mode row shows it only in devMode (dev server, TRIVIA_DEV_STEAM_USER), marked PLACEHOLDER(<task id>) with the task that ships it; the engine''s api new accepts an unbuilt mode only with a dev flag the client sets in devMode; a real Steam user never sees or starts one.', NULL, '2026-10-16T21:08:29Z', '2026-10-16T21:08:29Z', 1397, NULL, 'rule', 1397, '2026-10-16, task 1397: written into plan 162: Mode row, session setup and join screen.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (294, 'germany taxonomy: categories and subcategories', 'answered', '["the proposal as is (15 categories, 53 subcategories)", "changes (say which)"]', 'Bundle `germany` (German (Germany), `languages: ["de"]`, country DE, target 2000 ≈ 38 per subcategory).

Rule: Answering needs a connection to Germany: German places, food, customs, holidays, people, music, sport, media, words, sayings or history that players outside Germany mostly wouldn''t know; things known to families anywhere (Berlin''s wall, Beethoven, Oktoberfest as a fact) stay in `base`, and Austrian or Swiss topics are not here.

Voice: Standard German as spoken in Germany: German spelling with "ß", German words ("Brötchen", "Handy", "Fahrrad", "Bürgersteig"), dates as "20. Juli 1969", metric units

Proposal (`qgen taxonomy --bundle germany`, from the general list of decision 213: General category list for regional bundles): 15 categories, 53 subcategories, none of the general list left out, one region category (`east-germany`). Slug, English name, German name (the bundle''s language; es names are drafted on apply). Full proposal with scopes and samples: `work/taxonomy/germany/proposal.md`.

