## `authoring/tools/prompts/taxonomy-theme.md` (new, whole file)
```
# Task: propose a topic bundle''s taxonomy

A topic bundle is a pack of trivia questions for families who love one topic (the bundle''s rule below): they want more of it, and deeper, than the main pool, `base`, gives them. `base` stays as it is and keeps its own questions on the topic; its subcategories are listed below with their approved question counts. The bundle adds questions, it never repeats base''s: a family playing both gets new concepts, not the famous ones again.

You propose the bundle''s broad categories and their subcategories. Drafting later picks concepts per subcategory (150–250 each) and writes questions about them, at every difficulty from 1 to 15, in English first, then translated for players in other languages and countries.

## Keys and names
Every category and every subcategory has:
- `slug`: its key in code and files, never shown to players: English, lowercase ASCII words joined by dashes (`olympic-history`, `bridges-and-tunnels`); unique within the bundle among categories and among subcategories (a category and a subcategory may share one). No bundle name in it: `football-tactics`, not `sports-football-tactics`. It may equal a base slug only for the same topic seen deeper; prefer a slug of its own.
- `en`: the English name players see, short, sentence case, as on a game''s category screen ("Football tactics", "Bridges and tunnels").
- `name`: the same as `en`.

Names in the game''s other languages are made from `en` later.

## Steps
1. **Base on the topic.** Find base''s subcategories on the bundle''s topic. Each is a starting point: the bundle goes beyond it (lesser-known concepts, a narrower angle, a part base only touches) without asking base''s concepts again.
2. **Categories.** Group the bundle''s topic into broad categories, as a fan of the topic sees it. `origin`: `base_extends` when the category mainly goes deeper into topics base already has, `new` when base has next to nothing of it. Each with a `scope` and a `reason`.
3. **Subcategories.** For every category, its subcategories. Size them for the target: about {per_sub} approved questions per subcategory, so about {subs} subcategories in total; more where a category has more to ask, at least 2 per category. A subcategory must hold 150 concepts that families who follow the topic know at some level; narrower topics go into a wider neighbour.

## Each subcategory
- `slug`, `en`, `name`: as above; `en` unique within the bundle and different from every base name (a base name only if the bundle''s angle is clear from it: "Football tactics", not "Football").
- `scope`: one English line: what belongs in it, so two neighbours don''t overlap, and how it differs from base''s related subcategories.
- `reason`: one English sentence: why the bundle needs it, what it adds next to base and its neighbours.
- `base_related`: the slugs of base''s subcategories it goes beyond (the drafter keeps away from their questions), from the list below; empty when base has nothing close.
- `samples`: about 10 concepts from easy to hard, each with `known_at`, named in English; none that base''s related subcategories already ask about.

`known_at` (1–15): the lowest point on the difficulty scale at which families who follow the topic know the concept: 1 = a 6-year-old fan knows it, 5 = an 11-year-old, 10 = a 16-year-old with good school knowledge, 12 = most adult fans, 15 = trivia specialists. Spread the samples: a subcategory with only hard concepts or only easy ones is a bad split.

## Rules
- The bundle''s rule decides: every subcategory is on its topic.
- Deeper, not beside: no subcategory that merely renames one of base''s; where base has a subcategory, the bundle''s go into what base leaves out.
- Neighbours don''t overlap: a concept belongs to exactly one subcategory. Say in the scope where a borderline topic goes.
- Players are families in many countries: no subcategory only one country''s families know; a country''s league, team or history only where fans worldwide follow it.
- Mix across the families'' ages: something for children as well as for adults.
- No current party politics, no armed conflict as entertainment, no crime figures as celebrities.
- `scope` and `reason`: English.
```', 'Topic packs (sports, history, movies and so on) need their own list of categories and subcategories, and a model proposes that list from written instructions. These new instructions ask it to go deeper than the main question pool already does on the topic, and to note which main-pool subcategories each new one goes beyond, so later questions don''t repeat the famous ones. The choice is whether to use the instructions as drafted, use them with changes, or have them redrafted; until then the tool refuses to propose a topic pack''s categories.', 'apply as drafted', NULL, '2026-10-21T20:37:56Z', '2026-10-21T21:52:40Z', 1624, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-21, task 1625: Applied as drafted: taxonomy-theme.md unchanged, the guard in qgen taxonomy (THEME_DRAFT) removed, theme proposals now run. No scratch run (no change to the prompt since the draft''s check), no claude calls, no scratch traces. npm run check and qgen validate show failures unrelated to this change (knip electron-builder; demo.json reserve/missing translations).

2026-10-23, task 1649: 2026-10-22: all five theme taxonomies (stem, sports, history, movies, geography) proposed with the theme prompt as drafted and applied (decisions 395, 399, 403, 404, 410); no prompt change was needed.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (371, 'Topic bundles: pace and order of the content runs', 'answered', '["One topic bundle at a time below base and regional runs (−120), order stem, sports, history, geography, movies; the next starts when the previous one''s drafting ends; its translations keep running", "All five at once, one drafting pair each, −120", "Two at a time (stem and sports first), order as in option 1", "None until base''s exit check and plan 84''s regional chains are done", "Your own pace or order (state it)"]', '`plan 188: Content runs for the topic bundles` task 1. Binding: `decision 236: English-first drafting for base: what drives drafting and which locales steer it` (theme sources in en, the rest translated), `decision 267: Bundle tiles: question count strings` (2000 per bundle), `decision 110: Regional packs at launch (RG-5, OQ-46)` (regional chains, plan 84); cost is information only (milestone 9, 2026-10-15).

## Cost (`qgen report --cost`, 2026-10-21)
- No theme bundle exists yet (rows wait on `decision 369: Topic bundles: names, descriptions and rules`), so base''s rates, incl. 24 % overhead:
  - en drafting $0.32 per approved (batches 9–11, $0.26 + overhead)
  - translation es $0.14, es-ES $0.11, de $0.12 per approved
- Per bundle (2000 × en, es, es-ES, de): en ≈ $650, es ≈ $280, es-ES ≈ $225, de ≈ $240 → ≈ $1,400
- All five: ≈ $7,000 (a floor: questions that don''t travel need extra en drafts)
- Same order and pace options cost the same in total; pace only changes when it''s spent

## Drafting and spot checks
- English base batches 9, 10, 11: 874, 866, 836 approved (≈ 860 per batch)
- Per bundle: 2000 / 860 → 3 drafting pairs (run + QC), 3 blind spot checks of 30 questions (90 questions), 3 QC decisions
- All five: 15 pairs, 15 spot checks (450 questions)
- Translations: ≈ 290 approved per run (tr-de-2, tr-en-2, tr-es-ES-2) → ≈ 7 runs per locale per bundle, ≈ 100 runs for all five; no spot check, no QC task
- Agreement so far: batch-9 93 %, batch-10 97 % (bar 90 %)

## Your review time
- Not measured yet: colombia''s QC hasn''t run, spot checks store a date only (`plans/15c-regional-questions.md`, plan 98 task 830)
- Per bundle: 3 × 30-question spot checks + 3 QC decisions; all five: 15 of each

## What''s open ahead of it
- Base (`plan 17: Batch runs`), `qgen report --exit-check` fails: 4672 / 5000 approved, 10 difficulty levels short (level 1: 187/250, 15: 67/125), batch-10/11 drop rate, batch-11 no spot check yet (`task 1580: QC batch-11` open), `qgen validate` 31 errors; remaining base projection ≈ $1,850 (551 en drafts + translations)
- Plan 84: translation runs `task 639: run tr-en-2`, `task 641: run tr-de-2`, `task 1223: run tr-es-1`, `task 1226: run tr-es-ES-2`, `task 1228: run tr-es-2` open; colombia pilot pair `task 1295` / `task 1296` open; start tasks for usa, germany, uk, spain, mexico (`task 1268`–`task 1272`) pending; regional projection ≈ $4,900 (colombia es ≈ $550 + en ≈ $250, the other five ≈ $830 each)
- Plan 184 still open: `task 1625: Theme taxonomy prompt: apply per the developer''s answer`, `task 1630: End-to-end check on a throw-away theme bundle`; plan 186''s taxonomies not applied

## Recommendation: option 1
- Total cost and spot checks are the same for every pace; one at a time keeps your queue at one theme spot check at a time next to base''s and colombia''s
- `stem` is the first real theme run of an untested chain (task 1630 open): what its QC finds fixes the other four before they start
- Translations of a finished bundle keep running, so the machine side stays busy
- Order: `stem`, `sports`, `history`, `geography`, `movies` (movies last: low media share, `task 1631: Media for movies and sports: axes template and docs`)', 'The game is getting five optional question packs (science and technology, sports, history, film, geography), each to be filled with 2000 questions in English, two kinds of Spanish and German. Each pack needs about three English writing runs, each checked by a person on a blind sample of 30 questions, then machine translation; they compete with the main pack and the country packs for machine cost (about $1,400 per pack) and the developer''s review time. The answer sets how many packs are worked on at once and in what order.', 'One topic bundle at a time below base and regional runs (−120), order stem, sports, history, geography, movies; the next starts when the previous one''s drafting ends; its translations keep running', NULL, '2026-10-21T20:47:26Z', '2026-10-21T21:54:08Z', 1659, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-21, task 1659: Staggered, one bundle at a time at −120. Kept: `task 1660: Topic runs stem: start` (priority unchanged, −120). Dropped, each re-added (same body, its own bundle, −120) by the closing drafting QC of the previous bundle: `task 1661: Topic runs sports: start` (after stem), `task 1662: Topic runs history: start` (after sports), `task 1664: Topic runs geography: start` (after history), `task 1663: Topic runs movies: start` (after geography). A finished bundle''s translation runs keep running alongside the next bundle''s drafting.

2026-10-21, task 1660: Topic bundles run one at a time at priority -120 in order stem, sports, history, geography, movies. Start tasks for sports, history, movies, geography (tasks 1661-1664) were dropped; the closing drafting QC of each bundle re-adds the next bundle''s start task. Translations of a bundle keep running alongside. Stem started: first pair tasks 1676, 1677.');
