  - `latin-american-independence` Latin American independence: `history`
  - `pacific-peoples` Oceania and Pacific peoples: `islands`
- `early-modern-world` Early modern world (5, base_extends)
  - `renaissance-and-reformation` Renaissance and Reformation: `art`, `world-religions`
  - `ottoman-empire` Ottoman Empire: `history`
  - `colonial-empires` Colonial empires and trading companies: `explorers-and-discoveries`, `pirates`
  - `imperial-russia` Tsars and imperial Russia: `history`
  - `travellers-and-expeditions` Travellers and expeditions: `explorers-and-discoveries`, `amazon-history-and-exploration`
- `rulers-and-courts` Rulers and courts (3, base_extends)
  - `queens-and-empresses` Queens and empresses: `british-royals`, `pharaohs`, `french-revolution`
  - `royal-houses-of-europe` Royal houses of Europe: `british-royals`
  - `royal-titles-and-court-life` Royal titles and court life: `british-royals`
- `age-of-revolutions-and-industry` Revolutions and the 19th century (5, base_extends)
  - `age-of-revolutions` Age of revolutions: `french-revolution`, `us-presidents`
  - `napoleons-europe` Napoleon''s Europe: `french-revolution`
  - `unifications-and-new-nations` Unifications and new nations: `history`
  - `industrial-age-life` Life in the Industrial Revolution: `history`
  - `slavery-and-abolition` Slavery and abolition: `us-presidents`
- `twentieth-century` 20th century (5, base_extends)
  - `first-world-war` First World War: `history`
  - `between-the-wars` Between the world wars: `world-war-ii`
  - `home-fronts` Home fronts of the world wars: `world-war-ii`
  - `independence-movements` Independence movements: `cold-war`
  - `votes-and-civil-rights` Votes and civil rights: `famous-quotes`
- `everyday-life-in-the-past` Everyday life in the past (7, new)
  - `childhood-in-the-past` Childhood and school in the past: `toys`
  - `jobs-of-the-past` Jobs of the past: `history`
  - `trade-routes` Trade routes and markets: `history-of-money`, `explorers-and-discoveries`
  - `writing-and-records` Writing, scripts and records: `languages-of-the-world`, `pharaohs`
  - `archaeology-and-great-finds` Archaeology and great finds: `prehistory`, `mummies`, `explorers-and-discoveries`, `history`
  - `homes-and-towns-of-the-past` Homes and towns of the past: `castles`, `architectural-styles`
  - `plagues-fires-and-famines` Plagues, fires and famines: `volcanoes`, `earthquakes`, `medicine-and-health`
- `maps-and-borders` Historical maps and borders (3, new)
  - `old-place-names` Old place names: `countries`, `capital-cities`
  - `vanished-countries` Vanished countries: `countries`, `cold-war`
  - `maps-borders-and-treaties` Maps, borders and treaties: `cold-war`, `continents`', 'The new history question pack needs its own list of topics, so its questions go deeper than the main pool on every era and every part of the world, not only Europe, without repeating what the main pool, the country packs or the other topic packs already ask. The proposal splits the pack into 11 topics and 55 subtopics, about 36 questions each at 2000 questions. The choice is to apply it as proposed, apply it with changes, or have it redone; applying fixes the pack''s structure and drafts the Spanish and German topic names.', 'Apply as proposed (with the hand edits listed)', NULL, '2026-10-22T01:37:06Z', '2026-10-22T02:11:31Z', 1647, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-22, task 1647: Applied 2026-10-22 with `qgen taxonomy --bundle history --apply work/taxonomy/history/proposal.json` (hand-edited proposal): `app/data/bundles/history/categories.json` 11 categories, 55 subcategories; names.{en,es,de}.json drafted (es hand fixes: early-modern-world «Edad Moderna», plagues «Epidemias, incendios y hambrunas», home-fronts «La retaguardia en las guerras mundiales»); `authoring/data/bundles/history/base-related.json` 55 of 55; `plans/22-bundles.md` history row taxonomy cell. `task 1648: movies taxonomy` checks against this list. Ready for `qgen batch --bundle history` (content-runs plan).

2026-10-23, task 1649: 2026-10-22, task 1649: the geography taxonomy (decision 410) was checked against this applied list; boundary scopes written into geography''s subcategories, nothing changed in this bundle.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (404, 'movies taxonomy: categories and subcategories', 'answered', '["Apply as proposed (with the hand edits listed)", "Apply with changes (name them: merges, drops, scopes)", "Redo the proposal (say what to change)"]', 'Bundle `movies` (Movies & Series questions bundle, `kind: theme`, `languages: ["en"]`, target 2000 ≈ 38 per subcategory at 53; `task 1644: Create the five topic bundles`). Scope film and TV series per `decision 369: Topic bundles: names, descriptions and rules` (name and description say "movies and series").

Rule (`app/data/bundles.json`): More and deeper questions on film and TV series: films and series of every era and country, live action and animation, genres, directors, actors, characters, quotes, soundtracks, awards and screen history at every difficulty, from kids to adults, answerable by families anywhere in the world whatever their language; fan-level detail is welcome, while the famous core base already asks stays in `base`, and things only one country''s players know stay in that country''s regional bundle (`usa`, `spain`, …); reality TV and news stay in `base`.

No voice: base''s voice holds (`plan 184: Pipeline for topic bundles`).

Proposal (`qgen taxonomy --bundle movies`, theme prompt of `decision 370: Theme taxonomy prompt: apply?`; opus 1 call, $0.82): 11 categories (7 `base_extends`, 4 `new`: `screen-history`, `world-cinema`, `directors-and-stars`, `screen-music`), 53 subcategories, each with `base_related` (base subcategories it goes beyond; drafting keeps off their questions, `decision 350: Overlap rate in drafting: which changes?`). es/de names drafted on apply. Full proposal with scopes, reasons and samples (`known_at`): `work/taxonomy/movies/proposal.md` (before the hand edits); edited `proposal.json` (`proposal.orig.json` beside it is the unedited one).

Checks and hand edits:
- Base overlap: base''s `film-tv-and-superheroes` has 18 subcategories, 185 of 455 approved (`film` 26 and saturated, `tv-series` 20, `disney-films` 17, `pixar-films` 24, `anime` 26, `cartoons` 25, `james-bond` 31, `harry-potter` 32, `the-lord-of-the-rings` 25, `oscars` 20 …); the proposal''s subcategories go past that core ("deep cuts", "on screen", records) and name it in `base_related`. `reality-tv` and `telenovelas-and-soaps` stay base''s (scopes say so). Hand edit: the 6 subcategories without a related base subcategory (`european-cinema`, `east-asian-cinema`, `indian-cinema`, `westerns`, `child-stars`, `animal-stars`) get base''s general `film` (the last two `tv-series` too). All `base_related` slugs exist.
- Slugs: unique; none equals a base slug. Hand edit: `tv-shows` → `series` and `sitcoms` → `comedy-series` (`usa` has `tv-shows`, `uk` has `sitcoms`).
- Regional bundles (`usa` `tv-shows`, `kids-tv`, `movie-favorites`; `uk` `sitcoms`, `british-films`, `soaps-and-dramas`, `childrens-tv`; `spain` `spanish-cinema`, `tv-series`; `mexico` `golden-age-cinema`, `contemporary-cinema`; `germany` `series-and-crime`, `films-and-actors`; `colombia` `cinema`, `tv-shows-and-series`): drafting''s overlap wiring only sees base, so the boundary rests on the rule and scopes; hand edits: 10 scopes near regional ground (comedy, drama, detective, family and teen, international series, European and Spanish/Latin American cinema, golden-age Hollywood, 80s–90s hits, kids'' cartoons) say "one country''s detail its players alone know stays in that country''s regional bundle"; samples dropped: Fawlty Towers, Line of Duty, Midsomer Murders, I Love Lucy, Saved by the Bell, The West Wing, Cantinflas, Pinewood Studios. World-known films and series of one country stay here (Almodóvar, Money Heist, Dark, Mr. Bean, Wallace & Gromit).
- `stem` (applied, `decision 395: stem taxonomy: categories and subcategories`; `light-and-optics`, `sound-and-waves`, `electronics-and-gadgets`): hand edit: `filmmaking` leaves the physics and engineering of cameras, screens and sound to `stem`.
- `sports` (applied, `decision 399: sports taxonomy: categories and subcategories`): by its rule films about sport are `movies`'' (Rocky, Eye of the Tiger stay here); no sport subcategory.
- `history` (applied, `decision 403: history taxonomy: categories and subcategories`): no film subcategory there; hand edit: `screen-history` says the real events a film shows are history''s, the question asks about the film.
- `geography` (not yet applied): hand edit: `filming-locations` tests the screen link; the place itself is geography''s.
- Kids level (lowest sample `known_at`): 19 of 53 at ≤ 4 (Peppa Pig, Bluey, Big Bird, Minions, E.T., Home Alone, Wizard of Oz, Chaplin, Frankenstein''s monster, Paddington, Jackie Chan, clapperboard …), 19 at 5–6, 9 at 7–8, 6 at 9+ (`crime-and-thrillers`, `bond-deep-cuts`, `drama-series`, `detective-series`, `oscar-records`, `filming-locations`): every difficulty has subcategories to draw from.
- Free media (`authoring/data/bundles/movies/question-axes.json`: photos of people, places and objects from Commons; never stills, posters or footage): 22 subcategories marked "media" below have them as their subject (directors, actors, composers, authors, animal performers, locations, studios, festivals, statuettes, props and cars in museums, film equipment); the other 31 (characters, plots, animation, quotes, series, franchises) are text-only, with an actor''s or creator''s portrait now and then.
- Size: 53, near the planned ≈ 50; merges if wanted: `star-trek` into `blockbuster-sagas`, `child-stars` into `modern-film-stars`, `classic-directors` and `modern-directors` into one, `new-hollywood` into `golden-age-hollywood`.

- `screen-history` Screen history (4, new)
  - `silent-cinema` Silent cinema: `film` — media
  - `golden-age-hollywood` Hollywood''s golden age: `film`
  - `new-hollywood` New Hollywood and the 1970s: `film`
  - `eighties-and-nineties-hits` 80s and 90s hits: `film`
- `world-cinema` World cinema (4, new)
  - `european-cinema` European cinema: `film` — media
  - `east-asian-cinema` Japanese, Korean and Chinese cinema: `film` — media
  - `indian-cinema` Indian cinema: `film` — media
  - `spanish-and-latin-american-cinema` Spanish and Latin American cinema: `telenovelas-and-soaps` — media
- `film-genres` Film genres (5, base_extends)
  - `action-and-martial-arts` Action and martial arts films: `film` — media
  - `comedy-and-romcoms` Comedies and romcoms: `film`
  - `westerns` Westerns: `film` — media
  - `crime-and-thrillers` Crime films and thrillers: `crime-fiction`
  - `movie-musicals-and-dance` Movie musicals and dance films: `musicals`
- `fantastic-cinema` Fantastic cinema (3, base_extends)
  - `sci-fi-films` Sci-fi film deep cuts: `science-fiction`
  - `horror-and-monsters` Monsters and horror deep cuts: `horror-films`, `famous-villains`
  - `fantasy-adventure-films` Fantasy adventure films: `fantasy`
- `franchises` Franchises and sagas (8, base_extends)
  - `star-wars` Star Wars galaxy: `science-fiction`
  - `star-trek` Star Trek: `science-fiction`
  - `marvel-cinematic-universe` Marvel Cinematic Universe: `marvel-superheroes`, `famous-villains`
  - `dc-on-screen` DC on screen: `dc-superheroes`, `famous-villains`
  - `wizarding-world` Wizarding World deep cuts: `harry-potter`
  - `middle-earth-on-screen` Middle-earth on screen: `the-lord-of-the-rings` — media
  - `bond-deep-cuts` Bond deep cuts: `james-bond` — media
  - `blockbuster-sagas` Blockbuster sagas: `film`, `science-fiction`
- `animation` Animation (8, base_extends)
  - `disney-animation-deep-cuts` Disney animation deep cuts: `disney-films`
  - `pixar-deep-cuts` Pixar deep cuts: `pixar-films`
  - `other-animation-studios` DreamWorks, Aardman and more: `cartoons`
  - `ghibli-and-anime-films` Studio Ghibli and anime films: `anime`
  - `anime-series` Anime series: `anime`
  - `classic-cartoons` Classic cartoons: `cartoons`
  - `kids-tv-cartoons` Kids'' TV cartoons: `cartoons`
  - `adult-animation` The Simpsons and adult animation: `cartoons`
- `series` TV series (6, base_extends)
  - `comedy-series` Sitcoms: `tv-series`
  - `drama-series` Drama series: `tv-series`
  - `fantasy-and-sci-fi-series` Fantasy and sci-fi series: `tv-series`, `science-fiction`, `fantasy`
  - `detective-series` Detective series: `crime-fiction`, `tv-series`
  - `family-and-teen-series` Family and teen series: `tv-series`
  - `international-series` International series hits: `tv-series`, `telenovelas-and-soaps`
- `directors-and-stars` Directors and stars (5, new)
  - `classic-directors` Classic film directors: `film` — media
  - `modern-directors` Today''s film directors: `film` — media
  - `classic-film-stars` Classic film stars: `film` — media
  - `modern-film-stars` Today''s film stars: `film` — media
  - `child-stars` Child stars: `film`, `tv-series` — media
- `screen-icons` Quotes and animal stars (2, base_extends)
  - `movie-quotes` Movie quotes: `famous-quotes`
  - `animal-stars` Animal stars: `film`, `tv-series` — media
- `screen-music` Screen music (2, new)
  - `film-scores` Film scores and composers: `classical-music` — media
  - `screen-songs` Songs from films and TV: `pop-music`, `disney-films`
- `awards-and-industry` Awards and filmmaking (6, base_extends)
  - `oscar-records` Oscar records and trivia: `oscars` — media
  - `festivals-and-awards` Festivals and other awards: `oscars` — media
  - `filmmaking` How films are made: `technology` — media
  - `studios-and-box-office` Studios and box office: `famous-companies`, `famous-logos` — media
  - `book-to-screen` From book to screen: `literature`, `childrens-books` — media
  - `filming-locations` Filming locations: `famous-landmarks` — media', 'The new movies and series question pack needs its own list of topics, so its questions go deeper than the main pool on films and TV of every era and country, without repeating what the main pool, the country packs or the other topic packs already ask. The proposal splits the pack into 11 topics and 53 subtopics, about 38 questions each at 2000 questions, and says which subtopics can use free photos. The choice is to apply it as proposed, apply it with changes, or have it redone; applying fixes the pack''s structure and drafts the Spanish and German topic names.', 'Apply as proposed (with the hand edits listed)', NULL, '2026-10-22T02:21:24Z', '2026-10-22T02:23:45Z', 1648, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-22, task 1648: Applied 2026-10-22 as proposed with the hand edits listed: `qgen taxonomy --bundle movies --apply` wrote app/data/bundles/movies/categories.json (11 categories, 53 subcategories), names.{en,es,de}.json (es/de drafted), authoring/data/bundles/movies/base-related.json (53 of 53 go beyond base); plans/22-bundles.md movies row taxonomy cell filled. `task 1649: geography taxonomy` checks against this list (filming-locations tests the screen link, the place is geography''s).

2026-10-23, task 1649: 2026-10-22, task 1649: the geography taxonomy (decision 410) was checked against this applied list; boundary scopes written into geography''s subcategories, nothing changed in this bundle.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (405, 'QC batch-11: which changes?', 'answered', '["recommended set: proposal 1", "all", "none"]', 'Batch-11 QC (`authoring/reports/batch-11.md`): agreement 28/30 (93 %; batch-9 28/30, batch-10 29/30), release bar ✓. The two disagreements are single, with no shared problem kind (q-4315 false approve: trivial subject, difficulty only from style; q-4370 false needs_work). Audit: 2 major findings on approved questions, both giveaway (q-4907 description ''original alarm clock'' points to morning; q-4925 description ''always goes sideways'' names the crab''s best-known trait), which meets the threshold of 2.

Proposal 1 (`review.md` / `draft.md`): the description must not state the answer''s defining trait or the clue that rules out wrong options (check the description against the options alone, before the hints). Subject to `task 1251: Audit: hint giveaways are minor unless they name the answer (QC batch-8 120 D)`.

Note: batch health is ✗ on drop rate (21.1 % vs 20 %), as batch-9 and batch-10 (both acked); the next `qgen batch` stops until `qgen health ack batch-11`.', 'After each batch of new trivia questions we compare the developer''s blind spot check with the automated reviewers and propose fixes for recurring problems. This time the spot check agreed 28 of 30 (93 %), and one problem kind came up often enough in the second reader''s audit to justify a proposal. The choice is whether to turn that proposal into work before the next batch, or to skip it.', 'recommended set: proposal 1', NULL, '2026-10-22T02:21:58Z', '2026-10-22T02:24:47Z', 1580, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-23, task 1681: change from proposal 1 already done by task 1682 (description rule, decision 407).

2026-10-24, task 639: QC batch-11''s recommended proposal 1 was applied before; no change for tr-en-2.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (406, 'New menu open/close sounds: OK on the TV?', 'pending', '["Approve", "Changes: …"]', 'Listen with TRIVIA_SOUND=1, at /?efectos («menú abierto» / «menú cerrado»), or by opening and closing the Esc menu in a game. Now installed: Kenney Interface Sounds maximize_008 / minimize_008 (CC0), 0.23 s, pitch ≈350 Hz (old pair played at 0.85: ≈2.8 / 3.4 kHz). Alternative, staged in work/menu-sounds/ (menu-abre.m4a, menu-cierra.m4a; copy over app/client/public/audio/ to try): maximize_006 / minimize_006 trimmed to 0.32 s, a longer sweep, ≈310 Hz open / ≈500 Hz close.', 'The sounds played when the in-game menu or a dialog opens and closes were too high-pitched, so they were replaced by a lower, softer matched pair: a short rising note on opening and a falling one on closing, around 350 Hz instead of about 3 kHz. Measurements say they are lower, but only listening on the TV can say whether they are pleasant. If they are not right, an alternative pair is ready to compare.', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-22T03:16:31Z', NULL, 1678, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (407, 'Prompt wording: the description must not give away the answer''s defining trait', 'answered', '["apply as drafted", "apply with changes", "don''t apply"]', 'Proposed wording (applies to the prompts under authoring/tools/prompts/):

1. house-style.md, section Description (read by the drafter), append to the first paragraph''s ''must not give the answer away or rule out options (…)'': ''It also must not state the answer''s defining trait or the clue that rules out wrong options: test it against the four options alone, before any hint (a crab question must not say "always goes sideways"; a question about the first call to prayer must not say "the original alarm clock"). Playing on the topic''s theme without naming its trait is fine.''

2. review.md, Decide reason 3, extend to: ''3. Something gives the answer away: the description, the question text, hint 1 or 2, or the media. For the description, read it with the four options only, before the hints: if it states the answer''s defining trait or rules out options, it is a giveaway (the fix rewrites it to play on the theme without the trait).''

Consistent with task 1251: Audit: hint giveaways are minor unless they name the answer (hint 3 may come close; a hint that names the answer stays the major case); this only concerns the description.
Source: QC batch-11, audit findings q-4907 and q-4925 (decision 405: QC batch-11: which changes?).', 'The funny one-line teaser shown before each quiz question sometimes states the answer''s best-known trait, or hints at what rules out the wrong options. Two approved questions in the latest batch did this (a crab teased as always going sideways, a call to prayer teased as an alarm clock). The question writer''s and the reviewer''s instructions would get one extra sentence each so such teasers are caught, and the answer decides whether that wording is applied.', 'apply as drafted', NULL, '2026-10-22T05:10:58Z', '2026-10-22T21:27:55Z', 1682, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-22, task 1682: Description rule applied in authoring/tools/prompts/house-style.md (Description section, read by the drafter) and review.md (Decide reason 3): the description must not state the answer''s defining trait or the clue that rules out options; the review reads it against the four options alone, before the hints. Hints naming the answer stay the major case (task 1251). Already-approved questions are not re-audited.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (408, 'batch-12: health stop, ack?', 'answered', '["Acknowledge (qgen health ack batch-12) and continue", "Keep the stop, change something first"]', 'batch-12 fails the batch health thresholds: drop rate, merged/slots 11–15, top lens, media share, mean fun.
See `qgen report --batches` or authoring/reports/batches.md. The developer looks at it and clears the stop with: qgen health ack batch-12 "<note>"

(no claude calls in this run: the stop came before any step)', 'The question generator refuses to start a new batch because the previous batch scored below the quality thresholds on several measures. Someone has to look at the results and either acknowledge them, so generation can continue, or decide what to change first. Acknowledging lets the next batch run; otherwise generation stays stopped.', 'Acknowledge (qgen health ack batch-12) and continue', NULL, '2026-10-22T05:11:13Z', '2026-10-23T01:05:57Z', 1680, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-23, task 1681: nothing to act on; ack is the developer''s. Batch-14 health ✗ raised as decision 416.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (409, 'Spot check batch-12 done?', 'answered', '["Done: agreement ≥ 90 %", "Done: agreement below 90 %"]', 'Colombia batch-12 (colombia bundle, Colombian Spanish). Open http://127.0.0.1:8001/review?batch=batch-12&sample=1 (run trivia-authoring), judge the 30 sampled questions blind, and answer with the agreement (≥ 90 % is the bar, decision 15). Batch numbers: approved 239, needs_work 14, dropped by pipeline 85, $69.26 at list price (about $0.29 per approved). Not to be answered by an LLM.', 'A new batch of 253 quiz questions about Colombia was written and 239 passed the automatic checks. Before they count as good, someone who speaks Colombian Spanish natively has to read a sample of 30 and say whether they hold up. The answer decides whether the batch is accepted or needs changes.', 'Done: agreement ≥ 90 %', NULL, '2026-10-22T05:13:05Z', '2026-10-23T00:08:23Z', 1296, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-23, task 1681: spot check batch-12 agreement ≥ 90 %: no change task.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (410, 'geography taxonomy: categories and subcategories', 'answered', '["Apply as proposed (with the hand edits listed)", "Apply with changes (name them: merges, drops, scopes)", "Redo the proposal (say what to change)"]', 'Bundle `geography` (Geography questions bundle, `kind: theme`, `languages: ["en"]`, target 2000 = 40 per subcategory at 50; `task 1644: Create the five topic bundles`). Names and description per `decision 369: Topic bundles: names, descriptions and rules`.

Rule (`app/data/bundles.json`): present-day places: countries, cities, maps, flags, borders, landforms, waters, climate zones, more and deeper than base at every difficulty; historical borders, empires and old place names are `history`''s; geology, weather physics and ecology `stem`''s; one country''s detail its players alone know stays in that country''s regional bundle.

No voice: base''s voice holds (`plan 184: Pipeline for topic bundles`).

Proposal (`qgen taxonomy --bundle geography`, theme prompt of `decision 370: Theme taxonomy prompt: apply?`; opus 1 call, $0.45): 9 categories (7 `base_extends`, 2 `new`: `maps-and-borders`, `people-and-places`), 50 subcategories, each with `base_related` (base subcategories it goes beyond; drafting keeps off their questions, `decision 350: Overlap rate in drafting: which changes?`). es/de names drafted on apply. Full proposal with scopes, reasons and samples (`known_at`): `work/taxonomy/geography/proposal.md` (before the hand edits); edited `proposal.json` (`proposal.orig.json` beside it is the unedited one).

Checks and hand edits:
- Base overlap: base''s `world-geography` has 8 subcategories, 108 of 202 approved in en (`continents` 16, `countries` 22, `capital-cities` 31, `flags` 18, `rivers` 20, `mountains` 28, `deserts` 24, `islands` 21), plus `planet-earth`''s `the-sea`, `oceans`, `volcanoes`, `natures-records`, `weather-and-climate`; base asks the famous core (best-known countries, capitals, flags, rivers, peaks), the proposal splits it by region and goes to every country, capital, range, river, sea and island. Hand edit: the 12 subcategories without a related base subcategory get the nearest base one (`states-and-provinces`, `country-codes`, `enclaves-and-odd-borders`, `population-records`, `demonyms`, `place-name-meanings` → `countries`; `megacities`, `city-nicknames` → `capital-cities`; `national-emblems` → `flags`; `time-zones` → `planet-earth`; `map-reading` → `continents`; `peninsulas-and-capes` → `the-sea`). All `base_related` slugs exist.
- Slugs: unique; none equals a base slug. Hand edit: `national-symbols` → `national-emblems` (`usa`, `spain`, `mexico`, `colombia` have `national-symbols`), `national-parks` → `world-national-parks` (`usa` has `national-parks`).
- Regional bundles (`usa`, `uk`, `germany`, `spain`, `mexico`, `colombia` each have their own geography and symbols): drafting''s overlap wiring only sees base, so the boundary rests on the rule and scopes; hand edit: `states-and-provinces`, `national-emblems`, `world-national-parks`, `city-nicknames`, `famous-neighbourhoods` say "one country''s detail its players alone know stays in that country''s regional bundle".
- `stem` (applied, `decision 395: stem taxonomy: categories and subcategories`; `plate-tectonics`, `weather-science`, `climate-and-atmosphere`, `ocean-science`, `ecosystems-and-food-webs`, `conservation`): the proposal''s scopes already leave eruption science, how weather works and desert ecology to `stem`; hand edits: `forests-and-grasslands` leaves ecosystems and ecology to `stem`, `world-national-parks` conservation science.
- `history` (applied, `decision 403: history taxonomy: categories and subcategories`; `old-place-names`, `vanished-countries`, `maps-borders-and-treaties`, `travellers-and-expeditions`): `place-name-meanings` already leaves former names to `history`; hand edits: `borders-and-neighbours` and `enclaves-and-odd-borders` are today''s borders only, `map-reading` leaves the history of mapmaking, `polar-regions` polar expeditions to `history`.
- `movies` (applied, `decision 404: movies taxonomy: categories and subcategories`; `filming-locations` tests the screen link): hand edit: `natural-wonders` says a place asked for a film shot there is `movies`''.
- `sports` (applied, `decision 399: sports taxonomy: categories and subcategories`): no overlap; `country-codes` asks Olympic codes as country codes, not the Games.
- Kids level (lowest sample `known_at`): 25 of 50 at ≤ 4 (Madagascar, Italy''s boot shape, Japan''s red circle, the Equator, a globe, Niagara Falls, the South Pole, rainforest …), 20 at 5–6, 5 at 7–8 (`capitals-of-the-americas`, `port-cities`, `high-peaks`, `plateaus-passes-and-valleys`, `rivers-of-africa-asia-and-oceania`), samples up to `known_at` 14: every difficulty has subcategories to draw from.
- Size: 50, as planned.

- `countries-by-region` Countries by region (7, base_extends)
  - `countries-of-africa` Countries of Africa: `countries`, `continents`
  - `countries-of-asia` Countries of Asia: `countries`, `continents`
  - `countries-of-europe` Countries of Europe: `countries`, `continents`
  - `countries-of-the-americas` Countries of the Americas: `countries`, `continents`
  - `oceania-and-the-pacific` Oceania and the Pacific: `countries`, `continents`
  - `microstates-and-territories` Microstates and territories: `countries`
  - `states-and-provinces` States and provinces: `countries`
- `capitals-and-cities` Capitals and cities (8, base_extends)
  - `capitals-of-africa` Capitals of Africa: `capital-cities`
  - `capitals-of-asia-and-oceania` Capitals of Asia and Oceania: `capital-cities`
  - `capitals-of-europe` Capitals of Europe: `capital-cities`
  - `capitals-of-the-americas` Capitals of the Americas: `capital-cities`
  - `megacities` Megacities: `capital-cities`
  - `city-nicknames` City nicknames: `capital-cities`
  - `port-cities` Port cities: `the-sea`
  - `famous-neighbourhoods` Famous neighbourhoods: `famous-landmarks`
- `flags-and-symbols` Flags and symbols (5, base_extends)
  - `flag-designs` Flag designs: `flags`
  - `lookalike-flags` Lookalike flags: `flags`
  - `regional-flags` Regional flags: `flags`
  - `national-emblems` National emblems: `flags`
  - `country-codes` Country codes: `countries`
- `maps-and-borders` Maps and borders (6, new)
  - `borders-and-neighbours` Borders and neighbours: `countries`
  - `enclaves-and-odd-borders` Enclaves and odd borders: `countries`
  - `latitude-and-longitude` Latitude and longitude: `planet-earth`
  - `time-zones` Time zones: `planet-earth`
  - `map-reading` Map reading: `continents`
  - `extreme-points` Extreme points: `natures-records`
- `mountains-and-highlands` Mountains and highlands (4, base_extends)
  - `mountain-ranges` Mountain ranges: `mountains`
  - `high-peaks` High peaks: `mountains`, `natures-records`
  - `volcanic-peaks` Volcanic peaks: `volcanoes`
  - `plateaus-passes-and-valleys` Plateaus, passes and valleys: `mountains`
- `rivers-and-lakes` Rivers and lakes (4, base_extends)
  - `rivers-of-europe-and-the-americas` Rivers of Europe and the Americas: `rivers`
  - `rivers-of-africa-asia-and-oceania` Rivers of Africa, Asia and Oceania: `rivers`
  - `lakes` Lakes of the world: `natures-records`
  - `waterfalls-and-canyons` Waterfalls and canyons: `natures-records`
- `seas-coasts-and-islands` Seas, coasts and islands (5, base_extends)
  - `seas-and-gulfs` Seas and gulfs: `oceans`, `the-sea`
  - `straits-and-canals` Straits and canals: `the-sea`
  - `peninsulas-and-capes` Peninsulas and capes: `the-sea`
  - `islands-of-the-pacific-and-indian-ocean` Islands of the Pacific and Indian Ocean: `islands`
  - `islands-of-the-atlantic-and-europe` Islands of the Atlantic and Europe: `islands`
- `landscapes-and-climate-zones` Landscapes and climate zones (7, base_extends)
  - `deserts-of-the-world` Deserts of the world: `deserts`
  - `climate-zones` Climate zones: `weather-and-climate`
  - `polar-regions` Polar regions: `continents`
  - `forests-and-grasslands` Forests and grasslands: `amazon-plants`
  - `natural-wonders` Natural wonders: `natural-phenomena`, `natures-records`
  - `world-national-parks` National parks of the world: `african-wildlife`
  - `climate-extremes-by-place` Climate extremes by place: `natures-records`, `weather-and-climate`
- `people-and-places` People and places (4, new)
  - `population-records` Population records: `countries`
  - `demonyms` Demonyms: `countries`
  - `official-languages` Official languages: `languages-of-the-world`
  - `place-name-meanings` Place name meanings: `countries`', 'The new geography question pack needs its own list of topics, so its questions go deeper than the main pool on today''s countries, cities, flags, maps, mountains, rivers, seas and climates, without repeating what the main pool, the country packs or the other topic packs already ask. The proposal splits the pack into 9 topics and 50 subtopics, about 40 questions each at 2000 questions. The choice is to apply it as proposed, apply it with changes, or have it redone; applying fixes the pack''s structure and drafts the Spanish and German topic names.', 'Apply as proposed (with the hand edits listed)', NULL, '2026-10-22T05:19:10Z', '2026-10-23T00:11:30Z', 1649, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-23, task 1649: Applied 2026-10-22 as proposed with the hand edits listed: `qgen taxonomy --bundle geography --apply work/taxonomy/geography/proposal.json` wrote app/data/bundles/geography/categories.json (9 categories, 50 subcategories), names.{en,es,de}.json (es/de drafted), authoring/data/bundles/geography/base-related.json (50 of 50 go beyond base); plans/22-bundles.md geography row taxonomy cell. All five topic bundles now have an applied taxonomy; geography is ready for `qgen batch --bundle geography` (content-runs plan).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (411, 'Continue run batch-<n> (stem): batch-12 health stop, ack?', 'answered', '["Ack batch-12 (qgen health ack batch-12 \"<note>\") and continue", "Do not ack; investigate batch-12 first"]', '`nix develop -c qgen batch --bundle stem` exited 2 at once (after acking batch-11 per `decision 401: batch-11: health stop, ack? (blocks run batch-<n> (stem))`). Last output:

```
batch-12 fails the batch health thresholds: drop rate, merged/slots 11–15, top lens, media share, mean fun.
See `qgen report --batches` or authoring/reports/batches.md. The developer looks at it and clears the stop with: qgen health ack batch-12 "<note>"
```

The ack of batch-11 (`authoring/data/health-acks.json`, `authoring/reports/batches.md`) is left unstaged in the work tree.', 'The question-making tool again refuses to start a new set of questions, now because the latest batch (batch-12) missed several quality thresholds: too many drafts lost, uneven coverage, a low share of questions with media, and a low average fun rating. Only the developer can clear the stop after looking at the numbers. Until then, the first batch for the science, technology, engineering and maths pack cannot start.', 'Duplicate of decision 408: batch-12 health stop acked (107adcbf)', NULL, '2026-10-22T05:19:55Z', '2026-10-23T01:46:39Z', 1676, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (412, 'Should pm be changed so it never overwrites tasks.sql with fewer rows or a missing file?', 'answered', '["Make the change in pm as written in the write-up", "Only keep the warning in README, no pm change"]', 'Write-up: `plans/pm-tasks-sql-safety.md`. Cause: a missing `tasks.sql` is loaded as an empty project and written back at once.', 'The tool that keeps the project''s work list in one file once replaced that file with an empty one, because it looked at the file at the moment another program was rewriting it. The fix has to be made in the tool itself, which lives outside this project. The choice is whether to make that change (or ask for it) now, or to live with a written warning about which operations to avoid while the tool runs.', 'i ordered the change to pm elsewhere. nothing do here. delete the file `pm-tasks-sql-safety.md`', NULL, '2026-10-23T00:11:18Z', '2026-10-23T00:13:07Z', 1689, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (413, 'batch-12: health stop, ack?', 'answered', '["Ack batch-12 (qgen health ack batch-12 \"<note>\"), then rerun", "Keep the stop"]', 'Task 1690 `qgen batch --bundle colombia` exit 2. Output verbatim:

batch-12 fails the batch health thresholds: drop rate, merged/slots 11–15, top lens, media share, mean fun.
See `qgen report --batches` or authoring/reports/batches.md. The developer looks at it and clears the stop with: qgen health ack batch-12 "<note>"

(No claude: line: no calls made.)', 'The latest batch of Colombian questions came out below several quality thresholds (how many drafts were dropped, how many survived, topic spread, share of questions with pictures, average fun rating). The next batch will not start until the developer looks at the numbers and clears the stop. Choose whether to clear it and carry on, or to keep the stop.', 'Duplicate of decision 408 (same batch-12 health stop); task 1690 waits on 408', NULL, '2026-10-23T00:13:22Z', '2026-10-23T01:01:53Z', 1690, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (414, 'Human reviews don''t gate question generation or translation', 'answered', '[]', 'Developer, 2026-10-23. Health ✗, spot checks and QC proposals stay and are raised as pm decisions, but no task is ever linked to (waits on) them. The release bar (trivia-release-check) is the developer''s own gate, not a pm task. Partly supersedes D-55 / decision 13 (health stop), decision 313 (translation stop), QC.md step 5 ''Next batch waits''.', 'The developer trusts the automatic question writing and translation. Quality warnings, spot checks and change proposals still come to the developer as questions, but nothing waits for the answers; the work keeps going and the answers feed improvements later. The only human gate is the final release.', 'Review decisions are raised but block no task; health ✗ never stops a run; only the developer''s final release check gates', NULL, '2026-10-23T01:09:18Z', '2026-10-23T01:09:18Z', NULL, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (415, 'Back-audit: run the remaining slices?', 'answered', '["all: audit every remaining pre-D-56 question, slices of 200", "only batch-7", "stop: no more slices"]', 'Pilot slice `backaudit-1` (`task 339: Back-audit pilot slice (100) and cost decision`): 100 oldest approved (pilot 73, first-120 27), 19 with a major finding (19 %), $3.77 (≈ $0.038 per question); report authoring/reports/backaudit-1.md.
Rest: ≈ 1036 approved questions from before D-56 without an audit (first-120, batch-3 … batch-7; batch-7 ≈ 508 of them) → ≈ $40 for all, 6 slices of up to 200; only batch-7 ≈ $19, 3 slices.
Each slice raises its own spot-check decision (30 questions, ~15 min); nothing waits on those (`decision 414: Human reviews don''t gate question generation or translation`). Information only, no budget (milestone 9, developer 2026-10-15).
Spot check of the pilot (`decision 203: Spot check backaudit-1 done?`) is still pending; it doesn''t hold this one.
Plan: `plan 57: Release quality bar and back-audit (CT-5)`.', 'About a thousand of the oldest approved questions were never checked by the automatic reviewer that newer questions go through. A first trial on 100 of them found a serious problem in about one in five, at a cost of about four cents per question. You choose whether to check all the rest, only the largest older batch, or stop here; the answer decides how many more checking runs are queued.', 'all: audit every remaining pre-D-56 question, slices of 200', NULL, '2026-10-23T01:19:45Z', '2026-10-23T01:40:39Z', 1706, NULL, 'once', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (416, 'batch-14: health ✗, look at it?', 'pending', '["Look, then ack batch-14", "Ignore"]', 'batch-14 health ✗ (authoring/reports/batch-14.md, Health): drop rate 25.7 % (≤ 20 %), needs_work overlap 1.4 % (≤ 1 %), overlap rate 21.2 % (≤ 20 %). Look, then run qgen health ack batch-14 "<note>".', 'A new batch of trivia questions was generated and some of its quality measures fell outside the agreed limits, mainly the share of drafts that were dropped and the overlap with existing questions. Nothing was stopped; the developer can look at the report and record that they did.', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-23T07:55:31Z', NULL, 1712, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (417, 'Spot check batch-14 done?', 'pending', '["done"]', '30 questions (quality.spot_check_size), http://127.0.0.1:8001/review?batch=batch-14&sample=1 (run trivia-authoring). English batch: read agreement apart from es batches (batch-9 28/30, batch-10 29/30, batch-11 28/30).', 'After each batch of new trivia questions, the developer checks a small blind sample by hand to see whether their judgement matches the automatic review. This asks to do that for the newest batch; nothing waits for it.', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-23T07:55:51Z', NULL, 1681, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (418, 'batch-15: health ✗, look at it?', 'pending', '["Look, then ack batch-15", "Ignore"]', 'batch-15 health ✗ (authoring/reports/batch-15.md, Health): drop rate 28.6 % (≤ 20 %), needs_work overlap 1.3 % (≤ 1 %), overlap rate 23.8 % (≤ 20 %), max final−target gap 11–15 1.52 (≤ 1.50), mean fun 3.35 (≥ 3.40). Look, then run qgen health ack batch-15 "<note>".', 'A new batch of trivia questions was generated and several quality measures fell outside the agreed limits, mainly dropped drafts, overlap with existing questions and how fun the questions were rated. Nothing was stopped; the developer can read the report and record that they looked.', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-23T13:37:31Z', NULL, 1725, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, intro, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (419, 'Spot check backaudit-2 done?', 'pending', '["done"]', 'Back-audit slice 2 for `plan 57: Release quality bar and back-audit (CT-5)`: 200 approved questions from before D-56 were audited by opus. 16 have a major finding (8 %); report: authoring/reports/backaudit-2.md. Cost: $10.04, about $0.05 per question.
Blind spot check: 30 random approved questions of the slice, decide each one without seeing the audit. Start `trivia-authoring`, then open http://127.0.0.1:8001/review?run=backaudit-2&sample=1 (about 15 min).
Afterwards, the 16 flagged questions with the audit notes: http://127.0.0.1:8001/review?run=backaudit-2&audit=flagged. Send to needs_work whatever you want reworked; `qgen rework` runs only when you ask for it.
Answer once the 30 are decided. Then the next slice task (or the close task) runs `qgen report --qc backaudit-2` (release bar, decision 15).', 'A second batch of 200 older approved questions was re-checked by an automated reviewer, and 16 of them were flagged with a serious problem. To confirm the reviewer is trustworthy, a human decides 30 randomly chosen questions without seeing its verdict. The answer only records that this check is finished.', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-23T13:49:44Z', NULL, 1720, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
