INSERT INTO custom_report (id, task_id, question, answer, created_at, answered_at) VALUES (1, 1178, 'what was the result of the code review?', 'The code review is finished. Every piece of the game''s code and its supporting tools was read once, about 40,000 lines in all, between October 12 and 14. The verdict: the game worked, but the code had drifted the way code drifts when many hands each change one small part without seeing the whole. Nothing serious turned up. No problem could ruin a family game night, lose a saved game or stop the game working offline. The reviewers did find about 660 smaller issues, including some 115 hidden bugs in rare situations, such as pressing a button in the middle of an animation or restarting while an answer was being revealed. The rest was mostly the same code copied into several places, leftover code nobody used any more, and notes in the code that no longer matched what it does. About 380 issues were fixed on the spot, among them all the more important bugs: the in-game races, a difficulty setting with the wrong number of steps, a missing piece that would have broken the Steam version, and two ways the question-making tools could overwrite each other''s work. Most of the roughly 270 issues left alone are small judgement calls. Automatic checks now run before every change, so unused code and some common mistakes get caught right away. Some risk remains. The large tool that makes the questions is still one very big file and the most likely place for the next bug. An old copy of the game''s server is kept in step by hand until it is retired. Code added since the review has not been looked at. Three suggestions are waiting for your answer: reuse existing code instead of copying it, fix the notes whenever something is moved or renamed, and decide whether new code should be reviewed after each stage of work or before each release.', '2026-10-15T20:32:24Z', '2026-10-15T20:38:57Z');
